    ('RDL-081', 'Blackout Brings
Creativity to Finals Week
A sudden power outage during finals week left Smithfield
students scrambling for solutions. Students took advantage
of their smart-phone flashlights, and university staff provided
camping lights for dorm lounges.
"It was stressful but kind of fun," said sophomore Grace Lee.
The blackout lasted six hours, caused by a transformer failure
near campus. Dining services offered free coffee, and
professors extended deadlines to ease the pressure.
"We made the best of it," said senior Omar Davis, who
studied with his bike light. Social media buzzed with photos
of candlelit study sessions, turning the inconvenience into a
moment of campus unity.
Some students even organized impromptu acoustic concerts
on the quad, proving that creativity thrives under constraints.
"It reminded us that community matters more than
convenience," said Professor Laura Chen.
By the early morning hours, power was restored, but the
evening''s events left a lasting impression.'),
    ('RDL-082', 'LIT304 Shakespeare''s Kings
This course will explore the variety of ways in which Shakespeare''s
historical plays and tragedies depict monarchical power and its
effects on those who wield it.
Students will read a selection of plays and work in teams to stage
scenes from them.
Other assignments include texts on monarchy by Shakespeare''s
contemporaries and a 10-page reflection essay.'),
    ('RDL-083', 'Ella Morcant (4:45 P.M.):
What’s on the agenda for the student council meeting?
Ryan Quenby (4:47 P.M.):
Budget approvals, spring event planning, and drafting the
revised constitution.
Ella Morcant (4:49 P.M.):
Got it. Are we voting on new club proposals?
Ryan Quenby (4:51 P.M.):
If time permits.
Ella Morcant (4:53 P.M.):
Well, I’ll bring the tentative membership lists and the proposal summaries.
I sure hope we get to them.
Ryan Quenby (4:55 P.M.):
We shall see. Priority is finalizing funding allocations. I’ll print the revised
budget sheets and prep the financial summary. But the funding discussion
may not take very long, so fingers crossed for the club discussion. Maria
always keeps good track of time.
Ella Morcant (4:57 P.M.):
Great! You are the finance whiz! Thanks for handling the relevant documents.
I’ll review the last meeting’s minutes and highlight pending motions.
Ryan Quenby (4:59 P.M.):
Awesome. I’ll set up the projector and check the videoconferencing
link for remote members. Let’s hope Maria brings snacks this time!'),
    ('RDL-084', 'Global Culinary Institute
Spring 2024
CULN 310 Advanced Techniques: Sous Vide
Course Description: This course explores the sous vide culinary
method, a meticulous process in which vacuum-sealed pouches are
placed in a water bath at a precisely controlled temperature. Students
who successfully complete the course will be able to harness their
knowledge of the biochemical interactions and thermal dynamics that
underly the sous vide technique to achieve optimal flavor profiles and
textural nuances. Successful students will also be able to demonstrate
their understanding of the historical progression and contemporary
adaptations of sous vide technology, engage with state-of-the-art sous
vide equipment, and critically evaluate the applications of the sous
vide technique in haute cuisine.
Prerequisites: CULN 110 (Foundational Culinary Arts) and CULN 153
(Introduction to Gastronomic Science). Enrollment is limited to
Culinary Arts advanced track students.
Department: Culinary Arts
Schedule: MWF 2–5 P.M. Credits: Three (3)
Instructor: Marie Dubois, Certified Master Chef'),
    ('RDL-085', 'Kintsugi, an ancient Japanese art, transforms broken pottery into
objects of beauty by highlighting, not hiding, their cracks. Artisans
use lacquer mixed with powdered gold, silver, or platinum to repair
fractures, creating pieces that often become more
valued than their original form.
Originating in the late fifteenth century,
kintsugi reflects the philosophy of wabi-sabi,
which honors imperfection and transience.
Its message extends beyond ceramics,
inspiring personal growth and resilience by
advising people to embrace flaws as part of
life’s narrative. Rather than viewing damage
as failure, kintsugi reframes it as transformation,
symbolizing strength, authenticity, and renewal in
a perfection-driven world.
Today, workshops and studios worldwide teach both its
technical craft and its profound philosophy, offering learners not
only artistic skills but insights into acceptance, individuality, and
creative expression. Kintsugi stands as a timeless reminder that
beauty lies in uniqueness and in the stories our “cracks” reveal, an
idea that applies to academic life and beyond.'),
    ('RDL-086', 'The Rise of Sustainable Living
in University Housing
Martinsburg (April 10)
As universities trend toward eco-consciousness, more students
are embracing sustainable living in campus housing.
At Greenleaf University, the new EcoHousing initiative
features solar panels and rainwater-harvesting systems.
Select dorms also have communal gardens. This program
aligns with students’ growing demand for environmentally
friendly spaces.
In a recent survey, 78 percent of Greenleaf students expressed
interest in sustainable housing. The initiative also includes
workshops on recycling and energy conservation, aiming to
create environmentally aware graduates.
Emily Hart, a senior in environmental science, noted, “Living
sustainably on campus shows that daily choices have
global impact.” The administration hopes this program will
inspire other schools to follow suit.
Greenleaf even offers financial incentives: Students who
choose EcoHousing receive a 5 percent discount on housing
fees. The university plans to expand the initiative to more
dorms next year.'),
    ('RDL-087', 'www.univalencia.com
Junior Year in Valencia Located in the heart of Valencia,
Uni-Valencia offers immersive language mornings and
cultural afternoons.
Explore stunning architecture, vibrant heritage, and the
Albufera Natural Park—one of Europe’s most important
wetlands with breathtaking views and a unique ecosystem.
This program blends academic rigor with unforgettable
experiences.'),
    ('RDL-088', 'New Winter Term Writing Seminar
Please consider registering for the newly developed Creative
Writing Seminar, designed to help you enhance your writing skills
and unleash your creativity. Led by professors from our Creative
Writing Department, this seminar-style class will help you hone
your writing by giving tips on narrative structure, character
development, and dialogue crafting. Students will engage in
interactive exercises and group readings to refine their writing
abilities.
Please bring your laptop for writing activities.
The seminar will take place on Tuesdays and Thursdays from
10:00 A.M. to 12:00 P.M. in the conference room on the fifth floor
of the library.
To be eligible, students must have completed one first-year-level
course in either the Creative Writing or English department.
For more information, please contact the
Creative Writing Department administrator at
admin@creativewriting.edu/winterterm.'),
    ('RDL-089', 'Join the Mechanicsburg Clean-Up Day!
