    ('reading-ctw-ffb7a35f2093ce6109b97ed5', 'Fill in the missing letters in the paragraph. Plants have tiny holes called stomata on their leaves and stems that allow them to take in carbon dioxide from the air and release oxygen into the air. How_ _ _ _, stomata c_ _ also l_ _ water vapor esc_ _ _, which cau_ _ _ problems i_ dry enviro_ _ _ _ _ _. To man_ _ _ this, dur_ _ _ hot per_ _ _ _ when water loss is especially severe, some plants close their stomata temporarily. Another special adaptation is thick, waxy coatings on plant leaves that help conserve water. Plants have tiny holes called stomata on their leaves and stems that allow them to take in carbon dioxide from the air and release oxygen into the air. How____, stomata c__ also l__ water vapor esc___, which cau___ problems i_ dry enviro______. To man___ this, dur___ hot per____ when water loss is especially severe, some plants close their stomata temporarily. Another special adaptation is thick, waxy coatings on plant leaves that help conserve water. Plants have tiny holes called stomata on their leaves and stems that allow them to take in carbon dioxide from the air and release oxygen into the air. However, stomata can also let water vapor escape, which causes problems in dry environments. To manage this, during hot periods when water loss is especially severe, some plants close their stomata temporarily. Another special adaptation is thick, waxy coatings on plant leaves that help conserve water. However How____ ever can c__ an let l__ et escape esc___ ape causes cau___ ses in i_ n environments enviro______ nments manage man___ age during dur___ ing periods per____ iods'),
    ('reading-rap-00d1af757b20b7f4f6440076', 'What does the comparison of optical and radio telescopes suggest? 26. What does the comparison of optical and radio telescopes suggest? Traditional instruments are more susceptible to damage. Dust often interferes with the optical detection of objects in space. Radio telescopes are best for detecting massive stars. Optical telescopes were the first to detect cosmic microwave background radiation. The word “transformative” in the passage is closest in meaning to 27. The word “transformative” in the passage is closest in meaning to groundbreaking exciting concerning startling According to the passage, radio astronomy helps scientists study all of the following EXCEPT 28. According to the passage, radio astronomy helps scientists study all of the following EXCEPT the beginnings of the universe how to prevent the explosions of large stars the last stages of stars the way matter behaves under extreme conditions Why does the author mention “the global spread of wireless technology”? 29. Why does the author mention “the global spread of wireless technology”? To suggest that the issue of technological interference is not likely to end soon To argue that technological interference is not as severe as was once thought To support the argument that optical telescopes are more reliable than radio telescopes To highlight the sophisticated tools astronomers use to detect cosmic signals According to the passage, what is one way to address the issue of technological interference? 30. According to the passage, what is one way to address the issue of technological interference? Revive the use of optical telescopes Limit wireless routers to certain frequencies Place observatories far away from urban areas Mandate specific quiet times when the use of wireless technology is prohibited Radio astronomy is the study of celestial objects using radio waves rather than visible light. Unlike optical telescopes, radio telescopes can detect signals from sources obscured by dust or too faint to be seen with traditional instruments. This branch of astronomy has led to transformative discoveries, including the detection of cosmic microwave background radiation—a faint glow left over from the Big Bang—which has enabled scientists to reconstruct the universe’s early conditions. One of the most famous radio astronomy discoveries was the identification of pulsars in 1967. These rapidly spinning neutron stars, the dense remnants of massive stars that have exploded, emit beams of radio waves at regular intervals. Observing pulsars helps scientists understand the end stages of stellar life and the behavior of matter under extreme conditions. However, radio astronomy faces growing challenges due to technological interference. Devices like mobile phones, wireless routers, and satellites emit radio frequencies that can overwhelm the faint cosmic signals astronomers seek to detect. This “radio frequency pollution” makes it increasingly difficult to isolate genuine astronomical data. To combat this, observatories are often located in remote areas and protected by radio quiet zones, but the global spread of wireless technology continues to pose a serious threat.'),
    ('reading-rap-08b62a981873c3e2eef76ff4', 'Why does the author mention “neural pathways associated with attention and processing”? 11. Why does the author mention “neural pathways associated with attention and processing”? To explain one way in which music might improve certain mental skills To highlight the parts of the brain used when listening to music To contradict the results of a study in Frontiers in Psychology To suggest that music education has little effect on cognitive development Based on the discussion in the passage, what type of musical activity would be least likely to support social development? 12. Based on the discussion in the passage, what type of musical activity would be least likely to support social development? Playing in an orchestra Singing in a choir Calming oneself with peaceful music Being a band member According to the passage, music education promotes social development in all of the following ways EXCEPT 13. According to the passage, music education promotes social development in all of the following ways EXCEPT by developing listening skills by teaching students to communicate non-verbally by encouraging cooperative behavior by setting high standards for performance quality What does the passage suggest about music education in the United States? 14. What does the passage suggest about music education in the United States? It is acknowledged to be a necessary part of the school curriculum. It is at greater risk of losing its funding than other school subjects are. Some parents fear that time and money spent on music programs will limit their children’s academic growth. Some parents want to see further studies done on the benefits of music education. The word “holistic” in the passage is closest in meaning to 15. The word “holistic” in the passage is closest in meaning to documented celebrated comprehensive well-known In the United States, music education has been consistently linked to enhanced cognitive development. Studies such as those by the National Association for Music Education (NAfME) and research published in Frontiers in Psychology have shown that children who study music often demonstrate improvements in memory, language acquisition, and spatial-temporal reasoning. These cognitive gains are attributed to the discipline and structured practice inherent in learning music, which strengthens neural pathways associated with attention and processing. Beyond cognition, music education contributes meaningfully to social development. Participation in an ensemble—a group of musicians performing together—requires active collaboration, listening, and nonverbal communication, fostering empathy and cooperative behavior. This group dynamic differs from traditional classroom settings and can support the development of interpersonal skills. Moreover, music provides a safe space for emotional exploration. Students engage with complex emotions, both their own and those conveyed by others, through the expressive nature of music itself. This experience can enhance emotional intelligence and serve as a constructive outlet for stress. Despite these documented benefits, music programs in U.S. schools are often viewed as nonessential compared to core academic subjects and thus vulnerable to budget cuts. This reduction may disproportionately affect students who could benefit most from music’s holistic contributions, potentially limiting their cognitive, social, and emotional growth.'),
    ('reading-rap-08f66e6c2b3e25bd34fa0651', 'What can be inferred from the passage about the capabilities of optical astronomy BEFORE the late 1980s? 11. What can be inferred from the passage about the capabilities of optical astronomy BEFORE the late 1980s? Images of celestial objects from ground-based telescopes were often unclear. Astronomers used adaptive optics to distort light waves. Telescopes used deformable mirrors to see farther into space. Astronomers used the shimmering effect to find distant stars. How do proponents of adaptive optics counter criticism of the technology? 12. How do proponents of adaptive optics counter criticism of the technology? The observatories that use the technology have a lower carbon footprint than the critics maintain. The usefulness of the technology is more important than its drawbacks. The negative effects of the technology are localized and not globally significant. The technology is only in its early stages and will improve over time. The word "stewardship" in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word "stewardship" in the passage is closest in meaning to supervision achievement enthusiasm design Why does the author mention attempts to optimize algorithms? 14. Why does the author mention attempts to optimize algorithms? To give an example of how observatories could cut costs To suggest that current algorithms still contain some errors when adjusting mirrors To provide an example of how adaptive optics continues to evolve To show one way researchers are trying to reduce observatories'' energy consumption There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. To understand this phenomenon, imagine looking through the heat waves seen over hot asphalt or desert sand. Optical astronomy, the study of celestial objects through visible light, has been revolutionized by adaptive optics, first developed in the late 1980s. This technology, which allows ground-based telescopes at observatories to counteract atmospheric distortion, has enabled astronomers to observe the cosmos with exceptional clarity. Light waves from distant stars travel through Earth''s atmosphere, creating a shimmering effect that distorts them. Adaptive optics corrects these distortions in real time using a system of deformable mirrors controlled by computer algorithms. The mirrors can change shape rapidly to counteract the distortion, with the algorithms calculating the needed adjustments thousands of times per second. However, the technology is not without controversy. Some argue that the energy consumption of adaptive optics contributes significantly to observatories'' carbon footprints. Yet, proponents counter that the potential discoveries outweigh environmental concerns, citing recent breakthroughs in identifying exoplanets potentially hospitable to life. The debate highlights the complexities of balancing scientific advancement with environmental stewardship. Some researchers are actively exploring the use of solar panels and wind turbines to power these observatories, especially for daytime operations. Others are trying to optimize algorithms to reduce unnecessary computations and the need for constant high-speed corrections.'),
    ('reading-rap-0adaa3730f64172aac60187c', 'What can be inferred about the "Images such as bison (large, ox-like animals), handprints, and abstract symbols" that are mentioned in paragraph 1? 11. What can be inferred about the "Images such as bison (large, ox-like animals), handprints, and abstract symbols" that are mentioned in paragraph 1? They were created more recently than other cave painting images. They appear mainly in cave spaces used for daily living. They do not generally appear together in the same cave. They are not unique to the people of a particular region. According to the passage, the other scholars mentioned in paragraph 2 would agree with which of the following statements? 12. According to the passage, the other scholars mentioned in paragraph 2 would agree with which of the following statements? Most cave paintings were intended to depict successful hunts. The location of cave paintings within caves may offer clues to their purpose. Cave paintings illustrate the impulse of prehistoric people to share daily events. It is not likely that cave paintings were intended to influence natural forces. The word "speculate" in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word "speculate" in the passage is closest in meaning to suspect insist recall recognize Why does the author mention cave paintings found in the caves of Lascaux and Chauvet? 14. Why does the author mention cave paintings found in the caves of Lascaux and Chauvet? To indicate how the Lascaux and Chauvet cave paintings differ from cave paintings at other sites To provide evidence for the view that cave paintings were often tied to hunting rituals To provide some specific information about hunting as an initiation practice To give examples of cave paintings that did not have an obvious symbolic function According to the "third perspective," cave images 15. According to the "third perspective," cave images probably depicted imaginary landscapes are more related to social norms than to animals may have been intended for a particular age group were created mainly by young people Prehistoric cave paintings are both visually powerful and mysterious, inviting scholars to infer what motivated their creators and what the images may have meant. Many researchers argue that these paintings served as an early form of communication and memory-keeping, allowing prehistoric people to record experiences or announce their presence. Images such as bison (large, ox-like animals), handprints, and abstract symbols appear across continents, suggesting shared impulses to document life and identity. Other scholars emphasize possible spiritual or ritual motivations. Many paintings appear deep inside caves in spaces not used for daily living. Some archaeologists speculate that these locations had symbolic functions. The imagery may have been tied to hunting rituals, initiation practices, or attempts to influence natural forces. Paintings of animals and hunting scenes, common in sites like Lascaux and Chauvet, support interpretations that link art to belief systems or communal ceremonies. A third perspective views cave art as an expression of early storytelling and teaching. The images may have had a pedagogical function, serving to convey knowledge about animals, landscapes, or social norms to younger group members. Together, these interpretations suggest that cave paintings may not have been created for a single purpose but reflected a complex blend of communication, ritual, and creative expression.'),
    ('reading-rap-0dd51c79faabdb5cfd63f07e', 'The passage mentions all of the following as factors influencing road safety EXCEPT 11. The passage mentions all of the following as factors influencing road safety EXCEPT social influences personality traits environmental conditions vehicle maintenance The word “vigilant” in the passage is closest in meaning to 12. The word “vigilant” in the passage is closest in meaning to watchful experienced considerate assertive Based on the second paragraph, what can be inferred about the role of passengers in promoting safer driving? 13. Based on the second paragraph, what can be inferred about the role of passengers in promoting safer driving? Passengers usually encourage drivers to undertake riskier maneuvers. Passengers can influence drivers in a positive way, but this is not always the case. Passengers exert a stronger influence on drivers than structural factors do. The presence of passengers has no measurable effect on driving behavior. What does the passage suggest about structural factors? 14. What does the passage suggest about structural factors? They are the main cause of traffic accidents. Their importance is debated by researchers. They influence younger drivers the most. They are the foundation of traffic psychology. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. These findings suggest that drivers’ behavior is not solely rooted in personality. Traffic psychology explores how individual differences and social contexts shape driving behavior, often in counterintuitive ways. While traits like impulsivity are frequently linked to risky driving, research shows that this connection is not always straightforward. For instance, impulsive individuals may drive cautiously in familiar environments but exhibit recklessness when faced with novel or stressful situations. Moreover, environmental cues—such as heavy traffic or aggressive drivers—can amplify underlying character traits. Social influences further complicate the picture. Young drivers, for example, may engage in riskier maneuvers when accompanied by peers, driven by a desire to impress or conform. However, this effect is not universal. The presence of a vigilant passenger—such as a parent or responsible friend—can counteract impulsive tendencies and promote safer driving. This raises questions about how social roles and expectations modulate behavior. Some researchers argue that focusing too heavily on personality overlooks structural factors like road design or traffic law enforcement, which may exert a stronger influence on safety outcomes. Yet, understanding the psychological underpinnings of driving remains crucial for designing effective interventions. By integrating insights from personality psychology, social dynamics, and environmental design, traffic psychology offers a nuanced framework for improving road safety.'),
    ('reading-rap-120a73f7968ed936297b85fc', 'What does the passage suggest about origami during the Edo period in Japan? 31. What does the passage suggest about origami during the Edo period in Japan? It had not been introduced yet. It was mostly used in religious and ceremonial contexts. It was practiced with materials other than paper. Its practice involved folding paper into a greater variety of shapes than is common today. What is one way that the principles of origami affect modern scientific practices? 32. What is one way that the principles of origami affect modern scientific practices? They introduce mathematical principles that were previously unknown. Their influence has caused researchers to increase focus on artistic design. They provide effective ways of designing solar panel arrays. They are used to fold new microscopic biological structures. Why does the author mention the folding of 1,000 cranes? 33. Why does the author mention the folding of 1,000 cranes? To emphasize that bird shapes are highly popular in origami To illustrate the cultural significance of origami in today''s Japan To explain a new origami technique To support the point that origami is traditionally associated with longevity When is senbazuru commonly practiced today? 34. When is senbazuru commonly practiced today? When an artist needs inspiration for origami When representatives of different communities meet After many living cranes have been seen After a tragic event has occurred There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Artistic paper folding may have begun in China after paper was invented there, or it may have emerged in Japan itself. Origami, the Japanese art of paper folding, has fascinated individuals for centuries. Its name originates from the Japanese words ori (to fold) and kami (paper). While its origins are debated, origami was first extensively practiced during the Edo period (1603–1867), when paper became widely accessible in Japan. At this time, origami was used during ceremonies, such as folding paper cranes to symbolize peace and good fortune. Over time, its function has expanded beyond religious and formal contexts. The geometric precision of origami exemplifies an integration of art and mathematics. In fact, origami principles influence disciplines like engineering and design today. For example, researchers have applied origami-inspired folding techniques to create solar panel arrays that can compactly store and unfold when deployed. Biologists, too, model microscopic structures using folding patterns derived from origami designs. The cultural value of origami remains strong in Japan. The crane, a bird traditionally associated with longevity, has taken on new significance in modern times. Folding 1,000 paper cranes has become a symbol of hope and healing, particularly following tragedies. This tradition, known as senbazuru, reflects origami''s enduring capacity to bring comfort and inspiration to diverse communities worldwide.'),
    ('reading-rap-12e71c91e8c595abfee101fb', 'The passage mentions all of the following about cooperative education programs EXCEPT: 11. The passage mentions all of the following about cooperative education programs EXCEPT: They are based on the idea that practical experience is necessary in certain technical fields. They are recommended for students who are experiencing difficulties in theoretical courses. Engineering departments at universities were some of the first to adopt them. They allow students to receive payment while learning. What does the passage suggest about institutions such as Northeastern University and Drexel University? 12. What does the passage suggest about institutions such as Northeastern University and Drexel University? They have worked closely with employers to establish comprehensive co-op programs. They allow students who have completed multiple co-ops to graduate more quickly than they otherwise would. They prefer to admit students who have already had extensive practical experience in their chosen fields. They limit co-op placements to the top students in each field. The word "cultivate" in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word "cultivate" in the passage is closest in meaning to precede highlight demonstrate promote According to the passage, what is one potential drawback of co-ops? 14. According to the passage, what is one potential drawback of co-ops? Co-op students are less likely to graduate than students who do not participate in co-ops. Co-op students perfect their practical skills at the expense of their theoretical knowledge. Co-op students take longer to graduate than other students. Co-op students overextend themselves by working at both their co-op placements and their regular campus jobs. Which type of student is probably least likely to find cooperative education attractive? 15. Which type of student is probably least likely to find cooperative education attractive? A student who is most drawn to analyzing texts and doing academic research A student who is pursuing a degree in computer science A student who wants to have the best chance of finding a job after graduation A student who wants to network with professionals in her chosen field Cooperative education ("co-ops") emerged in the United States in the early twentieth century, when engineering programs seeking stronger industry alignment began alternating academic terms with structured, paid work experience. Although the model was first formalized at the University of Cincinnati, its rapid adoption across institutions specializing in scientific and technical fields reflected broader recognition that technical fields required applied practice alongside theoretical coursework. Today, co-ops are most prevalent at universities with deep employer partnerships. At Northeastern University and Drexel University, most students complete co-ops (multi-month paid placements) in fields ranging from engineering to computing to design. These programs often involve multiple sequenced work terms with increasing responsibility and are integrated into degree requirements rather than treated as optional internships. Proponents argue that co-ops cultivate professional maturity by requiring students to navigate real organizational structures, contribute to long-term projects, and build evidence-based portfolios before graduation. They also note that co-op graduates often report higher initial earnings and stronger job placement. Critics, however, contend that alternating semesters can disrupt academic continuity, extend time-to-degree, and disadvantage students who cannot relocate or afford breaks in campus employment. Despite these concerns, co-op education remains a defining form of work-integrated learning, especially in fields where applied expertise is essential.'),
