term ‘domestication.’ Maize, also known as corn, is one of the world''s most important crops. Scholars studying its domestication have debated about how a wild grass with limited utility transformed into a globally significant staple. The dominant theory, supported by genetic and archaeological evidence, posits that maize was domesticated from wild teosinte (a species that, like modern maize, belongs to the Zea genus) in southwestern Mexico roughly 9,000 years ago. Proponents argue that early cultivators selectively bred plants exhibiting desirable traits—larger kernels, reduced seed casings, and more robust cobs—thereby accelerating the cultivated plants'' divergence from teosinte. This model is reinforced by analyses demonstrating a close genetic relationship between modern maize and specific southwest Mexican teosinte populations. Alternative accounts have also been suggested. Some researchers propose a multiregional model, suggesting that while initial domestication occurred in Mexico, subsequent hybridization with other species of the Zea genus across Central America contributed to maize''s eventual diversity. Another hypothesis highlights the possibility of protracted domestication, in which early foragers managed wild-growing teosinte long before fully domesticated maize emerged, blurring the boundary between cultivation and wild harvesting. Although consensus increasingly favors a single primary domestication event, ongoing discoveries continue to refine our understanding of one of the world''s most transformative crops.'),
    ('reading-rap-17f77827600631995f5076ea', 'What major distinction does the author make in paragraph 1? 31. What major distinction does the author make in paragraph 1? Between a work of craft and a work of art Between functional craft and domestic labor Between intentional design and cultural expression Between quilts and mere works of household craft Why does the author mention that Gee''s Bend quilting grew out of "a rural, community-based practice rooted in everyday experience"? 32. Why does the author mention that Gee''s Bend quilting grew out of "a rural, community-based practice rooted in everyday experience"? To illustrate a point about the ways in which craft objects have historically been understood To account for the type of artistic expression found in the region''s quilts To draw a contrast between the modernist qualities of the quilts and their origins To argue that Gee''s Bend quilts had much in common with quilts created in other rural areas The phrase "operate as" in the passage is closest in meaning to 33. The phrase "operate as" in the passage is closest in meaning to are similar to serve as depend on connect with The author would be most likely to agree with which of the following claims about the hierarchy mentioned in paragraph 3? 34. The author would be most likely to agree with which of the following claims about the hierarchy mentioned in paragraph 3? It has contributed to a misunderstanding of the creative process. It has distorted the way many people perceive modernist painters. It has traditionally been responsible for the neglect of certain works of art. It has changed the way most people think about artistic innovation. Which of the following best describes the function of the highlighted sentence? 35. Which of the following best describes the function of the highlighted sentence? It introduces new criteria for defining the purpose of art. It explains why quilts have historically been misunderstood. It acknowledges an important difference between works of craft and works of art. It offers an alternative to a rigid distinction between art and craft. While quilting has historically been categorized as domestic labor, some quilts—bedcovers often made of pieces of fabric stitched together—cannot be adequately understood as mere works of household craft. For example, the quilts created by African American women from Gee''s Bend, Alabama should be regarded as works of art, displaying intentional design, cultural expression, and aesthetic innovation that extend far beyond the boundaries of functional craft. The Gee''s Bend tradition demonstrates how a utilitarian form can evolve into a powerful artistic language. The quilters'' bold improvisation, asymmetry, and inventive use of color parallel the visual strategies of modernist painters, yet they emerged independently from a rural, community-based practice rooted in everyday experience. These quilts also operate as visual narratives. Each piece reflects personal history, collective memory, and the resourcefulness of women who transformed fabric scraps into compositions of striking emotional depth. Their work challenges the hierarchy that has long separated "fine art" from "craft," a distinction often shaped by gender, race, and class. To recognize these quilts as art is to acknowledge the creative rigor embedded in a historically undervalued tradition. This is not to say that quilts are not a craft; rather, the quilts demonstrate that craft and art can coexist within a single object.'),
    ('reading-rap-18254e6c2471afbb626c22b4', 'The word “static” in the passage is closest in meaning to 31. The word “static” in the passage is closest in meaning to impractical unchanging wasteful simple The passage indicates all of the following about building data science EXCEPT: 32. The passage indicates all of the following about building data science EXCEPT: It uses data collected from Internet of Things devices. It requires special training in data analysis for architects who use it. It has an impact on how architects design new buildings. It can be used as a basis for decisions about a building’s operations. According to the passage, what is one drawback of the use of sensors? 33. According to the passage, what is one drawback of the use of sensors? Sensors lead to greater energy use. Sensors are known for supplying inaccurate data. The data collected is frequently hard to interpret. Some building occupants may not approve of their use. Why does the author mention “hospitals” and “office spaces”? 34. Why does the author mention “hospitals” and “office spaces”? To give examples of city structures that are often poorly designed To demonstrate how building data science can improve urban infrastructure To show the limitations of building data science To contradict the claim that building data science will be key to creating adaptable spaces Based on the passage, building data science can help most with which of the following? 35. Based on the passage, building data science can help most with which of the following? Increasing a building’s office space Limiting a building’s construction costs Reducing a building’s environmental impact Enhancing a building’s visual appeal Once static structures, buildings increasingly function as dynamic systems thanks to applications of data science to the built environment. Building data science uses sensors and Internet of Things (IoT) devices, such as smart thermostats, to collect and interpret data from buildings. This approach enables architects and planners to make informed decisions throughout a building’s design, operation, and renovation phases. Sensors embedded in modern buildings track variables such as energy consumption, temperature, air quality, and occupancy patterns. These insights help optimize energy use, reduce operational costs, and enhance comfort. Smart HVAC (heating, ventilation, and air-conditioning) systems can adjust airflow based on real-time occupancy, improving efficiency. Monitoring space usage may conflict, however, with occupants’ privacy concerns and dislike of being tracked without their consent. Despite high initial costs, building data science offers long-term benefits. Studies show that buildings using these systems can reduce energy consumption by up to 30 percent, making them attractive for sustainable development. As urban populations grow, building data science will be key to creating adaptable spaces. A skyscraper that adjusts its internal climate based on external weather conditions exemplifies this potential. Other applications include optimizing the layouts of hospitals for better patient flow or using occupancy data to redesign underutilized office spaces.'),
    ('reading-rap-1874915d13372083507eb759', 'The word “uptake” in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word “uptake” in the passage is closest in meaning to evolution harvest absorption diversity What is one difference between biofertilizers and traditional fertilizers? 12. What is one difference between biofertilizers and traditional fertilizers? Biofertilizers are less expensive to produce. Biofertilizers contain living microorganisms. Biofertilizers are used only in arid regions. Biofertilizers require synthetic chemicals to work. According to the passage, what makes biofertilizers particularly effective? 13. According to the passage, what makes biofertilizers particularly effective? They are compatible with multiple soil types. They prevent soil particles from binding together. They improve the movement of air through soil. They begin to release nutrients immediately. What does the author suggest about biofertilizers in the last paragraph? 14. What does the author suggest about biofertilizers in the last paragraph? Many farmers are already familiar with them. Governmental rules affect their use in some areas. They are readily available to farmers. They do not work as well as chemical fertilizers. What is the main focus of the last paragraph? 15. What is the main focus of the last paragraph? It describes new scientific research supporting biofertilizers. It compares the effectiveness of different types of fertilizers. It discusses barriers to adoption of biofertilizers by farmers. It offers advice to farmers on best agricultural practices. One of the latest inventions in agrochemistry is biofertilizers. This innovation aims to provide an environmentally friendly alternative to traditional fertilizers, which are infamous for causing soil and water pollution. Biofertilizers use living microorganisms to improve soil fertility by fixing nutrients such as nitrogen, ensuring that these nutrients remain available for plant uptake. Unlike conventional fertilizers that simply release nutrients into the soil, biofertilizers enhance the soil''s own biological activity, creating a more sustainable agricultural practice. Increased bioactivity results in improved soil structure. Microorganisms in biofertilizers produce substances that bind soil particles together, enhancing aeration and water retention. This mechanism is particularly vital in arid regions, where soil degradation poses a significant challenge. Yet, this ability makes the effectiveness of biofertilizers depend on specific soil types and crops, introducing complexities into their use. Despite their potential, biofertilizers face hurdles, including skepticism from farmers accustomed to chemical fertilizers. Concerns about cost, availability, and the need for new agricultural knowledge contribute to their slow adoption. Moreover, the regulatory landscape for biofertilizers is still developing, affecting their market presence. Still, some regions have started to implement subsidies and educational programs to encourage the transition to these sustainable options, hinting at a shift in agricultural practices.'),
    ('reading-rap-25e10401a0a3eeb038f2636e', 'The word "subtle" in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word "subtle" in the passage is closest in meaning to faint quick strong constant It can be inferred that microseisms are strongest during ocean storms because 12. It can be inferred that microseisms are strongest during ocean storms because the Earth''s crust and mantle are more active during such storms ocean waves are larger and collide more vigorously during such storms seismometers are designed to work most effectively during such storms the details of geological structures become more apparent during such storms Why does the author mention "variations in rock density and the presence of faults or fractures"? 13. Why does the author mention "variations in rock density and the presence of faults or fractures"? To suggest that more research is needed to understand the relationship between microseisms and earthquakes To identify the most important features of Earth''s crust and mantle To help explain how microseisms can alter the geological structures that they pass through To highlight the kinds of information microseisms can provide about Earth''s interior According to the passage, how can microseisms help in oil and gas exploration? 14. According to the passage, how can microseisms help in oil and gas exploration? By providing historical data on ocean conditions By pinpointing potential drilling sites By measuring the strength of seismic waves By reducing the need for seismometers What does the author suggest about "filtering methods and cross-referencing data from multiple seismometers"? 15. What does the author suggest about "filtering methods and cross-referencing data from multiple seismometers"? They are techniques used to study microseisms. They demonstrate the outdatedness of seismometers. They are popular ways of studying various types of seismic noises. They highlight a flaw in current research methods. The Earth is constantly vibrating, even when there are no earthquakes. These subtle vibrations, known as microseisms, are typically caused by ocean waves. When waves collide, they create pressure variations that travel through the water and into the sea floor. This energy transfer generates seismic waves detectable by seismometers worldwide. Interestingly, microseisms are strongest during storms when ocean waves are more powerful. Researchers have found these vibrations can provide important information about the Earth''s interior. Microseisms travel through the Earth''s crust and mantle, revealing details about the geological structures they pass through, including variations in rock density and the presence of faults or fractures. Another compelling use of microseisms is in climate studies. Scientists analyze historical data on these vibrations to reconstruct past ocean conditions and storm activities. This helps improve climate models and understand long-term weather patterns. Additionally, microseisms are used in oil and gas exploration. They identify potential drilling sites by highlighting subsurface structures without more invasive techniques. Studying microseisms is challenging. The mild signals are easily masked by other seismic noises. Advanced data analysis techniques and state-of-the-art equipment are essential for accurately interpreting microseisms. Researchers often employ filtering methods and cross-referencing data from multiple seismometers.'),
    ('reading-rap-26d0ed793e40bbe883e60a6a', 'Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that introduces the idea that thermodynamics has implications that the reader may not expect. Select the sentence to make your choice. 31. Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that introduces the idea that thermodynamics has implications that the reader may not expect. Select the sentence to make your choice. Why does the author note that "sunlight becomes chemical energy"? 32. Why does the author note that "sunlight becomes chemical energy"? To illustrate the claim that educators rely on simple analogies when teaching thermodynamics To challenge the idea that thermodynamics offers a way of understanding complex phenomena To provide an example of increasing entropy in a biological system To show how the first law of thermodynamics is applicable to food chains According to the passage, what principle explains the trend toward less efficient energy use in ecosystems? 33. According to the passage, what principle explains the trend toward less efficient energy use in ecosystems? Smaller systems tend to combine into larger ones. As entropy increases, less energy is available for work. Energy is never lost but takes on many different forms. Top predators are vulnerable to changes in an ecosystem. The word "disparity" in the passage is closest in meaning to 34. The word "disparity" in the passage is closest in meaning to tendency difference comparison conclusion What does the passage suggest about top predators? 35. What does the passage suggest about top predators? They are the most complex organisms in an ecosystem. They are metabolically more efficient than their prey. Their metabolic efficiency depends on their habitat. They use up their energy more slowly as they mature. When students first encounter the laws of thermodynamics, educators often rely on simplified analogies—billiard balls, falling stones—to convey foundational principles. Yet these laws extend far beyond mechanical systems, offering a powerful framework for interpreting complex phenomena such as energy flow in ecosystems. The first law, which asserts that energy cannot be created or destroyed but only transformed, is clearly exemplified in nature: sunlight becomes chemical energy via photosynthesis, which fuels plants, then herbivores and, ultimately, carnivores. This cascading energy transfer underscores the conservation principle while revealing the intricate interdependence among trophic (food chain) levels, with each organism depending on the energy efficiency of those occupying the level below. In contrast, the second law—which states that entropy, or disorder, tends to increase—illuminates why ecosystems evolve toward less efficient energy use. While simple organisms at the base of the food chain convert energy with relative efficiency, larger animals lose much of their energy intake. This disparity is not merely a biological curiosity; it reflects a fundamental thermodynamic constraint. As ecosystems mature, energy becomes increasingly fragmented and less available for productive work. Exceptions exist— some top predators exhibit surprising metabolic efficiency-but the general trend remains: Complexity breeds energy loss.'),
    ('reading-rap-27e9be152a971d49cc0f3d67', 'What best characterizes UBI as described in the passage? 11. What best characterizes UBI as described in the passage? An idea whose popularity has peaked and is now disappearing An unpopular idea that is now being tested on large populations An interesting idea that has been tried only on a small scale A popular idea that is still purely theoretical What is meant by an “unconditional sum of money” in the second sentence? 12. What is meant by an “unconditional sum of money” in the second sentence? A sum of money offered to a random group of people A sum of money that has no limit A sum of money that increases over time A sum of money given with no requirements placed on the recipient How did the UBI experiments mentioned in the passage affect labor markets? 13. How did the UBI experiments mentioned in the passage affect labor markets? A pilot program led to a small increase in employment. All of the programs described led to higher unemployment. A few of the pilot programs led to lower productivity and lower wages. The programs showed no noticeable effect on employment. The passage mentions all of the following as concerns about UBI raised by critics EXCEPT: 14. The passage mentions all of the following as concerns about UBI raised by critics EXCEPT: its effect on public finances its effect on economic productivity its effect on prices its effect on international trade What is suggested about UBI and inflation? 15. What is suggested about UBI and inflation? Pilot programs have not adequately tested a possible relation between UBI and inflation. Evidence shows that increased demand would lead to somewhat higher localized prices. Most pilot programs suggest that UBI would dramatically increase inflation. The larger pilot programs did not report noticeable inflation in local prices. The concept of universal basic income (UBI) is gaining attention worldwide. UBI provides all citizens with a regular, unconditional sum of money. Proponents argue that UBI can reduce poverty, streamline welfare, and offer a safety net in an era of automation and job loss. In Finland, a two-year pilot program gave 2,000 unemployed citizens a monthly payment. The results showed improved mental well-being and a modest increase in employment, but the findings were not definitive. Similarly, an experiment in Ontario, Canada, showed promising social benefits but was cut short due to cost concerns. Critics raise several concerns. One major argument against UBI is its cost. Implementing it could require significant tax increases or reallocating government resources, straining public finances. There are also concerns about the impact on labor markets. Opponents suggest that a guaranteed income might disincentivize work, leading to reduced productivity and economic stagnation. The relationship between UBI and inflation is another common concern. While most pilot programs have not reported noticeable inflation in local prices, the programs are limited in scale and have not injected enough money into economies to significantly affect prices. Some economists note that certain sectors, like housing or groceries, could see localized price increases due to increased demand.'),
    ('reading-rap-28a0107ab4f408924f5c8883', 'What is suggested by the example of Flemish towns importing grain and exporting finished wool? 11. What is suggested by the example of Flemish towns importing grain and exporting finished wool? Trade networks primarily benefited urban populations rather than rural producers. Flemish towns controlled the production of both raw materials and finished goods. Regions became dependent on one another for basic needs and manufactured goods. Population growth in towns led to decreased reliance on external food supplies. The author mentions all of the following features of the Western European recovery EXCEPT 12. The author mentions all of the following features of the Western European recovery EXCEPT higher crop yields establishment of new towns greater local self-sufficiency in agricultural production specialized production for the purpose of trade The word “amplify” in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word “amplify” in the passage is closest in meaning to maintain complicate disrupt increase Why does the author state that “innovations spread unevenly and did not transform every region at the same pace”? 14. Why does the author state that “innovations spread unevenly and did not transform every region at the same pace”? To suggest that some regions did not experience increased economic activity To support the claim that improved agricultural production may have been partly due to milder weather To explain why some historians continue to emphasize technological change over other factors To explain why technological change occurred at different rates across regions There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Yet not all of the changes can be explained by improvements in agriculture alone. Between the tenth and thirteenth centuries, Western Europe experienced a broad recovery following a long period of decline. Harvests improved, towns multiplied, and long-distance trade bound regions more tightly. Steadier trading and new settlements, in turn, supported larger populations that further widened markets. In Flemish towns, growing populations relied on imported grain from northern France and the Baltic while exporting finished wool cloth to Mediterranean markets. A common explanation for this recovery points to agricultural innovation. Heavier plows and new crop rotation systems made yields more consistent, which encouraged investment in mills and other infrastructure. In some regions, production itself was also shaped by expanding markets. As distant buyers and sellers became increasingly connected, agricultural producers began specializing for commercial purposes rather than for their own use. In other words, trade could amplify output by reducing the risk that producers would be unable to sell their goods at stable prices. Climate reconstructions point to relatively mild conditions in parts of Europe during this period, so higher yields may sometimes reflect favorable weather as much as innovation. If so, historians may overstate the importance of technological change, especially given that innovations spread unevenly and did not transform every region at the same pace.'),
    ('reading-rap-28b2cecf31bfee0a244d1a09', 'Which of the following is the most accurate description of the passage’s organization? 11. Which of the following is the most accurate description of the passage’s organization? A discussion of the theory of theater lighting followed by paragraphs detailing the different effects it can achieve An introduction followed by a summary of the disadvantages of older theater lighting systems An introduction followed by a series of paragraphs on successive developments in theater lighting A discussion of different theaters followed by a description of lighting technologies that complement each building According to the passage, gas lighting was an improvement on candles and oil lamps in all of the following ways EXCEPT: 12. According to the passage, gas lighting was an improvement on candles and oil lamps in all of the following ways EXCEPT: It was less likely to cause fires. It could be directed at individual actors. It produced a more constant light. It produced a softer light. The word “fundamentally” in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word “fundamentally” in the passage is closest in meaning to simply thoroughly surprisingly permanently Why does the author discuss Transformation? 14. Why does the author discuss Transformation? To illustrate how skillful lighting can communicate a mood to the audience To provide an example of a play that could have benefited from LED technology To explain why electric lighting was not as powerful for creating moods as gas lighting To argue that some plays relied too heavily on lighting effects What does the passage imply about modern lighting technology? 15. What does the passage imply about modern lighting technology? It can sometimes shift the focus from the play to the lighting. It requires directors who are skilled in computer programming. Theaters must now allocate more money to technicians and less to actors. Theaters must be redesigned to incorporate new lighting technology. Theater lighting has evolved significantly over the centuries. Initially reliant on sunlight, performances were limited to daylight hours. Candles and oil lamps enabled evening shows but posed fire risks and offered limited control over illumination. The introduction of gas lighting in the nineteenth century provided safer lighting that was brighter and more consistent. This advancement improved visibility on stage and allowed for more expressive acting. Lights that illuminated actors from below and the sides helped direct audience attention. The emergence of electric lighting in the late nineteenth century was transformative. Designers began using gels and filters to create a wide range of colors of varying intensity. These and other innovations, including focused spotlights and dimming systems, fundamentally reshaped the visual and emotional stage landscape. For example, the angled light beams and shadows in Toller’s 1919 play Transformation evoked the chaos and psychological unrest of post-war Germany. Today’s theaters leverage LED technology for energy efficiency and versatility, as it offers rapid color changes and programmable effects to create varied settings. Designers must now carefully balance technological prowess with artistic intent, ensuring that lighting supports rather than distracts from the narrative. Moreover, as lighting systems become increasingly complex, collaboration between lighting designers, directors, and technicians has become essential.'),
    ('reading-rap-2a8207a6f456303be8bfd836', 'The word "norms" in the passage is closest in meaning to 26. The word "norms" in the passage is closest in meaning to long histories hidden benefits practical effects unwritten rules Why does the passage mention "a person who is both a parent and an employee"? 27. Why does the passage mention "a person who is both a parent and an employee"? To show how a person can balance multiple obligations To imply that roles are not always rigidly defined To illustrate the concept of role conflict To emphasize the importance of good childcare Why does the passage mention "a teacher"? 28. Why does the passage mention "a teacher"? To support the idea that social role theory is often difficult to understand To explain how a person might experience competing demands within a role To imply that the resources provided to teachers are often insufficient To demonstrate how behavioral issues make learning difficult for everyone The phrase "these dynamics" in the passage refers to 29. The phrase "these dynamics" in the passage refers to the evolution of social roles over time competition for leadership positions interactions between men and women in the workforce decreasing prestige of some social roles There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 30. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. For example, a parent is expected to offer affection and emotional stability to their children, while a soldier is expected to follow orders. Social role theory is a significant framework in theoretical sociology, explaining how individuals adopt roles based on societal expectations. Society is structured by roles, each with specific behaviors and norms. A key aspect of social role theory is role conflict, which occurs when the expectations of one role clash with those of another. For instance, a person who is both a parent and an employee might struggle to balance caring for their children and meeting work obligations. This conflict can lead to stress and decreased performance in one or both roles. Another important concept is role strain, which is stress within a single role due to competing demands or insufficient resources. An example is a teacher who feels overwhelmed by the need to manage classroom behavior while also keeping lessons engaging. Social role theory also addresses how roles change over time. As society evolves, the expectations attached to roles can shift. For example, the role of women in the workforce has changed dramatically, with increasing acceptance of women in leadership. Understanding these dynamics is crucial for analyzing how social structures impact individual behavior and societal change.'),
    ('reading-rap-2d0806ae9fec7059ff899034', 'The word "posits" in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word "posits" in the passage is closest in meaning to suggests agrees proves determines Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that provides an example to help readers understand Plato''s idea about abstract forms. 12. Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that provides an example to help readers understand Plato''s idea about abstract forms. Select the sentence to make your choice. Platonism makes all of the following claims about abstract forms EXCEPT: 13. Platonism makes all of the following claims about abstract forms EXCEPT: They include ideas such as beauty, justice, and equality. They are more real than physical objects. They remain the same over time. They influence how the changing physical world is perceived. Which of the following can be inferred about nominalism? 14. Which of the following can be inferred about nominalism? It has fewer advocates than mathematical Platonism does. It disagrees with the claims of mathematical Platonism. It argues that mathematical objects are similar to physical objects. It supports the independent existence of mathematical entities. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Plato''s discussions of justice and virtue remain influential in moral philosophy. Platonism, the philosophy founded by the ancient Greek thinker Plato, has significantly influenced modern philosophical thought. At its core, Platonism posits that abstract forms or ideas, such as concepts of beauty, justice, and equality, are the truest reality, more real than the physical objects we perceive. While a beautiful painting is a physical object, the abstract idea of beauty is a form that exists independently of any particular occurrence. Plato''s theory of forms asserts that these forms are timeless and unchanging, unlike the fluctuating physical world. Platonism has found renewed interest among contemporary philosophers, particularly in the philosophy of mathematics. Mathematical Platonists such as Kurt Gödel and Sir Roger Penrose argue that numbers and mathematical objects exist independently of human minds, akin to physical objects. This perspective contrasts with nominalism, as advocated by philosophers such as Hartry Field, who argue that mathematical entities are mere symbols or constructs without any independent existence. Platonism is not without its critics. Philosopher W. V. O. Quine argues that the belief in abstract forms lacks empirical evidence and is overly speculative. Critics also point to the challenge of explaining how these abstract forms interact with the physical world. Despite these criticisms, the enduring influence of Platonism is evident in various branches of philosophy.'),
    ('reading-rap-2f249da7401dcbe241d03543', 'The word “foster” in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word “foster” in the passage is closest in meaning to predict support reveal reflect The passage mentions each of the following about children who participate in arts programs EXCEPT: 12. The passage mentions each of the following about children who participate in arts programs EXCEPT: They develop lasting artistic abilities. They demonstrate improved emotional growth. They improve their reading and math abilities. Their learning environments may be more positive. Which of the sentences below best expresses the essential information in the highlighted sentence in the passage? Incorrect choices change the meaning in important ways or leave out essential information. 13. Which of the sentences below best expresses the essential information in the highlighted sentence in the passage? Incorrect choices change the meaning in important ways or leave out essential information. For children, building a sense of belonging and shared purpose is a more important benefit than improving academic achievement. Different forms of arts programs promote varying levels of motivation, engagement, and academic achievement. Participation in arts programs helps children develop greater motivation and engagement, which supports better academic achievement. When children participate in arts programs, they may improve academic achievement despite being distracted from academic studies. The passage indicates which of the following about the “studies”? 14. The passage indicates which of the following about the “studies”? They do not clearly explain why they establish correlations between arts program participation and academic success. They do not definitively show that academic success is a result of participating in arts programs. They should have compared the effects of different kinds of arts programs on academic performance. They do not use a persuasive way of measuring academic success. According to the author, the “mixed findings” are partly due to 15. According to the author, the “mixed findings” are partly due to differences in the ages at which children first enroll in arts programs differences in the ways in which teachers define learning goals the limited expertise of researchers in arts education the relatively brief duration of some arts programs Several recent studies suggest that children''s participation in arts programs, whether visual arts, music, drama, or dance, can foster cognitive development. Empirical studies have highlighted positive correlations between arts engagement and improvements in reading, mathematics, language development, and social skills. Similarly, comprehensive analyses emphasize that arts education contributes to both cognitive and emotional growth, which in turn supports academic success. Broader educational reports reinforce these findings. Schools that integrate the arts into their core curricula often document more positive learning environments. When children collaborate in theater, music ensembles, or group visual arts projects, they build a sense of belonging and shared purpose—key components of motivation and engagement, factors known to influence academic achievement. Though persuasive, the evidence is not uniformly conclusive. Some studies rely on correlational data—meaning that they demonstrate that arts program participation is related to academic success without proving that arts programs cause academic success. Researchers also note that the benefits of arts education often depend on factors such as teacher expertise and skillful integration of the arts with broader learning goals. And, in some cases, arts programs are too brief or inconsistently delivered to produce measurable academic effects, which may partly explain mixed findings in the literature.'),
    ('reading-rap-31f04816b009eb25fe882757', 'The word “pivotal” in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word “pivotal” in the passage is closest in meaning to critical unexpected temporary positive The passage suggests that audience interactivity influences online writing styles by 12. The passage suggests that audience interactivity influences online writing styles by making the tone of a written work more important than its content allowing audiences to imitate the writing styles of their favorite authors rewarding writers who produce more dynamic and responsive material enabling authors to express complex ideas using simple and informal language Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as an effect of the rise of digital media? 13. Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as an effect of the rise of digital media? The style of print media is becoming more formal. Writers are getting to know their readers more directly. Language has been acquiring new elements. Online content is becoming less formal. Which of the following is mentioned in the passage as a result of integrating multimedia elements in writing? 14. Which of the following is mentioned in the passage as a result of integrating multimedia elements in writing? It helps maintain traditional writing styles. It can become a distraction for the reader. It guarantees improved reader comprehension. It simplifies the writing process significantly. There are four locations in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. On the other hand, such limitations can encourage greater creativity. Digital media''s rise has transformed writing styles, creating new genres and challenges. Unlike traditional print media, digital platforms enable instantaneous sharing, forcing writers to adapt their craft. Blogs, tweets, and other forms prioritize brevity. A pivotal change is the heightened focus on audience interactivity. Writers must now be mindful of how their work is received and discussed online, adjusting their style in response to audience feedback. This can lead to more dynamic writing but requires constant shifts in tone and content. Interaction between writers and readers thus becomes a crucial element of modern writing. Digital media also influences language evolution, introducing abbreviations, slang, and emojis as communication tools. Writers face the challenge of balancing informal language with clarity and professionalism. This language shift enriches writing but raises concerns about preserving linguistic standards. Along with all the disruptions, digital media also offers avenues for innovation. Writers can seamlessly integrate multimedia elements, crafting immersive narratives that go beyond text. However, this integration calls for careful evaluation of how these elements impact the core message, which can sometimes be overshadowed by the allure of visual and auditory effects.'),
    ('reading-rap-34f6bce8fefcc9ea8c94efb8', 'Why does the author mention the “higher prevalence of metabolic disorders”? 11. Why does the author mention the “higher prevalence of metabolic disorders”? To suggest a causal relationship between metabolic illness and sleep disturbances To explain why circadian misalignment is increasing in urban environments To support the claim that circadian misalignment affects sleep patterns To describe one effect of the disruption of circadian rhythms by artificial light Why might some nocturnal animal adaptations be important for human therapies? 12. Why might some nocturnal animal adaptations be important for human therapies? They provide evidence that light exposure is the greatest driver of circadian rhythms. They offer clues about thriving in conditions that cause circadian misalignment in people. They show that melatonin is not always effective in regulating sleep patterns. They prove that animals'' melatonin production cycles closely resemble those of humans. What does the author suggest about simulating natural light cycles as a way of resetting circadian clocks? 13. What does the author suggest about simulating natural light cycles as a way of resetting circadian clocks? Most people experiencing circadian misalignment have seen no benefit from the treatment. Most researchers agree that this treatment is currently the only effective approach. The approach is most effective for people with a genetic predisposition for sleep disorders. This approach has been successfully used to help many Individuals restore their sleep patterns. What is the relationship between paragraph 2 and paragraph 3? 14. What is the relationship between paragraph 2 and paragraph 3? Paragraph 2 defines a concept; paragraph 3 provides more detail by discussing specific examples. Paragraph 2 evaluates a focused approach; paragraph 3 points to broader challenges and solutions. Paragraph 2 proposes a theory; paragraph 3 notes potential objections to itand suggests responses. Paragraph 2 outlines a methodology; paragraph 3 reports the results of applying it to a problem. The word "rectify" in the passage is closest in meaning to 15. The word "rectify" in the passage is closest in meaning to disclose approve relieve document Circadian rhythms, the internal clocks regulating organisms'' physiological processes, are primarily driven by light exposure. These rhythms may be disrupted by the increased artificial light of urban environments, leading to what scientists call "circadian misalignment." This misalignment not only affects sleep patterns but is linked to a higher prevalence of metabolic disorders. Interestingly, research on nocturnal animals reveals that these creatures have evolved mechanisms, such as unique melatonin production cycles, for thriving in conditions that disrupt human circadian rhythms. Such adaptations could inform potential human therapies. Recent studies suggest that manipulating light exposure can help reset circadian clocks. Experiments demonstrated that disrupted rhythms can be realigned by the simulation of natural light cycles. However, this approach is not universally effective. Some individuals experience persistent misalignment, suggesting that other environmental or genetic factors may play significant roles. Moreover, the factors affecting circadian regulation extend beyond light: temperature, diet, and social interactions all influence these rhythms. This complex causal web requires a multidisciplinary approach to develop comprehensive solutions. Cutting-edge research is now exploring therapeutic drugs that can rectify disruptions to the natural circadian rhythmic cycles. Whether these efforts will yield sustainable treatment options remains to be seen.'),
    ('reading-rap-36ed75af95859100231f11d2', 'The word "aspiration" in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word "aspiration" in the passage is closest in meaning to effort preference ambition achievement Paragraph 2 implies that the curricula used in small boarding schools for girls 12. Paragraph 2 implies that the curricula used in small boarding schools for girls emphasized the learning of skills that women were unlikely to use later in life attempted to expose girls to a wide range of subjects provided a model that was adapted for the home-schooling of middle-class girls were dictated by views about the way women were expected to function in elite society Which of the following best describes the relationship between paragraph 1 and paragraph 2? 13. Which of the following best describes the relationship between paragraph 1 and paragraph 2? Paragraph 1 outlines certain attitudes that were common during a historical period, and paragraph 2 explains why it was difficult to change those attitudes. Paragraph 1 presents a thesis about a historical period, and paragraph 2 evaluates the validity of that thesis in light of specific evidence. Paragraph 1 describes key views held during a historical period, and paragraph 2 provides specific examples of how those views affected different parts of society. Paragraph 1 summarizes certain views that shaped a historical period, and paragraph 2 identifies certain contradictions among those views. According to the passage, Mary Astell and Mary Wollstonecraft based their arguments challenging the prevailing norms on which of the following claims? 14. According to the passage, Mary Astell and Mary Wollstonecraft based their arguments challenging the prevailing norms on which of the following claims? The capacity for reason does not differ by gender. The education of women would yield significant social reforms. Intellectual development is essential for the cultivation of moral virtue. Social class should not determine the level of education people receive. Why does the author mention Enlightenment discourse in paragraph 3? 15. Why does the author mention Enlightenment discourse in paragraph 3? To highlight the contradictory nature of Enlightenment views about women To identify a philosophical trend that Mary Astell and Mary Wollstonecraft rejected To point out an early philosophical foundation of feminism To provide an example of a movement that welcomed educated women into its ranks Education for girls in eighteenth-century England reflected entrenched assumptions about gender, social hierarchy, and learning. Dominant attitudes held that women required only limited instruction—sufficient to cultivate virtue, domestic skills, and refinement. Intellectual aspiration was generally regarded as unhealthy for women. Access to schooling reflected class distinctions. Girls from elite families often attended small boarding schools with curricula that emphasized so-called accomplishments such as music, drawing, needlework, modern languages, and basic literacy, subjects intended to enhance sociability rather than foster intellectual development. Middle-class girls typically received home-schooling, combining reading and writing with domestic training. For working-class girls, opportunities were minimal; many acquired only rudimentary literacy or vocational skills, while others received no schooling at all. Yet the period also saw the emergence of challenges to the prevailing norms. The philosophers Mary Astell and Mary Wollstonecraft contended that women possessed the same rational faculties as men and merited an education oriented toward intellectual development rather than ornamental refinement. Their critiques exposed tensions within eighteenth-century Enlightenment discourse, which celebrated reason while nonetheless denying its cultivation to women. Although these critiques did not immediately transform educational practice, they articulated a philosophical foundation that would inspire later feminist thinkers and broaden conceptions of women''s intellectual and civic potential.'),
    ('reading-rap-3c7da315889dceed32412dcf', 'The main purpose of this passage is to 31. The main purpose of this passage is to describe the birth of veganism in the United States list the benefits of a vegan diet analyze some changes in Americans'' attitudes toward veganism identify trends in American consumer preferences The author notes all of the following as factors driving the popularity of veganism EXCEPT 32. The author notes all of the following as factors driving the popularity of veganism EXCEPT concern for animals considerations about health the high cost of consuming meat a desire to protect the environment The word "involved" in the passage is closest in meaning to 33. The word "involved" in the passage is closest in meaning to unsatisfying expensive unpleasant complicated The author mentions "a vegan cheese substitute" primarily in order to 34. The author mentions "a vegan cheese substitute" primarily in order to note a challenge inherent in preparing vegan meals identify a popular item used in many vegan recipes contrast the healthfulness of vegan and animal-based foods describe a cooking process popularized by vegans The passage suggests which of the following about commercial meat-alternative products? 35. The passage suggests which of the following about commercial meat-alternative products? They became increasingly popular with home cooks during the mid-2020s. They are produced in a way that causes some people to doubt their healthfulness. They are not popular with Americans concerned about food allergies. They have greatly improved in flavor since their introduction. Veganism, a dietary approach that excludes all animal-based foods, has experienced notable shifts in popularity in the United States. A study conducted in 2020 showed a dramatic increase in the number of Americans adopting a plant-based diet between 2004 and 2019. This growth was fueled by expanding awareness of animal welfare, environmental concerns linked to industrial agriculture, and increased emphasis on healthy eating. During the 2010s, companies began to market plant-based meat alternatives that became highly popular with both restaurants and home cooks. By the mid-2020s, however, momentum began to level off. Surveys in 2025 indicated that only about three to four percent of Americans identified as vegan. Several factors contributed to this plateau. Preparing vegan meals can be more involved than preparing meat-based meals. For example, a vegan cheese substitute can be made with nuts, but the process is time-consuming. And while home cooks can buy commercially manufactured vegan foods, those foods are highly processed, raising concerns about their healthfulness. Additionally, some social media influencers have begun to promote increased protein consumption, sparking increased consumer demand for meat. American food trends come and go, but while some consumers adopt extreme diets, Americans overall seem to be favoring more flexibility in their eating habits.'),
    ('reading-rap-3cce0c715d4cc1638ab8e640', 'How did music therapy in its modern form begin? 26. How did music therapy in its modern form begin? As a way to improve the cognitive functioning of soldiers As a result of the careful study of ancient healing practices As a means to treat people suffering from Alzheimer''s disease As a tool to help veterans cope with the effects of the war The word “trigger” in the passage is closest in meaning to 27. The word “trigger” in the passage is closest in meaning to bring shoot heal enjoy Why does the author mention listening to music daily for 30 minutes? 28. Why does the author mention listening to music daily for 30 minutes? To indicate how long a music therapy session should last To support a claim about music therapy''s effectiveness To explain why a study was conducted in a hospital To suggest that music therapy should be practiced at home According to the passage, all of the following can affect the benefit of a music therapy session for a patient EXCEPT 29. According to the passage, all of the following can affect the benefit of a music therapy session for a patient EXCEPT what kind of music the patient prefers which culture the patient is from in what environment the music is played how knowledgeable the patient is about music There are four locations in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 30. There are four locations in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Both cognitive and social benefits persisted long after the treatment sessions had ended. Music therapy is known for its powerful effects on mental health. By using musical interventions, therapists aim to improve people''s emotional well-being and cognitive functioning. The roots of music therapy trace back to ancient healing rituals, but its modern form appeared after World War II, when musicians played for veterans suffering from trauma. Research shows that music therapy can reduce anxiety and stress. Listening to calming music can lower heart rates and cortisol levels. This is why music therapy is often used in hospitals. A study found that patients who listened to 30 minutes of music daily experienced significant reductions in anxiety. Engaging with familiar music can also trigger pleasant memories and improve mood. Patients who participated in music therapy showed better memory recall and increased social interaction. A study demonstrated that Alzheimer''s patients who listened to music from their youth had improved cognitive function. Despite its benefits, music therapy is not a one-size-fits-all solution. Its effectiveness varies depending on the individual and type of music used. Personal musical preferences, cultural background, and the context in which the music is played are crucial to the therapy''s success.'),
    ('reading-rap-4042fb1dc21bdf042d6ea366', 'What is the passage mainly about? 11. What is the passage mainly about? The influence of traditional theater on modern audiences The role of audience participation in theatrical performances New approaches to theatrical storytelling and audience involvement The challenges of interpreting nonlinear narratives in drama According to the passage, what is a key difference between Mother Courage and Her Children and Sleep No More? 12. According to the passage, what is a key difference between Mother Courage and Her Children and Sleep No More? Mother Courage and Her Children uses audience interaction to create emotional immersion, while Sleep No More avoids direct engagement. Mother Courage and Her Children involves some interaction with the audience, while Sleep No More involves actual audience participation. Mother Courage and Her Children is based on historical events, while Sleep No More is entirely fictional. Mother Courage and Her Children presents a fragmented narrative, while Sleep No More follows a linear storyline. Which of the following best describes the function of paragraph 3 in the passage? 13. Which of the following best describes the function of paragraph 3 in the passage? It offers a conjecture about what new playwriting techniques mean for theater. It summarizes the historical development of experimental theater techniques. It critiques the effectiveness of audience-centered performance styles. It introduces a counterargument to the use of nonlinear narratives in drama. The word "evoke" in the passage is closest in meaning to 14. The word "evoke" in the passage is closest in meaning to measure produce compare anticipate The passage introduces all of the following innovative approaches to playwriting EXCEPT 15. The passage introduces all of the following innovative approaches to playwriting EXCEPT presenting events out of order actors speaking to audiences viewers walking onto the stage playwrights joining the cast Innovative playwriting techniques have transformed the theatrical landscape by challenging linear storytelling and passive spectatorship. Nonlinear narratives, seen in plays like Harold Pinter''s Betrayal, disrupt chronological order to reflect the fragmented nature of memory and emotion. This structure invites audiences to actively reconstruct the story, deepening their engagement. Another innovation is the so-called breaking of the fourth wall, where characters directly address the audience. Used by Bertolt Brecht in Mother Courage and Her Children, this technique encourages viewers to reflect critically rather than become absorbed in illusion. Fully interactive theater, exemplified by the British production Sleep No More, goes further by allowing viewers to physically move through the performance space, personalizing the experience and making each viewing unique. These techniques suggest a broader hypothesis: that theatrical form is not merely a container for content but a vital part of creating meaning. By reshaping narrative structure and audience dynamics, playwrights can evoke new emotional and intellectual responses, revealing complex aspects of the human condition. Of course, such innovations may confuse rather than enlighten more traditional-minded theater audiences and also disrupt their emotional immersion and fragment the narrative. Thus, while these techniques can serve as powerful tools, their success depends on thoughtful execution and a clear artistic purpose.'),
    ('reading-rap-42058f5ff5d864b3435a87be', 'The word “stark” in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word “stark” in the passage is closest in meaning to severe classic graceful natural The passage suggests which of the following about Sontag? 12. The passage suggests which of the following about Sontag? She believed that Arbus called too much attention to her own technique in her portraits. She felt that Arbus misunderstood her subjects. She viewed Arbus'' photography as exploitive. She admired Arbus'' style but criticized her subject matter. The author mentions “complexity and dignity” primarily in order to 13. The author mentions “complexity and dignity” primarily in order to identify traits that Arbus looked for when choosing photographic subjects illustrate how some viewers of Arbus'' work have rebutted a negative view of her highlight one way in which Arbus'' photographs are unconventional explain why Arbus preferred frontal composition to other ways of posing subjects The passage indicates that Arbus'' style 14. The passage indicates that Arbus'' style helps explain why Arbus'' subjects often collaborated willingly in the creation of their portraits reflected Arbus'' preference for an unusual type of photographic film influenced the way in which other photographers approached portraiture has been viewed by some people as effective in revealing an authentic reality It can be inferred that the author regards the disagreements created by Arbus'' photographs as 15. It can be inferred that the author regards the disagreements created by Arbus'' photographs as evidence that Arbus has been misunderstood essential to the photographs'' power as works of art a reflection of the influence of Sontag on Arbus'' reputation arising from false assumptions about Arbus'' style Diane Arbus (1923–1971) occupies a singular place in the history of photography, celebrated and criticized for her stark, intimate portraits of a wide range of people, including some of unconventional appearance or marginalized social status. Her work has long provoked debate because it resists easy moral or aesthetic categorization. Some critics, such as Susan Sontag, argued that Arbus'' photographs create a troubling distance between viewer and subject. Sontag claimed that Arbus'' images risk turning people into spectacles of oddity, suggesting that her gaze could be predatory in its fascination with difference. Others, however, see Arbus as a profoundly empathetic artist. Writers like Arthur Lubow emphasize her ability to reveal her subjects'' complexity and dignity, noting that many of them collaborated willingly and even joyfully in the creation of their portraits. A further line of debate surrounds Arbus'' style: her use of frontal composition, square format, and direct flash has been interpreted negatively—as a cold, clinical approach—and positively—as a method that strips away artifice to expose deeper truths. Ultimately, Arbus'' work continues to inspire disagreement because it challenges viewers to confront their own assumptions about normalcy, beauty, and vulnerability. Her photographs remain powerful precisely because they refuse to resolve these tensions.'),
    ('reading-rap-458773fbc1c8f2b64fa959c6', 'Why does the author mention still objects and sudden motion? 31. Why does the author mention still objects and sudden motion? To explain why it is important for the brain to faithfully record what is in front of us To emphasize the enormous amounts of visual stimuli that enter the brain To illustrate how the human brain prioritizes elements necessary for survival To provide an example of a flaw of the filtering mechanism The phrase "its presence" in the passage refers to the presence of 32. The phrase "its presence" in the passage refers to the presence of the photoreceptor cell the blind spot incoming light the brain The word "simultaneously" in the passage is closest in meaning to 33. The word "simultaneously" in the passage is closest in meaning to by mistake at the same time eventually unfortunately According to the passage, when you are looking for a green book in a crowded room, each of the following is true EXCEPT: 34. According to the passage, when you are looking for a green book in a crowded room, each of the following is true EXCEPT: You become sensitive to green wavelengths. You may overlook objects of other colors. You may experience perceptual errors. You become sensitive to green objects that are in a shadow. What is the relationship between paragraphs 2 and 3? 35. What is the relationship between paragraphs 2 and 3? Paragraph 2 presents a theory of how vision works, and paragraph 3 presents a different theory. Paragraph 2 discusses an example of the complexity of vision, and paragraph 3 discusses another example. Paragraph 2 introduces a familiar phenomenon, and paragraph 3 explains what causes this phenomenon. Paragraph 2 describes the advantages of having a blind spot, and paragraph 3 describes the disadvantages. Far from functioning as a simple and faithful recording of what is in front of us, vision involves parts of the brain that engage in a complex interplay of processes. Specialized pathways filter enormous amounts of visual stimuli, prioritizing elements necessary for survival. For example, we are more likely to notice sudden motion than still objects. You may be familiar with the blind spot, the small portion of our visual field where the optic nerve passes through the retina. No photoreceptor cell exists in this region to detect incoming light. Yet, most people are entirely unaware of its presence. The brain compensates for this missing visual data, filling it in based on patterns and colors from surrounding areas. Also consider the role of attention in vision. If you are searching for a green book in a crowded room, your visual mechanisms amplify sensitivity to green wavelengths while simultaneously suppressing red or blue wavelengths. This ability, while useful, can lead to perceptual errors because heightened sensitivity comes at the cost of reduced tolerance for variation. This is why you might overlook a green object that falls under shadow.'),
    ('reading-rap-4986420f2a3c5df66f0fe8d2', 'The word “harness” in the passage is closest in meaning to 26. The word “harness” in the passage is closest in meaning to limit distribute release exploit What unique advantage do floating wind turbines have over traditional turbines? 27. What unique advantage do floating wind turbines have over traditional turbines? They are larger in size and have longer blades. They require less maintenance. They can be placed in deeper waters. They are easier to design. Why does the author mention “buoyancy principles”? 28. Why does the author mention “buoyancy principles”? To point out a challenge of creating floating turbines To suggest a new area for future research into floating turbines To emphasize the greater flexibility of floating turbines compared with traditional turbines To provide an example of cost-saving measures related to floating turbines What can be inferred about the acceleration of “wear and tear” in floating turbines? 29. What can be inferred about the acceleration of “wear and tear” in floating turbines? Any attempts to reduce it will likely prove to be unsuccessful. Scientists are working to control it through better designs and materials. It is prompting engineers to place floating wind turbines closer to shore. It makes floating turbines especially unattractive to look at. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 30. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Others assess economic models that might offset higher initial expenses. Wind turbines are large devices with long blades that capture wind energy and convert it into electricity. Floating wind turbines are a special type of wind turbines because they are not fixed to the seabed but are anchored using advanced mooring systems, allowing placement in deeper waters where winds are more consistent. In this context, anchoring refers to securing the turbine in place using cables or chains connected to weighted structures or anchors on the ocean floor, ensuring stability despite the lack of a fixed foundation. The deeper placement means the wind turbines can harness untapped wind resources that traditional turbines cannot access. Engineering floating turbines involves unique challenges. They rely on buoyancy principles rather than on solid ground, making their design and maintenance complex. Despite the increased costs, their ability to reduce visual and noise pollution makes them an attractive option for distant offshore locations. However, their long-term viability is threatened by harsh marine conditions that can accelerate wear and tear. Research continues to explore solutions for these problems. Some experts are investigating materials and designs to enhance durability. As efforts to capitalize on deeper offshore wind resources persist, floating turbines represent both a promising opportunity and a complex engineering puzzle.'),
    ('reading-rap-50450a7f34a629d0152abd52', 'What is implied about the building of temporary shelters by octopuses? 11. What is implied about the building of temporary shelters by octopuses? It has been observed in laboratories but not in the wild. It shows that octopuses can carry surprisingly heavy objects. It reveals that octopuses are capable of complex planning. It is an ineffective way for octopuses to protect themselves from predators. The word "manipulating" in the passage is closest in meaning to 12. The word "manipulating" in the passage is closest in meaning to understanding operating recognizing hiding Paragraph 2 indicates all of the following about octopuses EXCEPT: 13. Paragraph 2 indicates all of the following about octopuses EXCEPT: They are able to understand how predators perceive color. They can recognize the color of their surroundings. They are able to perceive the patterns in their surroundings. They can alter their appearance almost instantly. The passage indicates which of the following about biological pathways? 14. The passage indicates which of the following about biological pathways? Species that develop along very different pathways can come to possess surprisingly similar cognitive abilities. The biological pathways of octopuses and vertebrates have been difficult for scientists to determine. Octopuses'' biological pathway came to resemble the pathway of vertebrates around 500 million years ago. The cognitive abilities observed in certain species have caused scientists to reject a traditional way of understanding biological pathways. The author mentions a "separate model of intelligence" primarily to 15. The author mentions a "separate model of intelligence" primarily to introduce a theory of intelligence proposed by researchers who study octopuses emphasize that different biological pathways tend to lead to very different cognitive abilities explain how octopuses can be capable of long-term memory show how the study of octopuses has opened up new ways of thinking about intelligence Octopuses'' behaviors reveal remarkably sophisticated cognition. Researchers have documented octopuses'' use of tools—for example, gathering seashells to assemble temporary shelters. This behavior indicates not only problem-solving but also foresight, since the octopus must carry and later arrange the objects in a different context. Their ability to navigate mazes, remember solutions, and learn from experience further demonstrates a form of long-term memory unusual among invertebrates. In laboratory settings, octopuses have also been observed opening jars, manipulating latches, and escaping enclosures—tasks requiring both skill and an understanding of cause and effect. Another striking ability of octopuses is their rapid camouflage. Octopuses can alter their skin color, texture, and pattern in milliseconds to match their surroundings. This strategy for evading predators reflects complex sensory processing: They must interpret visual information and translate it into precise bodily changes. These capabilities are especially surprising because the evolution of octopuses took a different course from that of vertebrates more than 500 million years ago, meaning that their intelligence evolved independently. This deep evolutionary separation suggests that complex cognition can arise through very different biological pathways. For researchers, octopuses offer a rare opportunity to study an entirely separate model of intelligence, expanding our understanding of how minds can develop across the tree of life.'),
    ('reading-rap-50cfc4c5fd006c1ec78a60b8', 'What does the passage indicate about the "chemical changes" in the marble of the Getty Kouros? 11. What does the passage indicate about the "chemical changes" in the marble of the Getty Kouros? They could not have occurred naturally. They were caused by scientists testing the marble''s age. They explain some inconsistencies in features of the Getty Kouros. They may suggest an ancient origin for the marble of the Getty Kouros. The author mentions "a transitional moment in Greek sculpture" primarily in order to 12. The author mentions "a transitional moment in Greek sculpture" primarily in order to question a claim about sixth-century B.C.E. kouroi point out how some people explain the style of the Getty Kouros support the view that artistic styles evolve over time characterize sculptures typical of the Greek Archaic period What is the main reason that the authenticity of the Getty Kouros is in doubt? 13. What is the main reason that the authenticity of the Getty Kouros is in doubt? Modern forgers are known to have created similar sculptures. The Greeks did not create many sculptures during the sixth century B.C.E. Observers may have misunderstood Greek Archaic elements. The Getty Kouros combines styles that are too diverse. According to the passage, what is known about marble? 14. According to the passage, what is known about marble? It can be quickly produced by forgers in large amounts. It is difficult for forgers to imitate. It can be made to appear older than it actually is. Its traits could affect the style of Greek sculptures. Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage? 15. Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage? The Getty Kouros is not typical of Greek kouroi and may or may not be genuine. The Getty Kouros is composed of marble that has probably been artificially aged. The Getty Kouros is probably of Greek origin but unlikely to be from the Archaic period. The Getty Kouros was likely forged by a sculptor with limited knowledge of Greek sculptural traditions. In 1985, the J. Paul Getty Museum acquired what it believed to be a Greek kouros (a statue of a naked youth) dating from the Archaic period (650-480 B.C.E.), estimating the date of origin as approximately 530 B.C.E. The Getty Kouros''s authenticity soon came into question, however. Supporters cited scientific analyses suggesting that the statue''s marble is consistent with marble known to have come from ancient Greek quarries and that its surface shows chemical changes consistent with long. term burial. Some scholars also argue that peculiar inconsistencies in the statue''s style—it incorporates features from different regions and periods—could reflect a transitional moment in Greek sculpture. Skeptics contend that these inconsistencies undermine the case for authenticity. The Kouros combines traits from several other known sixth-century B.C.E. kouroi in a way that appears too eclectic to be genuine. To these observers, the statue looks like a modern forger''s creation—a confused imitation of Greek Archaic elements rather than the genuine product of a coherent sculptural tradition. Furthermore, scientists recognize that modern forgers can artificially age marble. Because neither scientific analysis nor stylistic evaluation yields decisive results, the question of the Kouros''s authenticity remains unresolved.'),
    ('reading-rap-51f18b2b4046d93a70bdd300', 'How does the placebo effect challenge traditional views on treatment? 11. How does the placebo effect challenge traditional views on treatment? By showing that patients should have more say in selecting their medications By suggesting that a patient’s belief can be as powerful as a medicine By pointing out a possible way to reduce the costs of clinical trials By questioning existing measurements of the effectiveness of a treatment What does paragraph 2 indicate about the placebo effect? 12. What does paragraph 2 indicate about the placebo effect? It affects patients much more powerfully than actual painkillers do. It may be related to the release of endorphins in the brain. It is enhanced when it is accompanied by actual painkillers. It has not been connected by researchers to any physiological changes. The word “deliberately” in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word “deliberately” in the passage is closest in meaning to on purpose with caution efficiently regularly Why does the author mention “autonomy and transparency in patient care”? 14. Why does the author mention “autonomy and transparency in patient care”? To argue for the use of the placebo effect To highlight an ethical challenge in using the placebo effect To suggest that trust is less important than other considerations in healthcare To explain how the placebo effect is misunderstood There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Researchers have long been puzzled by the mechanisms behind this phenomenon. The placebo effect challenges conventional ideas about medical treatment efficacy. What is a placebo? A patient’s condition sometimes improves if they believe that they are receiving an effective treatment, even if they are in fact receiving a sugar pill. In this example, the sugar pill, which has no active ingredient, is a placebo. Surprisingly, this effect can sometimes rival real medications in clinical trials. Recent studies suggest that the placebo effect might be rooted in the brain’s complex chemistry. When a patient anticipates positive effects, their brain can release chemicals like endorphins, which may cause genuine physiological changes. In one study, patients receiving placebo “painkillers” reported pain relief comparable to the one produced by actual painkillers, highlighting the brain’s powerful role. Some researchers propose using the placebo effect deliberately to enhance therapy outcomes, arguing that the mind-body connection should be tapped into more. However, ethical considerations arise: deceiving patients could undermine trust in healthcare. For example, even when a placebo leads to measurable improvement, knowingly administering it without full disclosure may violate principles of informed consent, raising questions about autonomy and transparency in patient care. This delicate balance between harnessing the placebo effect and maintaining ethical standards continues to be a topic of debate.'),
    ('reading-rap-51f55bc67989f22a818f028c', 'The passage indicates that urbanization 11. The passage indicates that urbanization was a factor in generating public interest in criminals led to significant changes in forensic science helped to increase the market for contemporary fiction created conditions in which criminal organizations grew bolder The word ‘sensational’ in the passage is closest in meaning to 12. The word ‘sensational’ in the passage is closest in meaning to mysterious shocking commonplace understandable The author mentions ‘The Murders in the Rue Morgue’ primarily to 13. The author mentions ‘The Murders in the Rue Morgue’ primarily to highlight a way in which fiction reflected rapid social change cite a work responsible for increasing the demand for detective fiction distinguish Poe''s story from later works of detective fiction note the invention of a particular type of character The passage suggests that a distinctive characteristic of nineteenth-century detective fiction was 14. The passage suggests that a distinctive characteristic of nineteenth-century detective fiction was the depiction of criminals as individuals driven by cultural anxieties the depiction of the detective as an inventor of new forensic techniques the incorporation of details taken from newspaper accounts of real crimes the celebration of logic and reasoning as counterforces to social disorder The passage indicates which of the following about detective fiction? 15. The passage indicates which of the following about detective fiction? It quickly became more popular with readers than other types of fiction. It contributed to the notoriety of certain real-life criminals. It met a psychological need common to many members of the public. It was often written by authors who had professional policing experience. Modern detective fiction emerged in the nineteenth century, a time of rapid social change, new technologies, and shifting ideas about crime and human psychology. Its roots lie partly in the rise of urbanization: As cities like London and Paris expanded, crime became more visible and more sensational, creating public fascination with both criminals and their pursuers. This environment helped inspire Edgar Allan Poe''s ‘The Murders in the Rue Morgue’ (1841), which introduced the archetype of the brilliant, analytical investigator. Equally important was the development of professional policing. France''s first investigative bureau, the Sûreté, was established in 1812 and London''s Metropolitan Police in 1829, inspiring writers with real models of investigative procedures. Advances in forensic science—fingerprinting, photography, and early criminology—reinforced the idea that crime could be solved through observation and logic. By the time Sir Arthur Conan Doyle introduced Sherlock Holmes in 1887, readers were primed for a detective who embodied scientific reasoning and methodical deduction. Cultural anxieties also played a role. Industrialization created both social disorder and a longing for narratives in which chaos was ultimately overcome. Detective fiction met that need, offering intellectual pleasure and moral reassurance. Together, these forces spawned a genre that continues to evolve, feeding our fascination with mysteries and their resolution.'),
    ('reading-rap-520073d086537b70cc6ba0fa', 'According to the passage, what significant achievement in fusion research occurred in 1957? 11. According to the passage, what significant achievement in fusion research occurred in 1957? Researchers successfully fused deuterium and tritium atoms for the first time. Researchers demonstrated that magnetic fields could contain extremely hot plasma. Researchers estimated the temperature of the sun’s core to be 150 million degrees Celsius. Researchers discovered the basic principle that explains the existence of heavy forms of hydrogen. Why does the author mention plasma touching reactor walls? 12. Why does the author mention plasma touching reactor walls? To explain how the plasma is contained within the reactor To point out how researchers cool plasma after heating it for experiments To demonstrate the final step in achieving nuclear fusion To clarify why magnetic confinement techniques are necessary The word “sophisticated” in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word “sophisticated” in the passage is closest in meaning to advanced large expensive successful According to the passage, what is ITER’s primary objective? 14. According to the passage, what is ITER’s primary objective? To generate commercial electricity for distribution to power grids To demonstrate that magnetic confinement techniques are not environmentally sustainable To prove that fusion reactions can produce more energy than they consume To challenge decades of magnetic confinement research Which of the following is mentioned as one factor that currently prevents magnetic fusion from generating commercial electricity? 15. Which of the following is mentioned as one factor that currently prevents magnetic fusion from generating commercial electricity? Insufficient global supplies of reactor materials Inability to achieve intense neutron radiation High energy demands for plasma maintenance Lack of magnetic fields that are powerful enough Magnetic confinement fusion is a promising approach for generating clean electricity. This technology fuses deuterium and tritium, heavy forms of hydrogen, to release energy while producing minimal radioactive waste when compared to current technologies to produce nuclear power. The basic principle was first demonstrated in 1957: powerful magnetic fields contain extremely hot plasma at temperatures reaching 150 million degrees Celsius—ten times hotter than the sun’s core. Without magnetic containment, the plasma would instantly cool upon touching reactor walls, halting the fusion process. Since those early breakthroughs, researchers have built increasingly sophisticated magnetic confinement reactors called tokamaks. The International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor (ITER), currently under construction, represents the culmination of decades of magnetic confinement research. ITER aims to demonstrate sustained fusion reactions that produce more energy than they consume—a critical milestone called net energy gain. However, significant challenges remain before magnetic fusion can generate commercial electricity. Current experiments require enormous energy inputs to maintain plasma conditions, and the intense neutron radiation gradually damages reactor materials. Additionally, converting fusion energy into usable electricity requires developing efficient heat-to-power conversion systems. Despite these obstacles, successful demonstration of net energy gain could pave the way for fusion power plants in the 2040s.'),
    ('reading-rap-56ded9095ae86ede0b2a8c60', 'The passage mentions “well-structured environments” primarily to 26. The passage mentions “well-structured environments” primarily to highlight the role of leadership in fostering good organization emphasize the difference between leadership and facilitation describe groups in which there will be the least pressure to conform show that good leadership does not eliminate social pressures The passage suggests which of the following about “individuals with varied backgrounds and viewpoints”? 27. The passage suggests which of the following about “individuals with varied backgrounds and viewpoints”? They may help reduce the tendency to suppress dissenting opinions. They are more likely to agree quickly on important decisions. They tend to avoid conflict by deliberately seeking consensus. They often struggle to be heard in structured environments. The word “surface” in the passage is closest in meaning to 28. The word “surface” in the passage is closest in meaning to promote eliminate question identify The passage suggests which of the following about “intellectual rigor”? 29. The passage suggests which of the following about “intellectual rigor”? It emerges naturally in diverse groups without intervention. It is less important than achieving consensus in decision-making. It can be promoted by avoiding disagreement within the group. It requires intentional strategies to be attained in collaborative settings. Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as a possible cause of groupthink? 30. Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as a possible cause of groupthink? Group members’ fear of conflict or exclusion Group members’ pressure on each other to conform Group members’ lack of experience in decision-making Group members’ inability to promote constructive disagreement Group decision-making is often praised for its potential to foster creativity and innovation through diverse perspectives. However, this collaborative strength can paradoxically become a liability when the pursuit of consensus suppresses critical thinking. A well-known example is the phenomenon of “groupthink,” whereby individuals, fearing conflict or exclusion, withhold dissenting opinions. This can result in decisions that are not only irrational but also poorly aligned with the group’s original goals. One might argue that groupthink is simply a failure of leadership or facilitation, yet even in well-structured environments, the pressure to conform can subtly influence behavior. Interestingly, some studies suggest that groups composed of individuals with varied backgrounds and viewpoints are less prone to groupthink, though this diversity can also introduce friction that slows decision-making. Alternatively, some theorists propose that the issue lies not in consensus-seeking itself, but in the lack of mechanisms to encourage constructive disagreement. For instance, assigning a “devil’s advocate” role or using anonymous feedback tools can help surface hidden concerns. Ultimately, while collaboration holds immense promise, its success depends on deliberate strategies to balance unity with intellectual rigor. Without such safeguards, the very process meant to enhance group cohesion may inadvertently undermine it.'),
    ('reading-rap-5817366a9ff4cc9c334fb39a', 'According to the passage, social capital refers to 31. According to the passage, social capital refers to how networks shape social interactions in the digital age the number of social connections an individual has the impact that individuals have on social networks the data sociologists use to make claims about digital networks What does the passage imply about homophily? 32. What does the passage imply about homophily? It ensures the dissemination of diverse ideas. It promotes similarity and continuity within groups. It emphasizes opinion leaders over network structures. It makes it unnecessary to have bridges across networks. Why does the author mention “echo chambers”? 33. Why does the author mention “echo chambers”? To highlight the risks of networks composed of like-minded individuals To introduce the role of opinion leaders in amplifying network structures To elaborate on the role of bridges in altering behaviors To identify one advantage of networks that connect various social classes The word “nuanced” in the passage is closest in meaning to 34. The word “nuanced” in the passage is closest in meaning to many complex powerful general There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. However, spreading information is not the only way to exert influence. In today’s digital age, social networks play a significant role in shaping opinions and behaviors. The influence of these networks is not merely about the number of connections one might have, but about the quality and nature of those interactions. Sociologists have long been interested in how individuals within a network can influence the group’s overall dynamics. This concept is sometimes referred to as social capital. The structure of a network can determine the flow of information. An individual positioned at the intersection of various groups, known as a bridge, often plays a crucial role in disseminating information. This person can introduce new ideas and perspectives to different parts of the network, thereby influencing opinions and possibly altering behaviors. Another factor is homophily, where individuals are more likely to form connections with others who are similar to themselves in terms of opinions, values, or social status. While homophily can reinforce existing beliefs, it can also create echo chambers, limiting exposure to diverse perspectives. The presence of influential individuals, often termed opinion leaders, can amplify or mitigate the effects of network structures by means of their social skills or charisma. Sociologists aim to uncover the nuanced ways social networks shape collective behavior.'),
    ('reading-rap-5a2d23cea681010acb956443', 'What does the passage indicate about sequential algorithms? 26. What does the passage indicate about sequential algorithms? They are less efficient than parallel algorithms are. They handle computational tasks in the same way that parallel algorithms do. They can solve problems that parallel algorithms cannot. They use more advanced hardware than parallel algorithms do. Why does the author discuss the simulation of the spread of a virus? 27. Why does the author discuss the simulation of the spread of a virus? To illustrate the challenges that public health officials face during disease outbreaks To demonstrate the significance of parallel algorithms in healthcare To highlight the data points that researchers focus on when a virus spreads through a population To emphasize the risk of using small datasets for analysis during disease outbreaks Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that indicates that parallel algorithms have challenges. Select the sentence to make your choice. 28. Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that indicates that parallel algorithms have challenges. Select the sentence to make your choice. The word “tailor” in the passage is closest in meaning to 29. The word “tailor” in the passage is closest in meaning to direct explore adapt prepare There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 30. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. In light of their many advantages, parallel algorithms will surely continue to drive innovation in diverse areas like climate modeling and financial analysis. Parallel algorithms, a computing method common in modern computers, solve problems faster by dividing a task into smaller parts that run at the same time. Instead of checking every item in a huge database one by one as in a sequential algorithm, a parallel algorithm might split the job across multiple processor cores, each checking a portion of the data simultaneously. In healthcare, parallel algorithms play a crucial role in analyzing large datasets quickly. When researchers simulate how a virus spreads through a population, they need to process millions of data points—like infection rates and demographics. Using parallel processing, these simulations can run in minutes instead of hours, helping public health officials make faster decisions during outbreaks. However, if too much data is sent to the processors at once without proper management, the system slows down. One of the strengths of parallel algorithms is their versatility. Developers can tailor them to work efficiently on different types of hardware, such as graphics processing units (GPUs), which are especially good at handling many tasks at once. This is a big reason why deep learning—used in things like facial recognition—has advanced so quickly. Training a deep learning model would be nearly impossible without parallel computing.'),
    ('reading-rap-5a5d313c494dc11f7d69121b', 'The word "integrates" in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word "integrates" in the passage is closest in meaning to improves requires produces combines The passage mentions all of the following as benefits of "efficiency" EXCEPT 12. The passage mentions all of the following as benefits of "efficiency" EXCEPT improved communication among medical professionals reduced reliance on the medical judgment of individuals faster delivery of medical care lower risk of medical mistakes In the second paragraph, what is the relationship between sentence 1 and sentence 2? 13. In the second paragraph, what is the relationship between sentence 1 and sentence 2? Sentence 1 describes changes in health outcomes, and sentence 2 provides examples. Sentence 1 presents a solution produced by health informatics, and sentence 2 describes flaws in that solution. Sentence 1 warns of problems related to health informatics, and sentence 2 provides examples. Sentence 1 notes a challenge that health informatics has solved, and sentence 2 describes new, unresolved challenges. Why does the passage provide the example of a patient flagged as low-risk for diabetes? 14. Why does the passage provide the example of a patient flagged as low-risk for diabetes? To illustrate how an algorithm might provide an incomplete assessment To show that body mass index is not a reliable health metric To suggest that family history is becoming more relevant to modern medicine To demonstrate the efficiency of digital diagnosis According to the passage, the tendency of health informatics to emphasize data that "fits neatly into templates" should cause doctors to 15. According to the passage, the tendency of health informatics to emphasize data that "fits neatly into templates" should cause doctors to watch closely for details that digital systems may not perceive work harder to streamline medical care participate in the design of digital records systems carefully evaluate their own ability to read electronic health data Health informatics, which integrates information technology and data science to enhance healthcare outcomes, is fundamentally reshaping how care is delivered. The transition from paper-based records to electronic health systems has significantly improved access to patient information and coordination among providers. Physicians can retrieve medical histories instantly, reducing the likelihood of errors and enabling more timely interventions. This efficiency, however, introduces new complexities. Some critics argue that the growing reliance on digital tools may have the unfortunate side effects of diminishing the role of clinicians'' intuition and reducing access to individualized care. For instance, when predictive algorithms assess disease risk using large datasets, they may fail to account for lifestyle choices and other unique patient variables that fall outside standard models. A patient might be flagged as low-risk for diabetes based on age and body mass index, while critical factors like family history or dietary habits are overlooked. In addition, the design of digital record systems can subtly influence how doctors think, drawing attention to whatever fits neatly into templates and downplaying things that may be harder to quantify. As a result, while informatics can streamline care, it also risks oversimplifying human health, challenging clinicians to stay alert to what the system might miss.'),
    ('reading-rap-5b0471ff27c2b69845312d0e', 'What is the passage mainly about? 11. What is the passage mainly about? A major historical event in medieval Europe An argument about the nature of abstract concepts A similarity between ancient Greek and medieval philosophies A legacy of two major medieval European thinkers According to the passage, which philosopher was a proponent of pure nominalism? 12. According to the passage, which philosopher was a proponent of pure nominalism? Abelard Aquinas Aristotle Plato Moderate realism claimed which of the following? 13. Moderate realism claimed which of the following? People can perceive universals by seeing particular objects. The idea of beauty is too abstract for the human mind to grasp. Nominalism focuses too much on people’s personal experience. Even particular things like flowers exist only in the human mind. The word “synthesis” in the passage is closest in meaning to 14. The word “synthesis” in the passage is closest in meaning to teaching improvement understanding combination There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Did universals exist in reality or only in language and the mind? In the European medieval period (between the fifth and fifteenth centuries), philosophy was a major field of study. Scholars like Peter Abelard and Thomas Aquinas were influenced by ancient Greek philosophy but also had their own ideas. A key focus was the nature of universals—whether abstract concepts like “beauty” or “justice” exist independently of the mind. Abelard, a twelfth-century philosopher, argued that universals are merely linguistic constructs, existing only in our language and thoughts. This nominalist perspective contrasted sharply with the realism of ancient Greek scholars like Plato, who believed that universals exist in an independent realm. Abelard''s views sparked intense debates among his contemporaries. Thomas Aquinas, influenced by the ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle, offered a different approach. He suggested that universals exist both in the mind and in reality. For Aquinas, the human mind perceives universals through experiences of particular things. Seeing a beautiful flower, for example, helps us grasp the idea of beauty itself. This theory, known as moderate realism, bridged the gap between nominalism and classical realism. Aquinas'' synthesis of Aristotelian philosophy and nominalism had an enduring impact on medieval thought, and the debate over universals shaped the entire medieval intellectual landscape.'),
    ('reading-rap-5b5f58e5ddc9c26304a64bba', 'The word “acute” in the passage is closest in meaning to 31. The word “acute” in the passage is closest in meaning to unexpected unwelcome severe growing Why does the author mention “welding aircraft frames, assembling munitions, and operating heavy machinery”? 32. Why does the author mention “welding aircraft frames, assembling munitions, and operating heavy machinery”? To acknowledge that certain kinds of work required specialized training To draw attention to the sectors with the greatest labor shortages To support the claim that 37 percent of the labor force worked in war industries To indicate the kinds of work that women began to perform during the Second World War What does the passage suggest about the government''s attitude toward women performing industrial labor? 33. What does the passage suggest about the government''s attitude toward women performing industrial labor? The government did not believe that women could perform industrial work as well as men. The government expected women to work only during a brief period when men were unavailable. The government was concerned that women were not fulfilling their patriotic duties. The government began to send women back home in 1944 when the end of the war was in sight. What is the nature of the debate among historians mentioned in the passage? 34. What is the nature of the debate among historians mentioned in the passage? Some historians emphasize the lasting effects of women''s wartime employment, while others see it as a short-lived phenomenon. Some historians argue that the Second World War benefited women, while most historians point to the hardships experienced by women during the war. Some historians criticize women for participating in war industries, while others praise them for their patriotism and bravery. Some historians highlight the gains women achieved in the 1940s, while others stress the continuity of traditional gender roles during the war period. The passage suggests that which of the following was a result of the end of the war? 35. The passage suggests that which of the following was a result of the end of the war? Women were shifted into different industries after defense contracts ended. Men replaced women in many industrial jobs. Women gladly gave up their jobs to return to their families. Employers lowered the pay of women. The Second World War created an acute labor shortage in the United States as millions of men entered military service, forcing factories, shipyards, and aircraft plants to seek new workers. In response, women were recruited into industrial jobs such as welding aircraft frames, assembling munitions, and operating heavy machinery—work that had previously been performed almost entirely by men. By 1945, women made up roughly 37 percent of the civilian labor force, an increase of about six million workers since 1940. Government campaigns framed this shift as both patriotic and temporary. Historians debate the significance of this wartime transformation. Some argue that women''s employment marked a lasting break with traditional gender roles, as women demonstrated their ability to perform skilled industrial labor. While the war did not produce immediate equality, they argue it helped lay the groundwork for later movements advocating equal pay and broader access to skilled occupations in the decades that followed. Others stress the short-lived nature of the change. When the war ended and men returned home, defense contracts were canceled and women were often dismissed. Female labor-force participation fell sharply in 1945 and 1946, approaching prewar levels as many women were encouraged—or required—to leave their jobs.'),
    ('reading-rap-5bf7db5133284be39a3b362d', 'What evidence does the passage present for the evolutionary advantages of laughter? 11. What evidence does the passage present for the evolutionary advantages of laughter? Laughter can be used to deceive people in social settings. Laughter triggers the release of chemicals that improve mood. Laughter can be a sign of awareness of one''s social context. Laughter is a necessary tool for interpersonal communication. Why does the passage mention the contagiousness of laughter at social events? 12. Why does the passage mention the contagiousness of laughter at social events? To illustrate the biological mechanism that causes laughter to spread among people in groups To provide evidence for the claim that laughter is a universal human behavior To show how laughter strengthens social bonds through shared group experiences To suggest that laughter primarily reflects individual emotional states The word "straightforward" in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word "straightforward" in the passage is closest in meaning to clear significant consistent effective What does the passage suggest about how observers interpret nervous laughter? 14. What does the passage suggest about how observers interpret nervous laughter? Observers may incorrectly interpret nervous laughter as a sign of amusement. Observers usually interpret nervous laughter correctly in relaxed social environments. Observers tend to interpret nervous laughter consistently across cultures. Observers interpret nervous laughter primarily based on sound rather than on contextual cues. Which of the following best summarizes the view of laughter presented in the passage? 15. Which of the following best summarizes the view of laughter presented in the passage? Laughter is primarily a biological response that improves mood through chemical processes in the brain. Laughter is a behavior whose meaning depends both on social context and on biological mechanisms. Laughter spreads mainly because it is contagious and strengthens group identity in social settings. Laughter reflects emotional intelligence by allowing individuals to express joy and relieve stress. Laughter, a universal human behavior, plays a vital role in social harmony. Biologically, laughter triggers the release of endorphins, enhancing mood and resilience. This neurochemical response suggests an evolutionary advantage: laughter may have developed as a stress-coping mechanism, helping individuals endure hardship through shared emotional relief. This challenges the simplistic view of laughter as merely a reaction to humor. Instead, it reveals its deeper connection to emotional intelligence, mental health, and interpersonal communication. Our laughter is often triggered when we hear others laugh, and this contagiousness makes it pivotal in group dynamics. For example, in social gatherings, laughter often spreads rapidly, reinforcing a sense of collective identity and emotional synchrony. However, this contagiousness is not always straightforward. In some contexts, such as forced laughter in professional settings or laughter used to mask discomfort, its role becomes ambiguous and subject to interpretation. While laughter can signal safety and shared understanding, it is complex and context-dependent. In relaxed environments, laughter often indicates trust and mutual comprehension. Yet, in tense situations, people may exhibit nervous laughter—such as during public speaking or conflict—which can mislead observers about their true emotional state. Research shows that identical laughter sounds can convey different meanings depending on social cues, tone, and setting, underscoring the importance of context in interpreting laughter.'),
    ('reading-rap-5f595954c9f377e35077c9a0', 'What does the author indicate about “standardized forms and rating scales”? 31. What does the author indicate about “standardized forms and rating scales”? They are a common feature in annual or semiannual employee evaluations. They help employers identify their organizational goals. They limit employers’ ability to align employees’ personal goals with company priorities. They provide a way of measuring the effects of workplace bias. The passage mentions each of the following drawbacks of traditional performance reviews EXCEPT 32. The passage mentions each of the following drawbacks of traditional performance reviews EXCEPT creating anxiety and impression management flattening job complexity into simple scores encouraging unhealthy competition among coworkers allowing some events to affect ratings too much The word “elicit” in the passage is closest in meaning to 33. The word “elicit” in the passage is closest in meaning to avoid reward mimic stimulate Which advantage of the new approaches to performance evaluations is mentioned in the final paragraph? 34. Which advantage of the new approaches to performance evaluations is mentioned in the final paragraph? They are suitable for use in many types of companies. They can be easily adjusted as business goals change. They take many different viewpoints into account. They will work even with relatively simple data tools. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. This means that small missteps can compound while helpful adjustments arrive too late. Modern businesses often struggle to assess individual contributions in ways employees experience as fair, accurate, and motivating. The standard model is the annual or semiannual review, where supervisors summarize achievements, identify development needs, and connect performance to rewards. Ideally, this process aligns employees’ personal goals with organizational priorities and creates a structured setting for feedback. Many companies use standardized forms and rating scales to improve consistency and limit obvious bias. In practice, however, this approach often fails to reflect the nature of contemporary workplaces. Numeric scores can flatten the complexity of modern jobs: managers must compress complex, collaborative work into simple ratings, while employees fear that one brief conversation may unduly influence pay, promotion, and professional identity. Ratings can be shaped by recent events, personal preferences, or cultural stereotypes rather than by sustained contributions. Long gaps between reviews may postpone useful course corrections. For these reasons, employees may spend more energy managing impressions than building skills. Meant to provide clarity, traditional performance appraisals often create anxiety and elicit careful self-presentation. To address these weaknesses, many organizations are adopting more continuous and multidimensional approaches. Regular check-ins, peer feedback, and project-based evaluations can spread judgment across time and perspectives. Combined with transparent goals and data tools, these iterative methods may better balance accountability, fairness, and learning.'),
    ('reading-rap-630c0bcef9fdc3314a2fab14', 'It can be inferred from the passage that space telescopes are used for gamma-ray astronomy primarily because 11. It can be inferred from the passage that space telescopes are used for gamma-ray astronomy primarily because gamma-ray sources produce signals that are too faint to detect with standard instruments gamma rays have physical characteristics that prevent ground-based observation gamma-ray events occur unpredictably and require constant monitoring gamma rays come from a region of the universe that is hard to observe from Earth The phrase “confined to” in the passage is closest in meaning to 12. The phrase “confined to” in the passage is closest in meaning to limited to returned to trapped by created by The Compton Gamma Ray Observatory enabled scientists to 13. The Compton Gamma Ray Observatory enabled scientists to create a more accurate map of the visible sky prove that gamma rays come from neutron stars find out where GRBs occur and learn more about them discover regular patterns in the occurrence of GRBs Based on the passage, which issue about GRBs remains unresolved? 14. Based on the passage, which issue about GRBs remains unresolved? How large GRBs are compared to other cosmic explosions How frequent GRBs are in different regions of the universe The specific processes that give rise to GRBs The total amount of energy released during a typical GRB Why does the author mention the Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope? 15. Why does the author mention the Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope? To emphasize the importance of international cooperation in gamma-ray research To explain how gamma-ray bursts were first detected To compare modern telescope technology with earlier optical instruments To suggest that new observational tools can help resolve unanswered questions Gamma-ray astronomy has transformed our view of the cosmos by enabling scientists to detect some of the universe''s most energetic and extreme phenomena, including gamma-ray bursts (GRBs). GRBs have drawn intense interest due to their mysterious origins and immense energy output. Unlike visible light, gamma rays do not penetrate Earth''s atmosphere. As a result, astronomers deploy space telescopes to capture signals from cosmic events like supernovae and neutron star collisions. The launch of the Compton Gamma Ray Observatory in 1991 provided the first comprehensive map of the gamma-ray sky, similar to the way optical telescopes map the visible sky. This breakthrough allowed scientists to pinpoint the locations of GRBs and study their properties in detail. Scientists learned that these bursts occur randomly across the sky and are not confined to our galaxy alone. Despite the advances, many questions remain. Some hypothesize that GRBs are the result of merging neutron stars, while others suggest they stem from hypernovae, explosive events that dwarf typical supernovae. Each explanation carries implications for our understanding of stellar evolution and cosmic history. New telescopes like the Fermi Gamma-ray Space Telescope may help astronomers uncover more secrets of these cosmic beacons.'),
    ('reading-rap-634cdcba16cac603d9436627', 'All of the following are mentioned as part of the formation process of opals EXCEPT: 26. All of the following are mentioned as part of the formation process of opals EXCEPT: Water flows into cracks in rocks. The water dries up, leaving silica deposits behind. Small spheres of silica create the structure of the gemstones. The structure formed is rigid and non-varying. Why does the author mention "a prism"? 27. Why does the author mention "a prism"? To illustrate how an opal affects light To explain how the internal structure of opals is created To give an example of how scientists study opals To argue that opals do not truly diffract light What is one consequence of the internal structure of opals? 28. What is one consequence of the internal structure of opals? 90 percent of them are red or orange. Relatively little ancient opal jewelry has survived. They are easily damaged by water. Jewelers must use special care to prevent opals from cracking. What does the passage suggest about ancient cultures'' view of opals? 29. What does the passage suggest about ancient cultures'' view of opals? They understood how opals were formed. They found opals difficult to work with. They believed opals had special qualities that benefited their owners. They considered opals to be very durable. The word "prophecy" in the passage is closest in meaning to 30. The word "prophecy" in the passage is closest in meaning to long life the ability to predict the future happiness high status Opal is a unique gemstone, known for its vibrant colors that shift as the stone moves. Unlike most gemstones, which form rigid, repeating crystalline structures, opals are amorphous, meaning they lack a fixed internal shape. This is due to the formation process, where silica-rich water seeps into cracks in rocks deep underground, leaving silica deposits when it evaporates. Tiny silica spheres create the internal structure of opals, and these spheres diffract light, separating it into colors, much like a prism does. The color and brilliance of opals depends on the size and spacing of the silica spheres. Larger spheres produce reds and oranges, while smaller spheres result in blues and greens. Because of Australia''s unique geological conditions, it is home to over 90 percent of the world''s opals. Opals are also fragile. Their high water content and noncrystalline structure make them prone to cracking when exposed to sudden temperature changes. Modern jewelers use techniques such as laser cutting and humidity-controlled storage to better protect opals. Ancient cultures believed opals had mystical properties and brought good luck. The Romans saw them as a symbol of hope and purity, while the Greeks thought they gave the gift of prophecy.'),
    ('reading-rap-6522c75a66bfa0f293e67432', 'The word “interplay” in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word “interplay” in the passage is closest in meaning to interaction interpretation interval intervention It can be inferred that music is similar to language in that music 12. It can be inferred that music is similar to language in that music matches the precision of written language engages only those areas of the brain that language engages has rules that are very similar to the rules of spoken language is a medium of expression that conveys complex emotions Why does the author mention ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle? 13. Why does the author mention ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle? To point out that music was once thought to have a significant moral influence To support the point that music is a powerful tool for emotional regulation To suggest that ancient philosophers had a more nuanced understanding of music than modern researchers do To identify the person who founded the academic field known as music theory Why does the author mention “patients before surgery”? 14. Why does the author mention “patients before surgery”? To demonstrate that the ancient Greek understanding of music was incorrect To argue that emotional states affect the enjoyment of music To provide an example of how music regulates emotions To suggest that slow-tempo classical music can lead to anxiety According to the passage, the existence of an innate human aptitude for musical expression suggests that 15. According to the passage, the existence of an innate human aptitude for musical expression suggests that music should only be seen as a form of art music may have played an important role in human development music that expresses joy is more universally appreciated than music that expresses sorrow music is not understood in the same way across different cultures Music is often hailed as a universal language. Philosophers and neuroscientists alike have explored music’s power to convey emotions without words. While lacking the precision of spoken language, music uniquely evokes complex emotions, a phenomenon attributed to its simultaneous engagement of various brain regions. This interplay not only stirs emotions but also triggers memories and bodily responses, making music a profound medium of expression. Historically, figures like the ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle argued for music’s ethical influence, positing that different musical modes could either promote virtue or incite vice. Modern research, however, leans toward music’s role in emotional regulation rather than moral alteration. Researchers have investigated how music interacts with human psychology beyond mere enjoyment. For instance, studies have shown that listening to slow-tempo classical music can reduce anxiety levels in patients before surgery, demonstrating music’s therapeutic potential. Surprisingly, the ability to perceive emotions in music transcends cultural contexts. Studies reveal that individuals worldwide can discern emotions like joy or sorrow in music, suggesting an innate human aptitude for musical expression. This universality has sparked inquiries into music’s evolutionary role, raising questions about whether music is merely an art form or a crucial aspect of human development.'),
    ('reading-rap-65673b292aab5d2297ddb13f', 'The word “anomalies” in the passage is closest in meaning to 31. The word “anomalies” in the passage is closest in meaning to irregularities indications solutions links What does the passage suggest about traditional data charts? 32. What does the passage suggest about traditional data charts? They reveal some important features hidden in raw data. They are limited in how effectively they convey complex information. They are especially helpful in capturing data related to climate change. They include heat maps, time-lapse animations, and network graphs. Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that highlights a limitation of interactive dashboards. Select the sentence to make your choice. 33. Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that highlights a limitation of interactive dashboards. Select the sentence to make your choice. The passage mentions all the following about interactive dashboards EXCEPT: 34. The passage mentions all the following about interactive dashboards EXCEPT: They represent an important development in data analysis tools. They let users change variables to see different outcomes. They can be used to explore how markets might behave. They help business leaders interact more effectively with one another. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Incomplete or biased data can lead to misleading conclusions, potentially hampering response efforts. Data visualization is transforming how we interpret complex datasets, moving beyond traditional charts, such as static charts and graphs, to dynamic graphical representations, such as heat maps, time-lapse animations, and network graphs. This shift allows for the discovery of patterns and anomalies otherwise hidden in raw data. For example, when studying climate change impacts, a time-lapse heat map might reveal unexpected temperature shifts over decades that spreadsheets alone could not capture. A significant development in this field is the use of interactive dashboards. These tools not only display data but also allow users to manipulate variables to explore different scenarios. In the business sector, decision-makers use dashboards to simulate market conditions, adjusting factors like demand and supply to forecast outcomes. However, the effectiveness of these dashboards often hinges on the user’s ability to interpret complex visual cues, which can be overwhelming. In the realm of public health, data visualization has proven invaluable in tracking disease spread. The combination of geographical maps and timelines helps analysts predict outbreak hot spots. Nonetheless, this predictive power depends heavily on the quality of data inputs. Still, data visualization tools help public health officials to act before a situation becomes unmanageable.'),
    ('reading-rap-69611be5fee90ca9b207c69e', 'Why does the author discuss earthquakes in paragraph 1? 31. Why does the author discuss earthquakes in paragraph 1? To demonstrate the fascination of geophysicists with Earth’s core To explain one way scientists gain insights into Earth’s core To highlight the destructive effects of seismic waves on Earth’s core To challenge the idea that Earth’s core is a mysterious world Why might seismic waves slow down when passing through Earth’s core? 32. Why might seismic waves slow down when passing through Earth’s core? Because of the solid nature of the core Because of the presence of iron and nickel Because of the possible presence of molten layers or unusual elements Because of the strength of the elements that make up the core The word “Collectively” in the passage is closest in meaning to 33. The word “Collectively” in the passage is closest in meaning to generally together somehow interestingly Advanced computer models suggest all of the following about Earth’s core EXCEPT: 34. Advanced computer models suggest all of the following about Earth’s core EXCEPT: Earth’s core has different layers. The inner part of Earth’s core rotates a little faster than other parts of Earth do. Wave behavior in Earth’s core shows directional differences. The super-rotation of Earth’s core is caused by anisotropies. Identify the sentence in paragraph 3 that describes a specific criticism of computer models of the inner core. Select the sentence to make your choice. 35. Identify the sentence in paragraph 3 that describes a specific criticism of computer models of the inner core. Select the sentence to make your choice. Geophysicists have long been intrigued by Earth’s core (the center of Earth). When earthquakes occur, they send seismic waves through the planet, offering rare glimpses into this mysterious world. Interestingly, these waves sometimes slow down in unexpected ways, suggesting that the core’s makeup may be more complex than just iron and nickel as previously believed. Some scientists suggest it might not be entirely solid, possibly containing molten layers or unusual elements that create a unique state of matter. Advanced computer models now depict the core not as a homogeneous sphere, but as a layered structure with distinct regions. These models also propose phenomena like super-rotation—where the inner core (the inner part of Earth’s core) spins slightly faster than the rest of the planet—and directional differences in wave behavior, known as anisotropies. Collectively, these findings portray a core in constant motion. But is this picture complete? Some researchers argue that these models rely on overly simplified assumptions. Truly understanding the inner core demands cutting-edge technology and fresh perspectives. The limitations of current theories remind us that the deeper we explore planetary science, the more we challenge our understanding of it.'),
    ('reading-rap-6995cc283fde7199f0e20580', 'The passage implies that bird migration began when 31. The passage implies that bird migration began when wintering grounds were closer to breeding grounds than they are now areas suitable for breeding were smaller than they are now there were many more birds than there are now climates were generally much colder than they are now What is the passage explaining when it mentions that "increased sunlight results in the increased availability of algae"? 32. What is the passage explaining when it mentions that "increased sunlight results in the increased availability of algae"? Why terns'' migrations result in increased food availability for them Why some tiny marine organisms migrate for long distances Why terns depend on sunlight while traveling long distances Why terns benefit from the migration of zooplankton The passage supports all of the following statements about Arctic terns EXCEPT: 33. The passage supports all of the following statements about Arctic terns EXCEPT: They take different migration routes depending on resource availability. They migrate over longer distances than all other birds do. They spend much of their lives in regions around Earth''s poles. They eat mostly small animals living in seawater. Why does the passage provide information about rock ptarmigans? 34. Why does the passage provide information about rock ptarmigans? To emphasize the usefulness of snow burrows in their habitat To contrast their behavior to that of Arctic terns To show that birch and willow trees provide food to both migratory and sedentary birds To provide another example of migratory birds The word "shelter" in the passage is closest in meaning to 35. The word "shelter" in the passage is closest in meaning to move land seek food take protection Every year, millions of birds travel huge distances to wintering grounds, and then back to breeding grounds when warmer weather returns there. Bird migration evolved in response to climatic changes. During the Ice Age, when average temperatures were frigid, migrating birds had a survival advantage, and the behavior became widespread. Migration is also linked to resource availability. Arctic terns, which undertake the longest migrations of any animal by flying from their summer breeding grounds in Earth''s north polar (Arctic) regions to their wintering grounds in Earth''s south polar regions, feed primarily on small fish and other small marine animals. This prey is most abundant when increased sunlight results in the increased availability of algae, the microscopic marine plantlike organisms that are food for the tiny marine animals known as zooplankton, which are in turn consumed by terns'' prey. Not all birds migrate. Rock ptarmigans also live in Arctic and sub-Arctic regions. Instead of flying to warmer climes in winter, they shelter in snow burrows and reduce activity to conserve energy. They feed on plants like birch and willow trees, which are available in their habitat year-round. The divergence between migratory and sedentary species represents different adaptations to environmental pressures.'),
    ('reading-rap-6c12040ffcf0dbfdb0313722', 'According to the passage, what advantage do closed-loop feedback systems provide? 26. According to the passage, what advantage do closed-loop feedback systems provide? They can be easily adjusted using remote controls. They continuously monitor their own performance. They are less complicated than other feedback systems. They assist engineers in creating more stable designs. What happens when a home heating system is not precisely calibrated as described in paragraph 2? 27. What happens when a home heating system is not precisely calibrated as described in paragraph 2? The temperature in the house goes up and down rather than remaining at a constant level. The temperature in the house remains well below the set point. The thermostat is unable to correctly determine the current temperature in the house. The home heating system is likely to break down under the stress of frequent oscillations. Why does the author discuss drones in paragraph 3? 28. Why does the author discuss drones in paragraph 3? To argue that drones are so unstable that they pose a safety risk to the public To suggest that controlling drones is easier than controlling robots To provide an example of chaotic behavior in a feedback system To explain how navigational sensors function during flight The word “mitigating” in the passage is closest in meaning to 29. The word “mitigating” in the passage is closest in meaning to preventing monitoring decreasing analyzing There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 30. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. A sudden gust of wind, for instance, might trigger an exaggerated correction, making the drone veer sharply or wobble midair instead of stabilizing smoothly. Control engineering is a branch of engineering focused on designing systems that regulate themselves to achieve desired behaviors, often through feedback mechanisms. These systems monitor their output and adjust their input accordingly, forming closed-loop mechanisms that maintain stability and performance. While this self-regulating nature is a core strength, feedback loops can also introduce unexpected complications. Consider how feedback controls temperature in a home heating system: A thermostat compares the current temperature to a set point and modifies the heating output based on the difference. However, time delays in this loop can cause overcorrections, resulting in temperature fluctuations. If the feedback response is not precisely calibrated, the system may oscillate continuously, failing to stabilize. In more complex systems, such as automated flight control or robotic motion, feedback mechanisms can behave unpredictably under certain conditions. For example, small timing mismatches or overly sensitive sensors may cause a drone to overreact to minor disturbances, leading to erratic flight patterns. This phenomenon, known as chaotic behavior, reveals that feedback is not inherently stabilizing. Instead, it demands careful tuning and predictive modeling. As control engineering advances, understanding and mitigating this unpredictability will be essential for ensuring reliability and precision, especially in high-stakes fields like aerospace and robotics.'),
    ('reading-rap-72e3e43b7ff17b9f1d25bd24', 'What surprising phenomenon is discussed in paragraph 1? 11. What surprising phenomenon is discussed in paragraph 1? The amount of chlorophyll produced by a certain type of phytoplankton The lack of nutrients in certain ocean regions The large number of microscopic organisms in environments that would not seem to support this The ability of satellites to measure chlorophyll concentrations in areas traditionally difficult to access What is one problem with the theory that upwelling is the cause of large phytoplankton populations in nutrient-poor ocean regions? 12. What is one problem with the theory that upwelling is the cause of large phytoplankton populations in nutrient-poor ocean regions? Upwelling can only account for a small rise in chlorophyll levels. Upwelling occurs only periodically, whereas raised chlorophyll levels are observed over long periods. No satellite data support the idea that currents shift at the same time that chlorophyll levels rise. The amount of nutrients brought up by upwelling is not large enough to support an increase in phytoplankton populations. The word "chronic" in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word "chronic" in the passage is closest in meaning to continuing sudden basic specific All of the following are true of phytoplankton EXCEPT: 14. All of the following are true of phytoplankton EXCEPT: They are at the base of ocean food webs. They release chlorophyll into the ocean. Some may rely on other microorganisms to help them survive. Their abundance is a frequent cause of harm to ocean environments. Which of the following best describes the structure of the passage? 15. Which of the following best describes the structure of the passage? The description of a puzzling finding, followed by some possible explanations and a conclusion questioning the finding The introduction of a type of organism, followed by examples of its adaptations and the description of a method scientists use to collect data about it The discussion of recent marine observations, followed by competing interpretations of the data and a conclusion resolving the dispute The analysis of one type of marine environment and its organisms, followed by some specific examples of conditions in that environment Nutrient-poor regions of the ocean have long been thought to support only sparse populations of phytoplankton, the microscopic organisms that form the foundation of marine food webs. Recent satellite observations, however, have revealed unexpectedly high chlorophyll concentrations—an indicator of phytoplankton abundance—in waters traditionally considered low in nutrients. Scientists are reconsidering how biological productivity can be sustained in such environments. One possible explanation involves a process known as upwelling, in which shifting currents can bring up nutrient-rich water from the deep to the surface, fueling short-lived phytoplankton blooms before conditions return to scarcity. Yet satellite data suggest that elevated chlorophyll levels persist over time rather than appearing in seasonal pulses, raising doubts about this explanation. Another line of inquiry focuses on phytoplankton themselves. Some species may be adapted to survive under chronic nutrient limitations, potentially through close associations with other microorganisms that help make otherwise inaccessible nutrients available. Still, adaptation may not fully explain the broad geographic extent of these apparent increases in phytoplankton. Finally, researchers have questioned whether the satellite data are accurate. Suspended particles unrelated to phytoplankton could distort chlorophyll measurements, complicating interpretations. If so, widely used datasets tracking ocean productivity may require reassessment, leaving the phenomenon unresolved.'),
    ('reading-rap-7447d5926c9979015af28e99', 'Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that illustrates how social network dynamics can affect personal beliefs. Select the sentence to make your choice. 31. Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that illustrates how social network dynamics can affect personal beliefs. Select the sentence to make your choice. According to the passage, computational sociologists study 32. According to the passage, computational sociologists study how having shared opinions strengthens a friendship why it is important to develop complex diagrams of network connections why emotionally charged conversations are ineffective at changing opinions how human behavior is affected by frequent interactions The word “incredibly” in the passage is closest in meaning to 33. The word “incredibly” in the passage is closest in meaning to generally extremely somewhat often According to the passage, what makes it difficult to model human behavior? 34. According to the passage, what makes it difficult to model human behavior? The interaction of various factors that play a role in human behavior The rapid spread of messages from one population to another The frequent movement of humans among countries with different cultural values The lack of cooperation among researchers from different cultural backgrounds There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. In fact, influence often emerges from the bottom up, rather than being imposed from the top down. Computational sociology studies how social networks—like friendships, professional connections, or online communities—affect how people think and act. Instead of just drawing diagrams of who is connected to whom, researchers now look more closely at how frequent interactions shape behavior. If someone regularly chats with a group of friends who share strong opinions about climate change, they may gradually adopt similar views. These influences are especially strong when the conversations are emotionally charged or align with the person’s values. Interestingly, it is not always the most famous or powerful people who have the biggest impact. Everyday conversations—like repeated exchanges between coworkers—can be more influential than a single message from a celebrity or expert. Researchers use computational models to analyze how messages spread. An accurate model can predict which types of messages are likely to spread widely. Still, predicting human behavior is incredibly complex. People are shaped by their culture, social norms, and personal experiences, which makes it hard to build models that work universally. For example, a message that spreads quickly in one country might fall flat in another due to different cultural values. The real challenge lies in understanding how all these subtle factors interact to drive large-scale changes in behavior.'),
    ('reading-rap-75ae096d9c6d8c0ca9c04298', 'Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage? 11. Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage? Urartu innovated rapidly in spite of military pressure from its neighbors. Local magnetite deposits were critical to Urartian iron innovation. Innovative iron production by Urartu was likely driven by a range of factors. The discovery of small village forges significantly changed scholarly views about Urartu''s iron industry. Why does the author provide the information that iron artifacts discovered in Urartu show “unusually low phosphorus content”? 12. Why does the author provide the information that iron artifacts discovered in Urartu show “unusually low phosphorus content”? To suggest that the high quality of local ore meant that innovations in processing were not important To highlight the idea that the workshops excavated near Teishebaini used iron brought in from elsewhere To help explain why Urartian kings felt the need to fund new, more modern iron foundries To help establish that nearby mineral resources played a role in advancing Urartian metalworking Paragraph 1 suggests that which of the following is true of the Urartian-Assyrian relationship? 13. Paragraph 1 suggests that which of the following is true of the Urartian-Assyrian relationship? The Assyrians had developed iron foundries long before the Urartians produced iron. The Urartians and Assyrians were military rivals. Assyrian iron foundries relied on Urartian geological deposits. Urartian kings funded ironworking mainly to strengthen trade with Assyria. Why does the author provide the information that satellite surveys have identified “small village forges” in Urartu? 14. Why does the author provide the information that satellite surveys have identified “small village forges” in Urartu? To introduce the idea that the Urartian iron industry included rural production To support the claim that Urartian kings increased centralized control over raw materials To provide evidence that large fortresses show the full scale of Urartian industry To suggest that Urartian industry was transitioning from urban to rural production The word “premise” in the passage is closest in meaning to 15. The word “premise” in the passage is closest in meaning to problem assumption detail effect The kingdom of Urartu in southwest Asia became a center of iron innovation during the 9th–6th centuries B.C.E. One explanation for Urartu''s importance proposes that Urartians'' mastery in locating, extracting, and processing local deposits of magnetite—an iron-rich material—accelerated their shift from bronze to iron. This geological explanation is supported by archaeological evidence, including evidence of metalworking at excavated workshops near the fortified city of Teishebaini and analysis of iron artifacts showing unusually low phosphorus content, a sign of high-quality local ore. An alternate view focuses on external pressures: Inscriptions suggest that Urartian kings funded iron foundries in order to produce chariots faster than neighboring Assyria could. Critics argue that this second theory assumes centralized control over raw materials and ignores small village forges identified by satellite surveys. The geological and military explanations do share this important premise: that large fortresses show the full scale of Urartian industry. Questioning this idea has led scholars to consider rural production alongside royal support as joint drivers of early ironworking. In this light, Urartu appears to have been a society in transition from bronze to iron thanks in part to a complex network of miners, smiths, and planners whose cooperation sparked one of history''s key metallurgical revolutions.'),
    ('reading-rap-7945215d82567f1f6f2af63a', 'The word “grappling” in the passage is closest in meaning to 31. The word “grappling” in the passage is closest in meaning to identifying interfering struggling experimenting How do green walls help control noise pollution? 32. How do green walls help control noise pollution? By purifying the air around buildings By disrupting the path of sound waves By making building facades more attractive By reducing temperatures inside buildings What can be inferred about noise pollution in the European cities mentioned in the passage? 33. What can be inferred about noise pollution in the European cities mentioned in the passage? It was likely a factor in causing stress-related health issues among residents. It has not been as effectively managed there as it has been in other places. It has been controlled most successfully by adding green walls to buildings. It has been funded primarily through the efforts of urban planners. Identify the sentence in paragraph 4 that names specific architectural elements built with noise-canceling materials. 34. Identify the sentence in paragraph 4 that names specific architectural elements built with noise-canceling materials. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. One common source is traffic, which includes cars, buses, motorcycles, and trucks. Urban areas across the globe are grappling with the challenge of noise pollution. This type of pollution can severely impact health, leading to sleep disturbances and cardiovascular problems. It has many causes. Innovative solutions to control noise pollution, from traffic or other sources, are emerging. One such solution is green walls, vertical gardens installed on building facades. Green walls can absorb, reflect, or deflect sound waves, reducing the noise that enters a building. Additionally, they offer benefits like air purification and temperature regulation—thus not only improving the outdoor environment around buildings but also enhancing indoor air quality and helping regulate interior temperatures by acting as natural insulation. Another approach involves quiet zones. These zones are designated areas where noisy activities are restricted, often including parks, residential neighborhoods, and hospital areas. By reducing noise levels in these zones, cities create pockets of tranquility. Some European cities have seen a decrease in stress-related health issues among residents in quiet zones. Advancements in technology have also led to the creation of noise-canceling materials. Such materials are often used in buildings or road surfaces. They work by actively reducing sound vibrations. Urban planners can use these materials to design acoustic panels and noise barriers, strategically placing these structures to combat the noise problem.'),
    ('reading-rap-7d5d0462cf877efd8ae523af', 'The word "consistent" in the passage is closest in meaning to 31. The word "consistent" in the passage is closest in meaning to rapid steady large impressive All of the following are mentioned in the passage as possible contributors to the trend of IQ scores EXCEPT 32. All of the following are mentioned in the passage as possible contributors to the trend of IQ scores EXCEPT better eating habits and medical care improvements in parental care the cognitive demands of contemporary life the extensive use of technology According to some researchers, the Flynn Effect may be a sign 33. According to some researchers, the Flynn Effect may be a sign people are becoming better at taking tests! IQ tests have become easier over time test-takers thrive in stimulating environments school systems have developed better examinations Why does the author mention that the Flynn Effect may be slowing down or reversing in some countries? 34. Why does the author mention that the Flynn Effect may be slowing down or reversing in some countries? To suggest that there may not be a global increase in intelligence To argue that cognitive abilities are declining around the world To show that the Flynn Effect requires further research To argue that the challenges of modern life can have negative effects on intelligence There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Changes in educational practices or shifts in societal values could account for the downturn. Researchers have observed a phenomenon known as the Flynn Effect: a consistent increase in Intelligence Quotient (IQ) scores from one generation to the next. This trend, named after intelligence researcher James Flynn, shows that average IQ scores have risen by about three points per decade in many parts of the world. Various theories have been proposed to explain this trend. One hypothesis is that improvements in nutrition and healthcare have enhanced brain development, leading to higher IQ scores. Another theory suggests that the complexity of modern life, with its increased cognitive demands, has driven this rise. The widespread use of technology and a more stimulating environment could also be contributing factors. However, the Flynn Effect does not necessarily indicate that people are becoming more intelligent. Some researchers argue that the rise in IQ scores reflects better test-taking skills or familiarity with the types of tasks presented in IQ tests, rather than a genuine increase in cognitive abilities. Recent studies have shown that the Flynn Effect may be slowing down or even reversing in some countries, challenging the notion of a universal and perpetual increase in intelligence.'),
    ('reading-rap-88bc32dacb3916e75ed13c09', 'Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that explains the natural process by which climate data is preserved. Select the sentence to make your choice. 11. Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that explains the natural process by which climate data is preserved. Select the sentence to make your choice. The word “escalating” in the passage is closest in meaning to 12. The word “escalating” in the passage is closest in meaning to increasing continuing powerful damaging According to the passage, all of the following are true about ice cores EXCEPT: 13. According to the passage, all of the following are true about ice cores EXCEPT: Ice cores are obtained by drilling far down into glaciers. Each layer in an ice core formed at a different point in time. Ice core layers are sometimes destroyed by severe environmental events. Ice cores can be used to identify when volcanoes erupted in the past. Why does the author mention "tree rings"? 14. Why does the author mention "tree rings"? To compare the usefulness of trees and ice cores for studying past climates To highlight the extreme age of some layers of ice cores To illustrate the layering that exists in ice cores To contrast the physical markers present in tree rings with the chemical markers found in ice cores The passage implies that scientists should 15. The passage implies that scientists should look quickly for other sources of valuable climate data increase the size of the ice cores they drill find ways to limit the rise of sea levels work as quickly as possible to collect more ice cores Glaciers, repositories of ancient ice, serve as frozen libraries of Earth’s climatic history. As snow accumulates and compresses over centuries, it traps air bubbles that create tiny atmospheric snapshots. These bubbles are crucial for understanding climate evolution. By analyzing the gases within the bubbles, researchers are able to reconstruct past concentrations of greenhouse gases like carbon dioxide and methane, revealing both natural climate cycles and the escalating impact of human activity. Though many glaciers are retreating due to rising temperatures, surviving ones offer a window into the past. Scientists drill deep into the ice to extract cylindrical cores, whose layers, much like tree rings, correspond to distinct time periods. These layers contain more than just gases: They hold chemical and physical markers of past environmental events. Ice cores can reveal data about volcanic eruptions via ash and sulfate layers, solar activity via isotopic changes, and forest fires via trapped particulates and black carbon. Recent studies underscore the precarious future of these icy archives. As glaciers melt, they contribute to rising sea levels, and the risk that invaluable climate data will be lost increases. The urgency to study and preserve these records grows. While scientists race against time, the question remains: what secrets do these frozen giants still hold?'),
    ('reading-rap-8dec9fc2ce3dd407af5bdf6c', 'According to the passage, what is an unexpected impact of major film festivals? 11. According to the passage, what is an unexpected impact of major film festivals? Restaurants experience more growth than small stores do. Small towns frequently experience longer-lasting economic benefits than large cities do. Global hubs experience economic gains at the neighborhood level rather than in the downtown district. Retailers attract more visitors at the beginning of film festivals than in the closing days. What does the passage suggest about film festivals in smaller towns? 12. What does the passage suggest about film festivals in smaller towns? They lead to increased government spending on infrastructure and the arts. They add a considerable amount of wear and tear to community streets. They primarily draw one-time visitors like film directors and journalists. They do not have large enough audiences to satisfy promoters. Why does the author mention “measurement artifacts” and “small baselines”? 13. Why does the author mention “measurement artifacts” and “small baselines”? To indicate that measuring the economic benefits of festivals in communities requires new measurement tools To explain that the economic benefits of film festivals in smaller communities may appear larger than they actually are To argue that larger communities have better ways of measuring economic activity than smaller ones do To suggest that the rent surges discussed are not as large as they seem The word “wanes” is closest in meaning to 14. The word “wanes” is closest in meaning to continues disappears shifts decreases According to the third paragraph, all of the following are possible consequences associated with film festivals EXCEPT: 15. According to the third paragraph, all of the following are possible consequences associated with film festivals EXCEPT: Large chain stores sometimes benefit more from visitors than local businesses. Local businesses find it difficult to pay increased rents. An influx of tourists often involves damage to the local environment. Festivals may slip in quality or size, leaving communities with lower-than-expected earnings. Recent analyses of the impacts of major film festivals reveal a pattern that surprises experts: small communities like Locarno or Telluride often report more durable, neighborhood-level economic gains than global hubs. Independent restaurants and boutique retailers in these towns see elevated sales months later, while larger cities observe sharp increases that quickly dissipate. This is unusual because big markets have deeper hospitality capacity, stronger brand recognition, and vast visitor pipelines; according to orthodox thinking, larger cities should capitalize best on high-profile events. One explanation is that festivals in smaller communities function as concentrated place-branding campaigns. The temporary influx of filmmakers, journalists, and audiences increases a town''s visibility, converting one-time visitors into repeat tourists and remote promoters. Local governments, compelled by the spotlight, accelerate street improvements and cultural programming that persist after the festival, seeding arts projects and year-round film screenings that encourage local spending. Yet this account has weaknesses. Persistent gains may partly reflect measurement artifacts—small baselines make percentage increases look dramatic. Benefits can be uneven, with national chains capturing much of the surge while higher rents pose issues for long-standing businesses. Moreover, reliance on festival-driven identity risks event dependency: if sponsorship wanes or programming slips, the fragile ecosystem can unravel, leaving communities with sunk costs and muted returns.'),
    ('reading-rap-8ed5cf11ef1ff55f75008441', 'The word “perplexing” in the passage is closest in meaning to 31. The word “perplexing” in the passage is closest in meaning to rare puzzling disturbing predictable The passage suggests which of the following about scientists’ research into whale strandings? 32. The passage suggests which of the following about scientists’ research into whale strandings? It has produced explanations for strandings that point primarily to changing environmental conditions. It has revealed that causes for strandings vary based on whale species and habitats. It has proposed several explanations for strandings but has not found a conclusive understanding of the phenomenon. It has been able to provide a clearer picture of whale navigation by utilizing naval sonar. The passage suggests that whales’ ability to navigate 33. The passage suggests that whales’ ability to navigate has declined over the last few decades is enhanced when whales travel in large pods is partly dependent on their proximity to familiar coastal regions may be sensitive to fluctuations in the planet’s magnetic field The passage indicates all of the following about naval sonar EXCEPT: 34. The passage indicates all of the following about naval sonar EXCEPT: It can prevent whales from forming pods. It can produce reactions in whales that lead to physical harm. It has been identified as a factor that may contribute to strandings. It has been subject to governmental restrictions in certain locations. The author mentions “critical areas” in order to 35. The author mentions “critical areas” in order to identify reasons that some countries have resisted the regulation of naval sonar provide a specific example of how regulations aim to reduce threats to whales explain why whale strandings are particularly common in some regions suggest that certain marine habitats are more important than others Few wildlife phenomena are as perplexing as mass strandings of whales. Sometimes, hundreds of whales or dolphins beach themselves on shores, often with fatal results. Scientists have sought explanations for decades, but as of yet, little is understood. Several theories suggest possible reasons for the strandings. Some researchers believe that disruptions to Earth’s magnetic field may interfere with marine mammals’ navigational abilities, while others point to changes in oceanic environments, such as temperature fluctuations or pollution, as factors that might drive the animals off course. Humans may play a more direct role, too. In recent years, there has been growing evidence linking naval sonar systems to whale strandings. The powerful sound waves emitted by these systems can cause disorientation, making it difficult for whales to navigate underwater. Startled by the noise, whales may panic and rapidly ascend to the surface, causing serious injuries. Because whales travel in pods, disorientation in one whale can draw others toward shore, increasing the risk of a mass stranding. Efforts to prevent strandings have yielded mixed results. While some countries now regulate naval sonar near whale habitats, requiring ships to lower sound levels or avoid critical areas, strandings continue to occur, reminding us that the reasons behind such events are complex.'),
    ('reading-rap-93ff31f6180db0b4ab186bf7', 'Paragraph 1 mentions all of the following techniques for selling soft drinks EXCEPT 31. Paragraph 1 mentions all of the following techniques for selling soft drinks EXCEPT using eye-catching packaging designing easy-to-remember labels giving discounts placing products at easily noticeable locations The word “discrepancy” in the passage is closest in meaning to 32. The word “discrepancy” in the passage is closest in meaning to success promotion strategy difference Which of the sentences below best expresses the essential information in the highlighted sentence in the passage? Incorrect choices change the meaning in important ways or leave out essential information. 33. Which of the sentences below best expresses the essential information in the highlighted sentence in the passage? Incorrect choices change the meaning in important ways or leave out essential information. While both soft drinks and health-oriented beverages are associated with pleasure, they do not align with impulsive tendencies in the same way. When soft drinks are associated with both pleasure and function, they activate impulsive tendencies and elicit automatic, emotion-driven responses. While soft drinks are associated with pleasure, behavioral economics seeks to align them with impulse and to activate automatic, emotion-driven responses in customers. Soft drinks are seen as pleasurable and align with impulsive behavior, whereas health-oriented beverages are seen as functional and do not elicit the same response. Why does the author discuss sunscreen? 34. Why does the author discuss sunscreen? To provide an example of a product that is difficult to advertise To illustrate how health-oriented drinks might be successfully marketed To explain why the sales figures of health-oriented products are typically low To emphasize the importance of emotional storytelling in marketing What does the author imply about marketing health-oriented drinks? 35. What does the author imply about marketing health-oriented drinks? Producers should rely on the same impulse-driven packaging and placement tactics used for soft drinks. Producers are more likely to succeed by emphasizing clear, informational messages based on rational product criteria. Emotional storytelling should play the primary role in advertisements for health-oriented drinks. Health-oriented drinks should be marketed mainly by associating them more strongly with pleasure. You have probably seen advertisements for soft drinks (soda and other non-alcoholic beverages, typically sweetened) that feature the drink in brightly colored bottles. Producers rely on behavioral economics to influence customer choice, and for soft drinks, distinctive colors and memorable labels have been shown to generate customer interest. It is also no coincidence that, in supermarkets, soft drinks are often found at eye level in aisles and near checkout areas. These locations reliably boost spontaneous purchasing. However, the same approaches often underperform when used to promote healthier beverage options like low-sugar drinks and sports beverages. What is the reason behind this discrepancy? While soft drinks are associated with pleasure, making them naturally align with the impulsive tendencies that behavioral economics seeks to activate, health-oriented beverages are often viewed as functional or even obligatory—associations that do not elicit the same automatic, emotion-driven response. How, then, can producers boost sales figures of health-oriented drinks? Consider sunscreen. As a functional product designed to prevent harm, sunscreen tends to be evaluated by consumers using rational criteria such as duration of protection and safety. As a result, sunscreen advertising typically emphasizes clear, informational messaging. Emotional storytelling plays only a secondary role.'),
    ('reading-rap-94b83368bbbce780f1162836', 'The passage suggests that Kanzi’s ability to communicate through language was limited in that it did not 11. The passage suggests that Kanzi’s ability to communicate through language was limited in that it did not involve basic symbolic representation succeed in expressing immediate needs arise naturally without training by humans include an understanding of syntactic rules According to the passage, nonhuman primates can do each of the following EXCEPT 12. According to the passage, nonhuman primates can do each of the following EXCEPT convey meaning through symbols teach each other basic grammar live in groups with social structures respond to instruction by humans The phrase “tethered to” in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The phrase “tethered to” in the passage is closest in meaning to hindered by useful for secondary to focused on The author notes that “all primates, including humans, are social creatures” primarily to 14. The author notes that “all primates, including humans, are social creatures” primarily to suggest that nonhuman primates may eventually develop humanlike language indicate a seeming commonality when it comes to prerequisites for developing language argue that nonhuman primates are more intelligent than was previously believed support the claim that no known animal communication system rivals human language There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Yet social organization alone does not seem sufficient to explain the emergence of a fully generative language ability. Language has traditionally been considered a uniquely human trait; however, this assumption has been increasingly challenged. Research involving chimpanzees and bonobos reveals rudimentary linguistic abilities: symbolic communication, basic grammar, and even sentence formation. Kanzi, a bonobo, famously used lexigrams (symbols that stand for specific concepts) to convey desires and even to learn to comprehend grammar, suggesting a grasp of syntactic rules. Yet, such abilities in nonhuman primates emerge only through intensive training and do not naturally evolve in primate communities. This distinction underscores a critical contrast: while humans spontaneously develop intricate language systems, other primates’ communication remains tethered to immediate needs and lacks the generative structure necessary for producing novel sentences and ideas. Some theorists propose that individual cognitive capacities are what constrains language development; others argue that social complexity plays a pivotal role. Language, they contend, may have coevolved with the demands of maintaining social hierarchies and alliances. While all primates, including humans, are social creatures, the fact remains that no known communication system among nonhuman primates rivals human language. Comparative linguistic studies may blur the boundaries between species, but they ultimately reaffirm the singularity of human linguistic capacity.'),
    ('reading-rap-9597f97b9636121c0ef7ec58', 'What is the relationship between the first sentence and the second sentence of the passage? 11. What is the relationship between the first sentence and the second sentence of the passage? The first sentence makes a generalization, and the second sentence introduces exceptions to the generalization. The first sentence introduces a question, and the second sentence offers possible answers to the question. The first sentence makes a claim, and the second sentence provides examples to illustrate that claim. The first sentence presents one view of a subject, and the second sentence presents a contrasting view. The passage suggests which of the following about viewers of videos? 12. The passage suggests which of the following about viewers of videos? They may not see actions that are critical to understanding an incident because of the way the video is framed. They may not find a video recording of an incident as revealing as an eyewitness account of the event. They are more likely to trust the accuracy of a video recorded on a police officer’s body camera than one taken with a smartphone. They are likely to regard video recordings skeptically because they know that videos can be misleading. What does the author point out when discussing “context”? 13. What does the author point out when discussing “context”? The setting in which a video is viewed may affect the way viewers perceive the recorded events. People may draw incorrect conclusions about others’ motivations from what they observe in a video. Video recordings can be used either to defend an individual’s actions or to depict the individual as an aggressor. The reliability of video recordings of events should be compared to the reliability of oral accounts of those events. The passage indicates that “legal experts” hold which of the following views? 14. The passage indicates that “legal experts” hold which of the following views? Jurors are likely to accept videos as objective depictions of events whereas they view witness testimony with skepticism. Recent improvements in technology have made it so easy to display video evidence to jurors that other kinds of evidence may be neglected. Video recordings should not be used as evidence in courtrooms unless multiple recordings taken from different vantage points can be shown. Courts should not undermine jurors’ confidence in their abilities by overemphasizing the possibility of misinterpreting videos. The word “cautiously” in the passage is closest in meaning to 15. The word “cautiously” in the passage is closest in meaning to reluctantly carefully decisively confidently In the United States, as in some other countries, video evidence has become integral to the criminal justice system as technological advances have made cameras nearly ubiquitous. Police officers often wear body cameras, security cameras monitor public areas, and nearly everyone carries a smartphone capable of recording high-quality videos. While such recordings are widely seen as reliable evidentiary tools, they have certain limitations. For example, videos are usually taken from a single vantage point, potentially giving viewers a misleading impression of events. A single camera angle might fail to capture what occurs just outside the frame, leading observers to misinterpret what they see. Additionally, video footage has no way of conveying interpersonal and emotional context: A person’s actions might appear antagonistic even if the person was acting in self-defense. Despite these limitations, legal experts emphasize that video evidence plays a significant role in courtrooms. Jurors (people chosen to consider evidence and decide a case) might dismiss eyewitness testimonies as unreliable or perceive oral accounts as inconsistent, while videos show events more directly. When multiple recordings are available from different angles, an even more reliable sense of an incident can emerge. Nevertheless, courts often remind jurors to evaluate video evidence cautiously.'),
    ('reading-rap-963d829d45c84c1e9e00c7d0', 'The color of quantum dots can be controlled by changing 26. The color of quantum dots can be controlled by changing their size after exposure to light the direction in which they are displayed the amount of light they are exposed to the color of the light they are exposed to What makes quantum dot technology in televisions efficient? 27. What makes quantum dot technology in televisions efficient? The vibrancy of the colors The reduced need for backlighting The larger color spectrum The size of the particles What is identified as an environmental benefit of quantum dots? 28. What is identified as an environmental benefit of quantum dots? The ability to make cadmium more pure The ability to remove lead from living organisms Limiting environmental impacts to small areas Less use of some poisonous substances Why does the passage state that the “manufacture of quantum dots requires extreme precision”? 29. Why does the passage state that the “manufacture of quantum dots requires extreme precision”? To imply that quantum dots'' environmental impact might increase To praise the hard work of quantum dot manufacturers To challenge the claim that using quantum dots is worthwhile To explain why the production of quantum dots is difficult to increase How can biologists use quantum dots? 30. How can biologists use quantum dots? For studying very small living organisms For observing the movement of molecules For including electronic tools in research For making some biological processes more dynamic Quantum dots are tiny semiconductor particles with unique optical properties, making them valuable in display technology. When exposed to light, these nanoparticles emit bright, pure colors that can be finely tuned by varying their size. This allows for more vibrant and accurate colors in screens, surpassing traditional display technologies. Their application in displays has revolutionized the consumer electronics industry. For example, quantum dot-enhanced televisions offer a broader color spectrum and energy conservation compared to conventional light-emitting diode (LED) displays because they require less backlighting. Environmental benefits are another advantage. Traditional displays often use harmful heavy metals like cadmium and lead. Quantum dots can be made from less toxic materials, and researchers are working on cadmium-free quantum dots to further reduce environmental impact. The manufacture of quantum dots requires extreme precision. As a result, challenges remain in scaling up production. However, this is a worthwhile endeavor because quantum dots potentially have many applications outside of electronics, such as in biological research. Their small size and bright emission are ideal for tagging and tracking molecules in living organisms. This allows for long-term observation of dynamic biological processes.'),
    ('reading-rap-964b2c0d86db237d8e65b24e', 'The word "profound" in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word "profound" in the passage is closest in meaning to gradual permanent complex fundamental Why does the author discuss Martha Graham''s approach to dance? 12. Why does the author discuss Martha Graham''s approach to dance? To illustrate an important shift from formalism to expressive movement To highlight the continuity between ceremonial dance and modern techniques To suggest that Graham''s style was more technically demanding than ballet To argue that ballet remains the dominant form of choreography What does the author suggest about the role of the choreographer in contemporary dance? 13. What does the author suggest about the role of the choreographer in contemporary dance? The choreographer''s role is increasingly shared with other contributors. The choreographer is solely responsible for all artistic decisions. The choreographer''s relation to collaborators has become more hierarchical. The choreographer no longer participates in the creative process. Which of the following best describes the evolution of choreography over time? 14. Which of the following best describes the evolution of choreography over time? A shift from narrative to social commentary A movement toward stricter formalism A transformation from rigid constraints to flexibility A return to ceremonial traditions There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. In many cases, contributors shape the thematic and emotional direction of a piece, influencing its development from within the creative process. Choreography (the composition and arrangement of dance), once a ceremonial and courtly discipline rooted in ritualistic precision, has undergone a profound metamorphosis shaped by cultural shifts and artistic redefinition. Initially governed by strict formalism—particularly in classical ballet—it emphasized symmetry, control, and hierarchy. However, the 20th century witnessed a radical departure from these conventions. Visionaries like Martha Graham redefined dance as a medium for emotional expression and psychological depth, challenging ballet''s aesthetic rigidity and introducing vocabularies of movement that embraced tension, release, and groundedness, which emphasizes a strong connection to the earth or floor. This evolution marked a transition from spectacle to narrative, with choreography becoming a conduit for storytelling and social commentary. Today, choreography increasingly reflects a decentralization of artistic authority. Rather than a single choreographer dictating movement, many contemporary works emerge from collaborative processes among dancers, composers, designers, and digital artists. This co-creative model not only democratizes authorship but also fosters performances that are adaptive and responsive—sometimes even improvised or altered in real time. The boundaries between creator and performer blur, yielding works that resist fixed interpretation. In this context, choreography becomes less a finished product and more a living dialogue among disciplines, identities, and ideas.'),
    ('reading-rap-96e6fb86c216f62eead5bb01', 'The word “invaluable” in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word “invaluable” in the passage is closest in meaning to convenient recommended extremely useful very expensive All of the following are mentioned as applications of polarized light EXCEPT: 12. All of the following are mentioned as applications of polarized light EXCEPT: It can reveal hidden properties in crystals. It is used in sunglasses to reduce glare. It can be used to alter cell structure. It aids in analyzing cellular composition. What can be inferred about the use of polarized light in medicine? 13. What can be inferred about the use of polarized light in medicine? It can help doctors diagnose diseases. It is still in its early stages of implementation. It offers new ways to treat Alzheimer’s disease. Its use is somewhat controversial. Polarized lenses are potentially dangerous in aviation because they can 14. Polarized lenses are potentially dangerous in aviation because they can give pilots a false sense of confidence increase glare in the cockpit become foggy at high altitudes make it difficult to see aircraft instruments There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Applications are not limited to the inorganic world. Polarized light, characterized by waves vibrating in a single plane, offers applications beyond its familiar use in sunglasses, where it reduces glare from reflective surfaces like water and roads. In certain scientific fields, it is invaluable for revealing hidden properties of materials. For instance, when researchers study the internal structure of crystals, polarized light provides insights that normal light cannot, by interacting differently with the distinct optical paths within the crystal lattice. In biology, polarized light uncovers structural details of cells and tissues, facilitating breakthroughs in understanding cellular organization and disease mechanisms. This is true for birefringent materials, which alter the polarization of light in ways that reveal microstructural patterns such as fiber alignment and intracellular organization, helping scientists assess variations in cell density and composition. Such techniques have been instrumental in identifying amyloid plaques in brain tissue, an indication of Alzheimer’s disease. However, the use of polarized light is not universally beneficial. In aviation, pilots may avoid polarized lenses because they can obscure critical visual cues, such as instrument readings, that are essential for navigation. Thus, while polarized light can solve certain visibility issues by reducing glare, it may also interfere with necessary visual information, prompting careful consideration of context before implementation.'),
    ('reading-rap-98e3b20d6b6e1d9ce46eac7c', 'Why does the author mention the "indiscriminate use of antibiotics"? 11. Why does the author mention the "indiscriminate use of antibiotics"? To suggest that the AMR crisis can be easily solved To argue that the World Health Organization has underestimated the extent of the problem To make a distinction between how antibiotics are used in healthcare and agriculture To explain why AMR is becoming a global health challenge Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that explains how antimicrobial resistance works. Select the sentence to make your choice. 12. Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that explains how antimicrobial resistance works. Select the sentence to make your choice. Which obstacle to the development of new antibiotics is mentioned in the passage? 13. Which obstacle to the development of new antibiotics is mentioned in the passage? Insurance companies do not support the necessary investment. Antibiotics are typically less profitable than some other drugs. The pace of bacterial gene mutation is too rapid to keep up with. Medications for chronic diseases are less complex to produce. The word "comprehensive" in the passage is closest in meaning to 14. The word "comprehensive" in the passage is closest in meaning to understandable well-researched thorough cost-effective The passage mentions each of the following strategies for addressing antimicrobial resistance EXCEPT 15. The passage mentions each of the following strategies for addressing antimicrobial resistance EXCEPT increasing investment in measures for preventing infections strengthening regulations that govern antibiotic prescriptions offering financial incentives to drugmakers to develop new antibiotics educating people worldwide about limiting the use of antibiotics Antimicrobial resistance (AMR)—the ability of microorganisms to withstand drugs that once killed them—threatens to reverse decades of medical advancement, posing an urgent public health challenge globally. In particular, the widespread and often indiscriminate use of antibiotics in medicine and agriculture has accelerated the emergence of resistant bacteria. Recognizing the gravity of AMR, the World Health Organization classifies it among the top global health threats, underscoring the critical need for immediate action. Bacteria develop resistance through a multitude of mechanisms, including genetic mutations and the sharing of genes, which allow them to survive antibiotic treatment and proliferate. Unfortunately, the pharmaceutical industry has been slow to produce new antibiotics, hampered by both scientific obstacles and economic disincentives. Antibiotics often yield lower financial returns compared to medications for chronic diseases. Therefore, they receive less investment. Addressing AMR requires a comprehensive and coordinated international strategy. Implementing stringent regulations on antibiotic prescriptions can curb misuse but might not eliminate it completely. Financial incentives for pharmaceutical companies could stimulate the development of novel antibiotics. However, this approach remains contentious due to the varying perspectives on economic feasibility. Public health campaigns aimed at educating communities about judicious antibiotic use are vital, but experts continue to debate the most effective strategies for combating AMR.'),
    ('reading-rap-9e68f83255448c35a0994ea1', 'The passage mentions all of the following about Newton’s law of universal gravitation EXCEPT: 11. The passage mentions all of the following about Newton’s law of universal gravitation EXCEPT: It helped explain motion on Earth and in space. It stated that all objects pull on each other. It was later replaced by another theory. It made predictions that were only confirmed later. Why does the author mention “gravitational lensing” in the passage? 12. Why does the author mention “gravitational lensing” in the passage? To help explain why Einstein’s theory was superior to Newton’s To further illustrate Newton’s law of universal gravitation To give an example of a prediction made by Einstein To contrast terrestrial and planetary motion The word “minimized” in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word “minimized” in the passage is closest in meaning to eliminated reduced observed applied Why is it important for drug developers to determine a crystal’s molecular structure? 14. Why is it important for drug developers to determine a crystal’s molecular structure? To understand the effects of gravity on proteins in the human body To create drugs that target specific proteins To create more perfect crystals for industrial use To produce existing drugs more quickly and cheaply There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. For instance, parts of a crystal will grow unevenly as heavier components settle, leading to distortions in its shape. The concept of gravity has evolved over time. In the seventeenth century, Isaac Newton described gravity as a universal force acting between masses, formalized in his law of universal gravitation. This theory provided a universal, mathematical framework for understanding both terrestrial and planetary motion. However, in 1915, Albert Einstein introduced general relativity, redefining gravity as the curvature of spacetime caused by mass and energy. This shift explained phenomena Newton’s model could not, such as the bending of light near massive bodies (gravitational lensing). Einstein’s theory, which marked a revolution in physics, also predicted black holes and gravitational waves, phenomena later confirmed by observation. Theoretical advances in gravitational physics have led to practical applications, such as in drug development. Proteins often form crystals that can be analyzed to determine their molecular structure. However, on Earth, gravity interferes with the uniform growth of crystals. In microgravity environments, such as those aboard the International Space Station (ISS), gravitational effects are minimized. Crystals grown in space tend to be larger, more uniform, and less flawed than those grown on Earth, enabling researchers to map the crystals’ atomic structure with greater accuracy. This is crucial for rational drug design, allowing scientists to develop medications that precisely target disease-related proteins.'),
    ('reading-rap-a079611e1a822b5658af4b64', 'According to paragraph 1, what does the demographic transition model primarily demonstrate? 31. According to paragraph 1, what does the demographic transition model primarily demonstrate? Decreasing birth rates as societies industrialize Changing rates of birth and death as economies develop Differences in healthcare access among societies Consequences of rising death rates in industrial societies Which of the following can be inferred about agrarian societies? 32. Which of the following can be inferred about agrarian societies? They are more prosperous than industrialized societies. They are an exception to the trends proposed by the demographic transition model. They experience high birth rates and also high death rates. They suffer sharp population declines as a result of high death rates. The word "aspirations" in the passage is closest in meaning to 33. The word "aspirations" in the passage is closest in meaning to goals resources changes challenges Which of the following is NOT mentioned about low birth rates? 34. Which of the following is NOT mentioned about low birth rates? Sustained low birth rates result in negative population growth. Demographic changes brought about by low birth rates can lead to societal tensions. Persistently low birth rates typically lead to economic decline. Understanding demographic transitions linked to low birth rates helps societies plan for the future. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. As societies navigate this demographic shift, proactive policies and innovations become essential to sustain economic growth and promote well-being among older adults. The demographic transition model (DTM) illustrates the shifting patterns of birth and death rates as societies evolve from agrarian to industrialized economies. Initially, both rates are high, resulting in slow population growth due to limited access to healthcare and sanitation. As countries develop, death rates decline sharply with advances in medical care and improved living conditions. Yet, birth rates often remain high, spurring rapid population growth that can strain resources. This transition highlights the tension between demographic momentum and societal capability to meet the growing needs. A further decline in birth rates eventually occurs, influenced by greater access to education and changes in cultural norms. Families begin to view fewer children as advantageous, aligning with shifts toward economic and personal aspirations. However, this stage brings its own challenges, including the potential for an aging population and workforce shortages. Some demographers postulate a fifth stage, characterized by persistently low birth rates that might lead to population contraction. The implications of this are complex, ranging from economic innovation driven by necessity to increased strain on social institutions and intergenerational support systems. Understanding these transitions enables societies to better plan for future demographic realities, though the dynamic interplay of these factors is still unfolding.'),
    ('reading-rap-a640ab10b6b27bb18a8b1180', 'The passage indicates which of the following about “classroom disruptions”? 11. The passage indicates which of the following about “classroom disruptions”? They have less impact on academic outcomes than does class size. They tend to have a greater impact on less experienced teachers. They sometimes increase in frequency when class sizes are reduced. They can be decreased by having fewer students in a class. The author mentions “the magnitude of these gains” primarily to 12. The author mentions “the magnitude of these gains” primarily to support an approach to improve academic outcomes point out limitations to a statement about the effect of class size cite a factor that a research study failed to measure acknowledge that a study’s findings appear contradictory Which of the following best describes the relationship between paragraph 1 and paragraph 2? 13. Which of the following best describes the relationship between paragraph 1 and paragraph 2? Paragraph 2 summarizes competing arguments on the topic introduced in paragraph 1. Paragraph 2 confirms a widely held view mentioned in paragraph 1. Paragraph 2 presents evidence that contradicts the findings discussed in paragraph 1. Paragraph 2 points out that the findings presented in paragraph 1 are not complete. The passage identifies each of the following as an effect of reducing class sizes EXCEPT: 14. The passage identifies each of the following as an effect of reducing class sizes EXCEPT: The costs to schools increase. Students can receive more individualized attention. Teachers'' job satisfaction increases. Students’ academic performance improves. The word “nuanced” in the passage is closest in meaning to 15. The word “nuanced” in the passage is closest in meaning to refined good traditional ambitious Class size remains one of the most debated variables in education. Studies consistently show that smaller classes can enhance student learning by enabling more individualized attention and reducing classroom disruptions. For example, a 2025 study found that reduced student-to-teacher ratios tend to improve academic performance. However, the magnitude of these gains is often modest and accompanied by substantial financial costs. Of course, academic performance is not the only factor affected by class size. Classroom dynamics, student engagement, and students’ satisfaction with their experience are also affected. Researchers Wang and Calvano argue that evidence about the effects of class size remains incomplete because many investigations focus narrowly on academic outcomes rather than broader educational experiences. Many regions have implemented class-size reduction initiatives, but fiscal pressures have forced reconsideration of these policies. Given the financial constraints on schools, a nuanced approach seems advisable. Research has shown that reducing student-to-teacher ratios in the early years of education, such as in preschool and primary levels, and in higher poverty areas, as well as implementing strategies like small-group tutoring, offer a preferable cost-benefit ratio compared to general class size reduction.'),
    ('reading-rap-a67642faa0d4a3f2aca802b5', 'The word “seamlessly” in the passage is closest in meaning to 26. The word “seamlessly” in the passage is closest in meaning to generally smoothly repeatedly slowly What can be inferred about myoelectric sensors? 27. What can be inferred about myoelectric sensors? They function less effectively in traditional prosthetics. They rely less on electric signals than other types of sensors do. They allow for more natural movement in cybernetic prosthetics. They make cybernetic prosthetics somewhat cumbersome. What does the passage indicate about cybernetic hands? 28. What does the passage indicate about cybernetic hands? They are very similar in appearance to natural human limbs. They are currently limited in their range of motion. They rely on algorithms that turn muscle signals into movements. They can cause skin irritation if worn for extended periods. According to the passage, using haptic feedback in artificial limbs involves each of the following EXCEPT 29. According to the passage, using haptic feedback in artificial limbs involves each of the following EXCEPT detecting electrical signals from muscles changing the temperatures of objects stimulating skin and nerve endings using multiple sensors and actuators There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 30. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. To take full advantage of these amazing new technologies, a few obstacles need to be overcome. Cybernetic prosthetics have revolutionized medical technology, offering hope to individuals who have lost limbs. Unlike traditional prosthetics, which are often cumbersome and limited in function, cybernetic versions are designed to integrate seamlessly with the human nervous system. This integration allows for more natural movement and even sensation. A key development in this field has been the use of myoelectric sensors, which detect electrical signals generated by muscle movements. One example is a cybernetic hand that can perform delicate tasks such as picking up small objects or typing. This is achieved through advanced algorithms that translate muscle signals into controlled movements. Researchers are also exploring haptic feedback, which would enable users to feel textures and temperatures through their prosthetic limbs. It involves integrating sensors and actuators that mimic the sensation of touch by stimulating the user’s nerve endings or skin with electrical signals. This could significantly improve the quality of life for amputees. The high cost of these prosthetics makes them inaccessible to many. Additionally, the technology requires regular maintenance and updates, which can be a barrier. Current battery life is another limitation, as it restricts the user’s ability to wear the prosthetic for extended periods. Researchers are continually working to make these devices more affordable and user-friendly.'),
    ('reading-rap-a892b1f2362ab7405dd93573', 'The word “prevailing” in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word “prevailing” in the passage is closest in meaning to dominant successful traditional scientific According to the passage, all of the following were characteristics of Rogers'' patient-centered approach EXCEPT 12. According to the passage, all of the following were characteristics of Rogers'' patient-centered approach EXCEPT relying on each patient''s own diagnostic expertise creating a supportive context for treatment listening carefully to the patient promoting self-development by patients Why does the author of the passage mention “paraphrasing, summarizing, and clarifying”? 13. Why does the author of the passage mention “paraphrasing, summarizing, and clarifying”? To specify the note-taking that therapists do during patient sessions To clarify the concept of reflection in patient-centered therapy To illustrate some tools patients use to promote personal growth To explain how Rogers conducted research on therapeutic models The passage suggests which of the following about therapeutic approaches in the United States before the mid-twentieth century? 14. The passage suggests which of the following about therapeutic approaches in the United States before the mid-twentieth century? They were abandoned after Rogers became influential. They were not effective for patients with severe mental-health challenges. They were not backed by sufficient clinical research. They did not view empathy as a key therapeutic methodology. Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that best explains the intended outcomes for a patient receiving person-centered therapy. 15. Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that best explains the intended outcomes for a patient receiving person-centered therapy. When American psychologist Carl Rogers first introduced person-centered therapy in the mid-twentieth century, the prevailing view was that therapists should be experts in diagnosis and treatment. Instead, in his new approach, Rogers centered the patient as an individual who is able to discover and take steps toward personal growth. Counseling was viewed as a collaborative interaction between the professional and the patient, with the latter playing a key role in effecting change. One of Rogers'' core beliefs was that a nonjudgmental environment fosters self-discovery. The therapist acts as an empathetic facilitator, listening actively and using reflection—paraphrasing, summarizing, and clarifying the client''s words—to elicit the client''s feelings. Rogers claimed this approach allows individuals to gain the self-awareness and self-acceptance needed to grow personally and resolve issues. Rogers'' research suggested that the most successful patients were those who experienced the highest degree of empathy in therapy. Unlike Rogers'' model, earlier clinical approaches foregrounded issues and behaviors of concern, with the therapist diagnosing them and specifying treatment courses. Critics question the effectiveness of Rogers'' approach for patients seeking expert guidance, especially those with severe mental-health challenges who need structured intervention. However, Rogers'' theories remain influential: The person-centered paradigm is seen in various therapeutic approaches.'),
    ('reading-rap-a8c23b36a475b7ccee0d6756', 'Why does the author discuss silent films in paragraph 1? 31. Why does the author discuss silent films in paragraph 1? To describe the impact of the advent of sound in cinema To acknowledge that silent films limited actor expression To critique the use of acting techniques in modern films To show that gestures have always been crucial to acting The word “overt” in the passage is closest in meaning to 32. The word “overt” in the passage is closest in meaning to direct clever concise nuanced Paragraph 2 mentions all of the following about the use of gestures in modern theater and film EXCEPT: 33. Paragraph 2 mentions all of the following about the use of gestures in modern theater and film EXCEPT: It makes performances more genuine. It enables actors to convey more meaning. It is less common than it was in silent films. It is especially important for weaker actors. The passage suggests that contemporary actors 34. The passage suggests that contemporary actors minimize the use of gestures to avoid seeming old-fashioned rely more on physical gestures than on spoken dialogue employ gestures thoughtfully to enhance emotional authenticity favor gestures whose meanings are shared across many cultures According to paragraph 3, what is one skill that theater actors need in multicultural contexts? 35. According to paragraph 3, what is one skill that theater actors need in multicultural contexts? Anticipating audience interpretations across cultures Maintaining consistency in physical expression Avoiding gestures that carry emotional intensity Knowing when words alone cannot convey an emotion Gestures have always been integral to acting, serving as silent yet powerful tools for conveying emotions and intentions. Before the advent of sound in cinema, actors relied on exaggerated gestures to communicate, creating a universal language that transcended spoken words. This visual communication was essential in silent films, allowing actors to express complex narratives through movement alone. In modern theater and film, gestures have evolved into subtler devices, designed to complement spoken dialogue and enhance realism. A nuanced gesture, such as the slight arch of an eyebrow or a lingering touch, can reveal a character''s inner conflict or emotional transitions, adding layers of meaning without overt expression. This transformation reflects a broader shift toward naturalism in acting, where authenticity is paramount, and even the smallest gesture is deliberate. With such a great expressive power, as they say, comes great responsibility: cultural interpretations of gestures add complexity to acting in multicultural contexts. A gesture considered respectful in one culture might be misinterpreted in another as offensive or dismissive. Actors must adapt and modify their gestures to resonate with diverse audiences, a skill that distinguishes adept performers. This adaptability highlights how gestures are not merely physical movements but are deeply rooted in cultural understanding and empathy.'),
    ('reading-rap-abf7cca7bbd44e6e78417d21', 'The word "anomalies" in the passage is closest in meaning to 31. The word "anomalies" in the passage is closest in meaning to falsehoods gaps inconsistencies patterns All of the following are mentioned about harmonic analysis EXCEPT 32. All of the following are mentioned about harmonic analysis EXCEPT It involves the way functions and signals are represented. It has practical uses in several areas. It can reveal unseen structures in complex signals. It is more effective in music than in finance. In which of the following ways does the Fourier transform benefit signal processing? 33. In which of the following ways does the Fourier transform benefit signal processing? By making signals travel far By making signals clearer By protecting signals from noise By increasing the frequency of signals Why does the author discuss "quantum mechanics" in paragraph 2? 34. Why does the author discuss "quantum mechanics" in paragraph 2? To explain why the Fourier transform is a key tool of harmonic analysis To illustrate an application of breaking down functions into their constituent frequencies To highlight an important difference between signal processing and quantum mechanics To demonstrate the challenge of analyzing and predicting quantum systems There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. They enable the reduction of file sizes without significant loss of quality. Harmonic analysis studies how functions or signals can be represented as combinations of basic waves, typically sine and cosine functions. This field has applications in signal processing, quantum mechanics, and data compression. One intriguing aspect is its ability to reveal hidden structures within complex signals, such as identifying the fundamental frequencies in a musical piece or detecting anomalies in financial data. The Fourier transform is a key tool in harmonic analysis, breaking down functions into their constituent frequencies. For example, in signal processing, it isolates different frequency components, making it easier to filter out noise and enhance signal clarity. This decomposition is also crucial in quantum mechanics, where wave functions are expressed as combinations of base states, allowing for the analysis and prediction of quantum systems. Wavelet transforms offer precise localization of signals in both time and frequency domains, making them highly effective for analyzing transient or non-stationary signals. Their ability to adapt to various signal characteristics makes them versatile tools in numerous applications. These methods are particularly useful in data compression. In image processing, wavelet transforms facilitate efficient encoding of information, allowing for enhanced image reconstruction and feature extraction.'),
    ('reading-rap-af2f63bf59d1743d597f47f4', 'According to the passage, value theory is concerned with all of the following EXCEPT: 31. According to the passage, value theory is concerned with all of the following EXCEPT: how to determine what is good whether values are personal preferences how to distinguish between values and principles the difference between intrinsic and instrumental value What does the phrase “This consequentialist framework” refer to? 32. What does the phrase “This consequentialist framework” refer to? The view that the consequences of an action determine its morality Theories that foreground the happiness of the individual in assigning value The underlying view that theories of value have an impact on making life decisions The debate about how best to balance competing outcomes of happiness and utility The word “allocating” in the passage is closest in meaning to 33. The word “allocating” in the passage is closest in meaning to distributing acquiring increasing tracking According to the passage, what is one main criticism of utilitarianism? 34. According to the passage, what is one main criticism of utilitarianism? Its implementation may benefit public policy, but it is less helpful for economic decisions. Its priority of making healthcare more accessible creates higher costs for most people. In its efforts to benefit large numbers of people, it may harm some individuals. There is little evidence that it leads to improved healthcare for the majority. What does the passage suggest about value pluralism? 35. What does the passage suggest about value pluralism? It was a popular system for making decisions until quite recently. It gives primary consideration to compassion while autonomy plays a secondary role. It states that ethical decisions require the input of multiple decision-makers. It argues that ethical decisions should not be based on just one criterion. Value theory investigates the nature of values and the principles that determine what is worth pursuing, both individually and collectively. Central questions include: What makes something good? Are values objective features of the world or subjective preferences? A key distinction in value theory is between intrinsic value, which denotes value something has in itself, and instrumental value, which refers to the value of something as a means to an end. One influential theory within this domain is utilitarianism, which posits that the moral worth of actions is determined by their consequences, specifically the maximization of overall happiness or utility. This consequentialist framework has shaped ethical reasoning in fields ranging from public policy to economics. However, critics argue that utilitarianism’s aggregation of happiness can negate individual rights and seemingly justify morally troubling trade-offs. For example, allocating limited healthcare resources to benefit the majority may disadvantage those with rare or costly conditions, raising concerns about fairness and equity. Contemporary value theorists often endorse value pluralism, the view that multiple, sometimes conflicting values—such as autonomy, justice, and compassion—are irreducibly important. This pluralistic approach emphasizes the complexity of ethical decision-making and the need to navigate competing goods instead of reducing them to a single metric.'),
    ('reading-rap-b58232c37a080aa5aad5e9d1', 'The passage suggests that astronomers discovered dark galaxies by 31. The passage suggests that astronomers discovered dark galaxies by studying numerous cosmic phenomena caused by them observing their gravitational effects on other galaxies seeing them through conventional optical telescopes detecting UV radiation emitted by them What might prevent the formation of stars in dark galaxies? 32. What might prevent the formation of stars in dark galaxies? The accumulation of too much gas The turbulence inside gas clouds The presence of nearby galaxies The collapse of gas clouds Observations from the Hubble Space Telescope and other UV-sensitive instruments indicate that 33. Observations from the Hubble Space Telescope and other UV-sensitive instruments indicate that the number of dark galaxies in the universe increased over time dark galaxies contain more gas than previously believed dark galaxies emit more UV radiation than previously believed dark galaxies represent an early stage in galaxy development Observations of dark-galaxy regions with high UV radiation indicate that 34. Observations of dark-galaxy regions with high UV radiation indicate that dark galaxies evolved very slowly stars in the early universe were fairly cool and dense UV radiation in the early universe kept gas temperatures high a traditional theory of how dark galaxies formed is incorrect There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Therefore, one might expect all galaxies to have a significant number of stars. Ultraviolet (UV) astronomy has unveiled numerous cosmic phenomena. An especially intriguing discovery is that of dark galaxies. Unlike typical galaxies (gravitationally bound systems of stars, gas, and other particles), dark galaxies emit little to no visible light, making them nearly impossible to detect using conventional optical telescopes. However, these galaxies emit UV radiation, providing astronomers with a unique window to study them. Dark galaxies challenge our understanding of galaxy formation. Traditional models suggest that galaxies form through gas accumulation and star formation. But although dark galaxies contain vast amounts of gas, they have few, if any, stars. This raises questions about the processes that inhibit star formation. Some researchers propose that turbulence within the gas clouds might prevent the gas from collapsing to form stars. Observations from the Hubble Space Telescope and other UV-sensitive instruments have revealed that dark galaxies were more common in the early universe. These findings suggest that dark galaxies may represent a primordial phase in galaxy evolution. One hypothesis is that high levels of UV radiation in the early universe prevented the gas in dark galaxies from cooling and condensing into stars. Observations of regions with high UV radiation support this theory.'),
    ('reading-rap-b5efb7a2f7351888b25fd0ae', 'The word “remarkable” in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word “remarkable” in the passage is closest in meaning to mysterious abundant analyzed extraordinary Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that contains the definition of a term. Select the sentence to make your choice. 12. Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that contains the definition of a term. Select the sentence to make your choice. The passage indicates all of the following about spider silk-based sutures EXCEPT: 13. The passage indicates all of the following about spider silk-based sutures EXCEPT: They are less likely to cause inflammation. They are easily incorporated into human tissues. They are less expensive than traditional sutures. They are less likely to fail when placed under stress. All of the following are benefits of wound dressings made from spider silk EXCEPT: 14. All of the following are benefits of wound dressings made from spider silk EXCEPT: They speed up the recovery process. They are simple to prepare and apply. They do not dry out as quickly as other dressings. They make localized drug delivery easy. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. By contrast, silkworms, traditionally used for obtaining silk, are domesticated and easy to manage. Spider silk is one of nature’s most remarkable materials, known for its strength and elasticity. Stronger than steel and highly stretchable, spider silk has inspired researchers to explore its medical potential. Researchers have experimented with spider silk as a surgical suture material for use in stitching wounds, praising its strength and biocompatibility—a material’s ability to interact with the human body without causing adverse reactions, such as inflammation or rejection. Spider silk meets this criterion exceptionally well. It integrates smoothly with human tissues, minimizing immune response and promoting faster healing. Additionally, its natural elasticity helps the sutures adapt to bodily movements, reducing the risk of sutures rupturing under tension. Spider silk has been woven into advanced dressings for wounds that accelerate recovery. Spider silk’s biocompatibility and ability to retain moisture make it ideal for treating chronic wounds and joint injuries. Spider silk-based bandages can also be infused with medications for localized drug delivery, reducing infection risk and improving treatment outcomes. However, large-scale production is challenging. Spiders are territorial and cannibalistic, making farming them for silk impractical. Scientists have turned to synthetic methods of producing spider silk using genetically modified bacteria and yeast, but scaling up these techniques remains difficult. Researchers continue refining techniques to make spider silk more accessible.'),
    ('reading-rap-b83b93ea17489f66c6076362', 'The word "fostering" in the passage is closest in meaning to 31. The word "fostering" in the passage is closest in meaning to demanding promoting analyzing limiting Why does the author ask the question near the end of paragraph 2? 32. Why does the author ask the question near the end of paragraph 2? To suggest that recent studies about civic knowledge in the United States are wrong To offer a solution for increasing civic knowledge in the United States To suggest that participation in the democratic process increases civic knowledge To highlight the importance of having civic knowledge What does the author suggest about mock legislatures or congresses? 33. What does the author suggest about mock legislatures or congresses? They have been a part of civic knowledge curriculums for many years. They help students learn about different socioeconomic backgrounds. They are more appropriate for the classroom than mock trials are. They are an engaging way to teach civic knowledge. Identify the sentence in paragraph 3 that describes a problem in conducting civic education. 34. Identify the sentence in paragraph 3 that describes a problem in conducting civic education. Select the sentence to make your choice. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Understanding their rights and responsibilities helps citizens engage in society, advocate for change, and contribute to community development. A comprehensive civic education includes understanding the structure of a country''s government, electoral processes, and system of laws. It equips citizens with knowledge about their rights and responsibilities, fostering informed participation in the political process. Recent studies show a decline in civic knowledge among young people in the United States. Many are unfamiliar with even the most basic concepts. They struggle to describe how laws are made and name the roles of different government branches. Who is more likely to actively participate in the democratic process: a young person who knows how the government functions or one who does not? A successful democracy depends on active participation by its citizens. Efforts to address the problem include integrating civic education into school curriculums with activities that students find fun and exciting. Teachers can organize mock legislatures or congresses, where students role-play as members of a legislative body and draft, debate, and vote on bills. Students can also learn about the legal system by conducting mock trials. However, it should be noted that factors such as socioeconomic background and access to resources may limit access to high-quality civic education.'),
    ('reading-rap-b8ea2d783c95958891704f5a', 'Why does the author mention “a teacher’s communication with parents” in paragraph 2? 31. Why does the author mention “a teacher’s communication with parents” in paragraph 2? To illustrate the interactions among microsystems that characterize the mesosystem To support the claim that the institutions of the microsystem affect children directly To show that Bronfenbrenner’s theory is primarily linear To suggest that microsystems are more important than the mesosystem The phrase “subtly affecting” in the passage is closest in meaning to 32. The phrase “subtly affecting” in the passage is closest in meaning to affecting in harmless but unpredictable ways affecting in long-lasting ways affecting in ways both positive and negative affecting in small, barely noticeable ways The passage suggests that criticisms of Bronfenbrenner’s model call for which of the following? 33. The passage suggests that criticisms of Bronfenbrenner’s model call for which of the following? Its replacement with a less intricate framework Its modification to include virtual interactions Its replacement with a model that gives greater consideration to geographical boundaries Its exclusion from fields such as education and public policy Which of the following best describes the influence of Ecological Systems Theory? 34. Which of the following best describes the influence of Ecological Systems Theory? It has had little impact on psychology but has unexpectedly affected other fields. It revolutionized the field of human psychology but is not taken seriously in other fields. It changed the way human psychology is understood and continues to influence other fields. It has gone mostly unnoticed among psychologists and has had little impact on other fields. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Each one plays a distinct role in shaping behavior. Ecological Systems Theory, introduced by Urie Bronfenbrenner, revolutionized our perception of human psychological development. It posits that individuals are shaped by interactions among multiple overlapping environmental systems. This theory reshaped developmental research, offering a multi-faceted lens that surpasses earlier, linear models. By considering the dynamic interplay between a person and their environment, it highlights the multifactorial nature of psychological growth. The theory delineates several environmental layers, beginning with the microsystem, which refers to the institutions and groups that most directly impact the child’s development, such as family and school. The mesosystem encompasses the relationships among the microsystems, such as the impact of a teacher’s communication with parents on a child’s education. Beyond these, the exosystem consists of indirect influences like a parent’s workplace stress subtly affecting the home environment. The macrosystem, encompassing broader cultural and societal contexts, frames these interactions with underlying norms and policies. While Bronfenbrenner’s model offers an intricate framework, it is not without critique. Some argue that the model underestimates the role of technology, which has created virtual microsystems that transcend geographical limits. Despite these critiques, the theory remains influential in fields ranging from education to public policy, prompting continuous exploration of its applications and adaptations.'),
    ('reading-rap-b97ff220fda7f1599f4df33e', 'What does the passage indicate about rice bran? 31. What does the passage indicate about rice bran? It is present in white rice. It is more nutritious than pork and legumes. It contains a life-essential nutrient. It contains chemicals that cause disease. Why does the author mention “The predominant belief about disease at the time”? 32. Why does the author mention “The predominant belief about disease at the time”? To cite a widely held belief about the importance of vitamins To point out why Casimir Funk held a wrong assumption To suggest that toxins or infections may cause the loss of nutrients To explain why Casimir Funk''s proposal was revolutionary According to the passage, Casimir Funk was wrong about 33. According to the passage, Casimir Funk was wrong about whether thiamine was present in rice bran whether thiamine was an amine whether the absence of thiamine could cause beriberi whether the substance he extracted was in its pure form The passage supports all of the following statements about the term vitamine EXCEPT: 34. The passage supports all of the following statements about the term vitamine EXCEPT: It was invented by Casimir Funk. It was a combination of the words vita and amine. It was changed after scientists discovered that not all vitamins were essential for life. It was considered inaccurate because of its association with amines. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Vitamin C, for example, is an acid and contains no nitrogen at all. The first vitamin discovered was thiamine (vitamin B1). It is found in pork, legumes, and rice bran—the outer layer of brown rice that is removed during the milling process to make white rice. Around 1900, researchers realized that rice bran could cure a disease called beriberi. In 1912, Casimir Funk isolated an extract from rice bran that he determined to be an amine (a type of nitrogen compound). Funk proposed that the absence of certain amines in the diet can cause beriberi as well as other diseases. Because these amines were essential for life (vita in Latin), Funk coined the term vitamine. The predominant belief about disease at the time was that it was caused by toxins or infections. The idea that disease could arise from the absence of nutrients was revolutionary. Thiamine was indeed an amine whose absence can cause beriberi, but Funk''s extract likely contained a mixture of substances, and his chemical analysis was incomplete. Only later did researchers successfully isolate thiamine in its pure form. Additional life-essential nutrients were soon discovered. The problem was that many of them were not amines. The final e was thus dropped from vitamine to make the term less misleading.'),
    ('reading-rap-bad55ea4a5cad4980668095d', 'The word “revolutionize” in the passage is closest in meaning to 31. The word “revolutionize” in the passage is closest in meaning to simplify transform study use Why does the author discuss binary bits and qubits? 32. Why does the author discuss binary bits and qubits? To explain why quantum computers are more powerful than classical computers are To discuss the two ways in which quantum computers store data To emphasize the fast pace at which computing technology is advancing To explain what causes the state of superposition According to the passage, new encryption techniques are needed in the post-quantum world because 33. According to the passage, new encryption techniques are needed in the post-quantum world because quantum computers are less secure quantum computers store more valuable data than classical computers do quantum computers can break existing encryption quantum computers cannot process current encryption algorithms Why does the author mention “external disturbances”? 34. Why does the author mention “external disturbances”? To point out that qubits can only function in certain environmental conditions To introduce a similarity between quantum computing and traditional computing To explain how quantum computing creates unwanted fluctuations in magnetic or electric fields To suggest that quantum computing can be prohibitively expensive There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Seeing progress, companies and governments have begun to invest heavily in quantum research, aiming to unlock its potential. Quantum computing promises to revolutionize technology by delivering unprecedented computational power. Traditional computers store data in binary bits, representing 0 or 1. Quantum computers use qubits, which can represent 0, 1, or both simultaneously (a state called superposition). This allows quantum computers to process vast amounts of data simultaneously, solving complex problems much faster than classical computers. Quantum computing has promising applications in cryptography—techniques for encoding and decoding messages. Current encryption (encoding) methods rely on complex algorithms that classical computers struggle to break. Quantum computers could potentially break these encryption algorithms with ease, requiring new, quantum-resistant techniques. Researchers are exploring such methods to secure data in a post-quantum world. One challenge to fully realizing quantum computing is that qubits are highly sensitive to external disturbances. Maintaining coherence, the state needed by qubits to perform calculations, requires extremely low temperatures and isolation from environmental noise, such as magnetic or electric field fluctuations. Despite the challenges, progress is accelerating. Scientists are developing error-correcting codes and stable qubit designs to address these issues. Quantum computing holds promise for advancements in fields like medicine, finance, and artificial intelligence.'),
    ('reading-rap-bbe98bba9b4c8463d35912f4', 'According to the passage, metaphilosophy challenges which of the following basic assumptions of philosophical inquiry? 11. According to the passage, metaphilosophy challenges which of the following basic assumptions of philosophical inquiry? The world can be understood through logical analysis. Argumentation is a suitable tool for analyzing ideas. The conceptual tools that philosophy provides are universal. Human experience is a good source of philosophical insight. Why does the author note that concepts may carry different connotations across different cultures? 12. Why does the author note that concepts may carry different connotations across different cultures? To support the claim that philosophical frameworks apply globally To challenge the claim that language affects how ideas are expressed To provide a justification for metaphilosophical investigations To illustrate the idea of crossing cultural and linguistic boundaries The word "milieus" in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word "milieus" in the passage is closest in meaning to environments categories centers identities Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that describes an argument against the claims of metaphilosophy. Select the sentence to make your choice. 14. Identify the sentence in paragraph 2 that describes an argument against the claims of metaphilosophy. Select the sentence to make your choice. Which of the following is one criticism of metaphilosophy mentioned in the passage? 15. Which of the following is one criticism of metaphilosophy mentioned in the passage? It fails to challenge existing philosophical frameworks. It may prevent philosophy from accomplishing its mission. It is used by some philosophers to keep new ideas from becoming established. It is incapable of evaluating ideas from diverse traditions. Metaphilosophy is a field that looks critically at whether philosophical inquiry can truly transcend cultural and linguistic boundaries. While traditional philosophy often aims to uncover universal truths, metaphilosophy questions whether such truths are even accessible across diverse contexts. Language, far from being a neutral vessel, shapes and limits how ideas are expressed and understood. A concept that resonates deeply in one culture may carry entirely different connotations—or none at all—in another. This raises concerns about the global applicability of philosophical frameworks developed in specific cultural milieus. Are we uncovering truths, or merely reinforcing culturally contingent assumptions? Some insist that despite linguistic and cultural variation, certain philosophical concerns—like suffering, justice, or mortality—are shared across societies, suggesting a basis for universality. Yet even these themes may be interpreted through culturally specific lenses. Metaphilosophy doesn''t deny the possibility of cross-cultural dialogue but cautions against assuming it is seamless. It encourages philosophers to examine how their methods and assumptions travel—or fail to—across contexts. Critics worry this reflexivity may stall progress, but others see it as essential for avoiding intellectual overreach. By foregrounding these tensions, metaphilosophy aims not to dilute philosophy''s aims, but to sharpen them through greater self-awareness and methodological rigor.'),
    ('reading-rap-bd62b1abcf4df07dd1af993d', 'According to the passage, how is CRISPR-Cas9 technology superior to traditional genetic modification? 11. According to the passage, how is CRISPR-Cas9 technology superior to traditional genetic modification? It is easier for scientists to implement. It is supported by more extensive research. Its modifications do not involve foreign DNA. Its modifications are only temporary. Why does the passage mention "soybeans"? 12. Why does the passage mention "soybeans"? To illustrate CRISPR-Cas9''s potential to improve the nutritional content of crops To show that CRISPR-Cas9 technology works better in soybeans than in rice To highlight the role of food manufacturers in agricultural biotechnology To explain how crop traits relate to heart health The passage mentions all of the following potential negative effects of gene editing in agriculture EXCEPT 13. The passage mentions all of the following potential negative effects of gene editing in agriculture EXCEPT a decreased ability to survive in harsh conditions an increased chance of being harmed by pests a greater likelihood of suffering from diseases a higher risk of harming the wider environment The word "mitigate" in the passage is closest in meaning to 14. The word "mitigate" in the passage is closest in meaning to determine lessen describe warn about There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. One study about this danger cited the Irish Potato Famine, in which genetically similar varieties were all susceptible to late blight, causing a major catastrophe. Gene editing technologies, particularly CRISPR-Cas9, have transformed agricultural biotechnology by enabling precise modifications to plant genomes (genetic material). This allows scientists to enhance crop traits such as drought tolerance, pest resistance, and nutritional content without introducing foreign DNA (DNA from another species), distinguishing it from traditional genetic modification. For example, researchers have used CRISPR to develop rice varieties with improved yields and resistance to bacterial blight, a plant disease that is a major threat to global food security. In another case, gene-edited soybeans have been engineered to produce oil with reduced saturated fat and increased oleic acid—an unsaturated fat associated with heart health—making them more appealing for both consumers and food manufacturers. Despite its promise, gene editing in agriculture raises important concerns. Unintended off-target effects, where edits occur in nontarget regions of the genome, could potentially alter plant metabolism or reduce resilience to environmental stress. Additionally, widespread adoption of uniform edited traits may diminish genetic diversity, making crops more vulnerable to future pests or diseases. Such outcomes highlight the need for comprehensive field testing and multi-season trials to mitigate risks before commercial release. Scientists must evaluate not just the intended trait but also how gene edits affect overall plant health and adaptability.'),
    ('reading-rap-c2f68bcc6afcbbe710f5c87d', 'Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that contrasts two branches of mathematics. Select the sentence to make your choice. 11. Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that contrasts two branches of mathematics. Select the sentence to make your choice. Why does the author mention “fitting oranges into boxes”? 12. Why does the author mention “fitting oranges into boxes”? To explain the basic principles of discrete geometry to nonspecialists To help communicate the idea that packing problems are not as simple as they sound To help describe the historical development of packing problems To highlight one of the challenges faced by researchers in discrete geometry The study of computer-chip design and data storage has revealed that 13. The study of computer-chip design and data storage has revealed that the principles of continuous geometry are surprisingly relevant traditional assumptions about efficiency are still valid there are interesting connections between how shapes are arranged and how spaces are used there are new ways of enhancing the security of digital information The word “belie” in the passage is closest in meaning to 14. The word “belie” in the passage is closest in meaning to hide confirm enhance undermine There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. For example, Penrose-inspired patterns have been used in mosaic art and floor designs to create visually striking compositions that evoke mathematical harmony. Discrete geometry is a fascinating branch of mathematics that explores the hidden patterns formed by distinct geometric objects. Unlike continuous geometry—which deals with shapes and structures that are smooth and unbroken, like curves and surfaces—discrete geometry focuses on objects that are finite or countable like points, lines, and polygons. These distinct components reveal unexpected structures and relationships. Such revelations occur particularly in packing and tiling problems. Packing problems involve finding the most efficient arrangement of objects in a given space. While this might sound like a simple exercise—such as fitting oranges into boxes—it has far-reaching applications in fields like computer-chip design, where components must be arranged to maximize performance and minimize space, and in data storage, where digital information is packed efficiently to save memory and speed up access. Researchers have discovered intriguing connections between the arrangement of shapes and space utilization, challenging assumptions about efficiency. Tiling problems further illustrate the depth of discrete geometry. Penrose tiling, for instance, creates a pattern with non-repeating shapes that fill a plane, revealing underlying symmetrical properties. The insights gained from such tiling patterns have influenced architectural designs and artworks. The challenging, abstract nature of discrete geometry can all too easily belie its practical value. Mathematicians continue to show how these hidden structures drive innovation across fields.'),
    ('reading-rap-c508471e22cdb15e9cf6d0e3', 'Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that explains how CFD works. 11. Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that explains how CFD works. Select the sentence to make your choice. According to the passage, all of the following are true about CFD EXCEPT: 12. According to the passage, all of the following are true about CFD EXCEPT: It has supported innovations in aircraft design that enhance overall efficiency. It offers insights comparable to those offered by wind tunnels. It improves the accuracy of climate pattern forecasting. It has replaced physical experimentation in the field of aerospace engineering. Why does the author mention “improved turbine placement”? 13. Why does the author mention “improved turbine placement”? To examine the limitations of wind farms as sources of renewable energy To describe a specific application of CFD in the field of renewable energy To emphasize the computational demands associated with using CFD To highlight the role of rapid prototyping in wind turbine design The word “alleviate” in the passage is closest in meaning to 14. The word “alleviate” in the passage is closest in meaning to support meet reduce modify What can be inferred about the current state of machine learning models in CFD? 15. What can be inferred about the current state of machine learning models in CFD? They are more reliable than traditional models. They still face challenges in modeling real-world physical systems. They have been widely used in renewable energy optimization. They have not been known to improve algorithms. Computational fluid dynamics (CFD) is transforming the study of how fluids, including air, move. With CFD, digital models can offer insights previously limited to physical models like wind tunnels. This digital approach uses algorithms to simulate complex fluid interactions, pushing boundaries in fields from aerospace engineering to oceanography. In aerospace engineering, CFD enables the design of more efficient aircraft by modeling airflow over wings and fuselage, reducing drag and improving fuel efficiency, while in oceanography, it helps predict ocean currents, wave dynamics, and climate patterns with greater precision, aiding environmental monitoring and disaster preparedness. Renewable energy is another area where CFD is proving invaluable. In designing wind farms, CFD simulations predict airflow patterns, enabling improved turbine placement for energy maximization. Yet, this benefit is often counterbalanced by the computational intensity required, which can limit accessibility and rapid prototyping. The integration of machine learning and artificial intelligence into CFD promises to alleviate some of these computational demands. These technologies can optimize algorithms, enhancing simulation speed and accuracy. However, this integration is not without hurdles. The reliability of machine learning models in accurately reflecting physical phenomena is still a topic of ongoing research, as flawed models could yield misleading results.'),
    ('reading-rap-c60864a200a09f24ffb1835d', 'What can be inferred about the 1952 Great Smog of London? 31. What can be inferred about the 1952 Great Smog of London? It affected several other European cities. It resulted from multiple factors that occurred simultaneously. It disrupted the normal activities of factories for five days. It made residents switch to coal to heat their homes. The word "premature" in the passage is closest in meaning to 32. The word "premature" in the passage is closest in meaning to early recorded preventable likely Why was the Great Smog of London considered a pivotal event? 33. Why was the Great Smog of London considered a pivotal event? It lasted longer than usual. It occurred at an unlikely time of the year. It led to significant environmental regulations. It caused the immediate closure of all factories. Why does the author bring up the impact on the ambulance service? 34. Why does the author bring up the impact on the ambulance service? To highlight one of the results of low visibility To explain the main cause of the surging death rate To suggest a reason why hospitals were overwhelmed To indicate that authorities closed London roads for travel There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Local authorities created "smoke control areas" where only smokeless fuels could be burned. The Great Smog of London in 1952 was a pivotal event in environmental history. For five days in December, a thick layer of smog blanketed the city, caused by cold weather, windless conditions, and pollutants. The primary sources were coal-burning fires for heating homes and factories, releasing sulfur dioxide and other toxic chemicals. As the smog settled, it became a health crisis. Visibility was reduced to a few meters, and transportation was disrupted. Many people abandoned their cars, and the ambulance service was severely impacted. Thousands developed respiratory problems, and the death rate surged. The Great Smog caused up to 12,000 premature deaths and countless illnesses. Hospitals were overwhelmed with patients, and morgues quickly reached capacity. In response, the British government took action. New regulations aimed at reducing air pollution were established. The most significant was the Clean Air Act of 1956, which restricted coal burning in urban areas and promoted cleaner energy sources. Despite progress, air pollution remains a global challenge. Many cities still struggle with poor air quality due to industrial activities, vehicle emissions, and other sources. The Great Smog of London serves as a stark reminder of the need to address environmental issues to protect public health.'),
    ('reading-rap-c63fb3651607230289cc9a21', 'The word "retention" in the passage is closest in meaning to 31. The word "retention" in the passage is closest in meaning to creation replacement transfer preservation REM sleep and deep sleep differ in which of the following ways? 32. REM sleep and deep sleep differ in which of the following ways? Deep sleep is limited to the early stages of the sleep cycle, while REM sleep is not. REM sleep is more important for skill development than deep sleep. REM sleep involves less brain activity than deep sleep. REM sleep and deep sleep are each linked to separate types of memory. The passage suggests which of the following about the effect of sleep on memory? 33. The passage suggests which of the following about the effect of sleep on memory? Sleep improves memory primarily by increasing the total number of neural connections. Sleep improves memory by selectively eliminating some neural connections. Sleep affects the strength of memories only during the rapid eye movement (REM) stage. Sleep strengthens the memory of emotionally charged events but not of factual information. Why does the author use the phrase “Not surprisingly” to begin paragraph 3? 34. Why does the author use the phrase “Not surprisingly” to begin paragraph 3? To suggest that the effects of sleep deprivation follow logically from earlier claims To emphasize that people are often conscious of memory lapses after a period of poor sleep To point out a reason for scientists’ interest in establishing a link between sleep and memory To acknowledge that sleep quality is as essential to memory retention as sleep quantity There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. This interpretation focuses on the vital role sleep plays in helping the brain function in an optimal way. Sleep is not merely a passive period of rest; it plays an active role in the consolidation of memory, a process that has been extensively examined by neuroscientists over several decades. During sleep, distinct stages—most notably rapid-eye-movement (REM) sleep and deep sleep—contribute in different ways to how memories are stored and organized. Experimental research suggests that while REM sleep strengthens emotionally charged memories, deep sleep supports the retention of factual knowledge and the acquisition of skills. Some researchers further propose that sleep functions as a kind of neural "data management" system. While awake, the brain continuously forms new connections as it processes incoming information, creating a dense and potentially inefficient network. During sleep, however, unnecessary connections are trimmed, whereas essential ones are reinforced, a reorganization that enhances the brain''s ability to retrieve information accurately. Not surprisingly, sleep deprivation has been shown to impair memory retention and conceptual integration. Even a single night of lost sleep can reduce recall and interfere with learning. Nevertheless, studies also indicate that recovery sleep—particularly when it is deep and uninterrupted—can partially reverse these effects, suggesting that although sleep is critical for memory, the brain retains a limited capacity to adapt to disruption.'),
    ('reading-rap-cb3cd375aebae1a5539edb81', 'All of the following are true about libertarianism EXCEPT: 31. All of the following are true about libertarianism EXCEPT: It emphasizes free markets. It champions personal freedom. It supports state involvement in people’s lives. It has recently grown in popularity. Libertarians might consider taxation immoral because it violates 32. Libertarians might consider taxation immoral because it violates the principle of self-ownership laws against use of coercive force legal standards about bodily autonomy conventions of liberal society The word “impediments” in the passage is closest in meaning to 33. The word “impediments” in the passage is closest in meaning to rules barriers measures costs Libertarians themselves do not agree with one another about whether 34. Libertarians themselves do not agree with one another about whether the libertarian approach creates educational opportunities a laissez-faire approach is good for businesses healthcare requires more government involvement than education personal freedom should be balanced against the need for social welfare There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Such intervention constrains innovation and can lead to unintended outcomes. Libertarianism is a political philosophy emphasizing individual liberty, minimal government intervention, and free markets. It advocates personal freedom, arguing that individuals should make choices without undue state interference. This ideology has gained traction in recent years among those who value economic freedom. A central tenet of libertarianism is self-ownership, which asserts that individuals own their bodies and labor. Libertarians argue that people should control their lives and property and be free from coercive forces. For instance, libertarians often oppose taxation, viewing it as involuntary servitude that infringes on autonomy. Libertarianism champions free markets as the most efficient means of allocating resources. By allowing supply and demand to dictate prices, free markets encourage innovation and competition, leading to better products and services. Government regulations are seen as impediments that stifle economic growth and limit freedom. Libertarians believe that a laissez-faire approach creates an environment where businesses can thrive and individuals can pursue their economic interests without constraints. Critics of libertarianism argue that a lack of regulation can lead to social inequality and exploitation. Some contend that public goods, like education and healthcare, require government involvement to ensure equitable access. Balancing personal freedom with the need for social welfare remains contentious within libertarian thought.'),
    ('reading-rap-cb5360908903e7adcdf0760d', 'The word “exacerbate” in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word “exacerbate” in the passage is closest in meaning to contribute to cause control increase Why does the author mention Bangladesh and the Netherlands? 12. Why does the author mention Bangladesh and the Netherlands? To illustrate that strong storms pose threats to infrastructure in coastal areas To give examples of countries for which coastal erosion presents challenges beyond environmental ones To provide examples of countries that have recently invested in protecting their coastal infrastructure To acknowledge that coastal erosion is a problem that affects countries in a variety of regions The passage indicates which of the following about “beach nourishment”? 13. The passage indicates which of the following about “beach nourishment”? It involves an ongoing commitment to maintain the advantages it offers. It is effective primarily in areas that already have natural dune systems. It is a relatively expensive method of combating erosion. It is primarily effective in areas where the coastal zones have little residential development. What can be inferred about the “natural approaches” mentioned in the passage? 14. What can be inferred about the “natural approaches” mentioned in the passage? They offer significant protection of coastal areas but are short-term measures. They are easier to implement in areas with high population density. They are less likely than man-made structures to cause unintended harm to surrounding shorelines. They are more effective as a way of protecting biodiversity than as a measure to combat erosion. The passage indicates each of the following about erosion-control measures EXCEPT: 15. The passage indicates each of the following about erosion-control measures EXCEPT: They can sometimes deflect wave energy. They sometimes disrupt transportation in coastal areas. They vary in the amount of space they require. They have greater economic impact in some countries than in others. Coastal erosion, the gradual loss of shoreline due to natural forces, poses challenges worldwide. Waves, currents, and rising sea levels are primary contributors to the phenomenon. Strong storms can exacerbate erosion, reshaping coastlines abruptly. For countries with extensive coastal zones, such as Bangladesh and the Netherlands, managing erosion is not only an environmental issue but also an economic and social one. Coastal erosion threatens infrastructure, transportation, and residential areas, necessitating costly investments in protective measures. One strategy to combat erosion is beach nourishment, the addition of sand or sediment to shorelines. While this method provides short-term benefits, frequent replenishment is required. Seawalls and breakwaters, solid structures designed to deflect wave energy and protect coastlines, are longer-term solutions but can alter natural sediment movement, unintentionally accelerating erosion in nearby areas. Recent studies suggest that some natural approaches may be more sustainable. For instance, mangrove forests and dune systems serve as buffers, reducing wave energy before it reaches populated areas. Mangroves not only provide erosion control but also support diverse ecosystems. However, such methods require extensive space and careful planning, making them unsuitable for heavily urbanized coastal zones. This highlights the tension between maintaining natural landscapes and safeguarding human developments along vulnerable coastlines.'),
    ('reading-rap-cb8348e4dc572c5c5d97239a', 'What can be inferred about emerging diagnostic methodologies? 11. What can be inferred about emerging diagnostic methodologies? They may be more effective at detecting diseases early than traditional methods. They have already become standard practice in certain parts of the world. Their complexities are comparable to those of traditional methods. Their use for continuous disease detection eliminates the need for clinical oversight. What problem associated with liquid biopsies is mentioned in the passage? 12. What problem associated with liquid biopsies is mentioned in the passage? They are very costly to perform. They can lead to unnecessary medical treatments. The results of the biopsies take a long time to process. The biopsies must be supplemented by surgical tissue sampling. The passage suggests that wearable devices 13. The passage suggests that wearable devices will not likely be adopted by healthcare systems produce large amounts of data to be monitored and stored have consistently yielded reliable information for clinicians are uncomfortable and therefore unpopular with patients The word “rigorous” in the passage is closest in meaning to 14. The word “rigorous” in the passage is closest in meaning to general preliminary rapid thorough There are four locations in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. They are less expensive and less invasive for the patient than surgical biopsies. Diagnostic methodologies in clinical laboratory sciences are undergoing significant shifts. Traditional methods, such as blood tests and imaging techniques, have been standard for decades. However, emerging technologies like liquid biopsies (highly specialized forms of blood testing) and wearable devices are reshaping diagnostics by offering the potential for earlier and more continuous disease detection, though their complexity demands careful evaluation. Tumors release cells and DNA fragments into the bloodstream, which liquid biopsies can detect through a simple blood draw—eliminating the need for surgical tissue sampling while still enabling the monitoring of tumor progression and genetic mutations. Yet, this innovative technique has drawbacks such as false positives, which can lead to unnecessary medical interventions. Meanwhile, wearable devices provide continuous monitoring of vital signs, supplying real-time data to clinicians. The integration of such devices into healthcare systems introduces challenges related to managing vast amounts of patient data and ensuring the reliability of the collected information. The potential of these technologies is undeniable, but they are not without hurdles. Liquid biopsies and wearable devices illustrate the intersection of innovation and practicality in diagnostics. As they evolve, they present opportunities to redefine disease management, although rigorous validation is essential for clinical integration.'),
    ('reading-rap-cc2ae528502c9a992ff2f2a4', 'The passage suggests that, unlike traditional drug discovery, drug discovery based on computational chemistry 31. The passage suggests that, unlike traditional drug discovery, drug discovery based on computational chemistry is far less time-consuming overall is significantly more expensive to conduct eliminates the need for lab work requires a greater number of researchers According to the passage, how do machine learning algorithms speed up the drug discovery process? 32. According to the passage, how do machine learning algorithms speed up the drug discovery process? By narrowing the size of the chemical spaces that need to be analyzed By helping to eliminate compounds that are not promising for testing By increasing the number of proteins that can be targeted by compounds By providing a comprehensive list of drug candidates According to the passage, what is the purpose of repeatedly simulating various modifications to a molecular structure? 33. According to the passage, what is the purpose of repeatedly simulating various modifications to a molecular structure? To create new biological targets To reduce the number of simulations needed in the long run To enhance the therapeutic profiles of drug candidates To eliminate the need for experimental validation The word “formidable” in the passage is closest in meaning to 34. The word “formidable” in the passage is closest in meaning to very challenging very exciting urgent manageable There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 30. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Despite these advancements, challenges remain. Computational chemistry has transformed drug discovery by providing tools to model and predict molecular behavior. Traditionally, drug discovery involved labor-intensive trial and error. Now, with advanced algorithms and powerful computers, researchers can test interactions between drug candidates and biological targets via computer simulations before moving to the lab. One major advantage of computational methods is their ability to analyze vast chemical spaces. For example, machine learning algorithms can predict the binding affinity of millions of compounds to a target protein, narrowing down candidates for testing. This speeds up the discovery process and reduces costs. In one case, researchers identified a promising compound for treating a rare disease in weeks, a task that would have taken years using traditional methods. Moreover, computational chemistry optimizes drug properties. By simulating different modifications to a molecular structure, researchers can predict changes in a drug''s efficacy and safety. Repeating and refining this process fine-tunes drug candidates to achieve optimal therapeutic profiles. For example, modifying a molecule to enhance stability in the bloodstream can prevent it from breaking down too quickly, ensuring it reaches its target. Computational predictions must be validated experimentally, as computer models can sometimes produce false positives. Accurately simulating the human body''s complex environment remains a formidable task.'),
    ('reading-rap-cc8108658460dd4758f81cea', 'Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage? 11. Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage? Italian lute songs became more sophisticated in the sixteenth century as a result of Italian musicians’ incorporation of foreign influences. The increased availability of printed lute music, together with courtly patronage, fostered a flourishing culture of lute music in sixteenth-century Italy. Innovations in printing have emerged as the most likely cause of a surge in the composition of sixteenth-century Italian lute songs, replacing older, now disproven theories. As professional sixteenth-century Italian musicians gained more opportunities to offer private performances in aristocratic homes, composers altered the kind of music written for the lute. The passage suggests which of the following about amateur musicians in sixteenth-century Italy? 12. The passage suggests which of the following about amateur musicians in sixteenth-century Italy? Their repertoire prior to the sixteenth century did not include compositions for the lute. They performed in private homes alongside professional musicians invited by aristocratic patrons. Their musical tastes influenced the types of compositions commissioned for the lute by patrons like Alfonso d’Este. The extent to which they performed lute music in private homes was affected by an innovation in the printing industry. The word “lavish” in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word “lavish” in the passage is closest in meaning to traditional practical fancy common The author mentions “heraldic emblems” primarily to 14. The author mentions “heraldic emblems” primarily to cite evidence of the influence of patronage on Italian lute music provide evidence that printed lute music grew more complex over time demonstrate that music written for the lute became very fashionable indicate one way in which aristocrats personalized books of printed music Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as contributing to the mid-sixteenth-century surge of Italian lute songs? 15. Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as contributing to the mid-sixteenth-century surge of Italian lute songs? The availability of printed lute music Courtly patronage of virtuosos Improvements in lute construction Stylistic borrowings from the Spanish vihuela Some historians explain the mid-sixteenth-century surge of Italian lute songs as the product of technological innovation combined with shifting social tastes. Around 1500, Ottavio dei Petrucci developed a groundbreaking movable-type printing technique for music, making it easier and cheaper to produce books of lute music. Suddenly, amateur musicians from Naples to Venice could acquire fashionable arrangements of madrigals, villanellas, and solo ricercars. This ease of access dovetailed with an expanding culture of private performance in aristocratic homes. Other historians point to a different catalyst: courtly patronage. Alfonso d’Este, the duke of Ferrara, played a decisive role by commissioning virtuosos like Francesco da Milano and Antonio Valente, whose published collections set a high artistic benchmark. Evidence for both theories emerges in extant manuscripts: Some contain personalized annotations indicating home practice, while lavish presentation copies bear heraldic emblems of noble households. Furthermore, stylistic borrowings from the Spanish vihuela and French lute idioms suggest a pan-European dialogue that reinforced Italian composers’ ambitions. While the printing press democratized repertoire, it was the symbiosis of domestic enthusiasm and aristocratic sponsorship—each reinforcing the other—that, arguably, propelled the Italian lute song to its enduring prominence.'),
