key concept in sociocybernetics is self-organization. This is the process in which social systems develop ordered patterns without centralized control. Feedback loops are crucial in sociocybernetics. Positive feedback amplifies changes, leading to rapid social transformations. Negative feedback stabilizes systems, maintaining equilibrium. In economic systems, positive feedback can result in booming markets, while negative feedback can prevent financial crises. Recognizing these mechanisms helps sociologists predict and influence social dynamics. Communication is another critical aspect. Effective communication channels within social systems impact their stability and adaptability. Misinformation or communication breakdowns can disrupt social cohesion and lead to chaos. Sociocybernetic models highlight the importance of transparent information flow in maintaining healthy social systems. These models also explore how digital technologies influence communication patterns. Sociocybernetics is important to urban planning. Cities are complex systems with numerous interacting components. Using sociocybernetic principles, urban planners design more resilient cities by modeling traffic patterns, resource distribution, and emergency response strategies. This approach helps create sustainable urban environments that better respond to changes.'),
    ('reading-rap-d27de865cadb77d675378075', 'What can be inferred about intricate manuscripts during the Middle Ages? 31. What can be inferred about intricate manuscripts during the Middle Ages? They were individually made. They were produced using the printing press. They were widely available to the general public. They depicted mostly landscapes. The phrase "advocated for" in the passage is closest in meaning to 32. The phrase "advocated for" in the passage is closest in meaning to mentioned promoted debated inspired Why does the author discuss the "grid-based approach"? 33. Why does the author discuss the "grid-based approach"? To argue that the approach should be adopted more widely To question the relevance of the approach in modern design To show how the principles of minimalism and functionality were used by Swiss designers To indicate how Swiss designers differed from the majority of Bauhaus designers It can be inferred that many of today''s graphic designers are concerned that digital tools 34. It can be inferred that many of today''s graphic designers are concerned that digital tools undermine the democratization of the arts are not responsive to public debates in the arts are not precise enough to be useful in the design process do not always reflect the warmth of human creativity There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. However, this clarity sometimes came at the cost of creativity. Graphic design has transformed repeatedly throughout history, influenced by both cultural shifts and technological advancements. In Europe, during the Middle Ages (the fifth to fifteenth centuries), the creation of intricate manuscripts was predominant. The advent of the printing press in the fifteenth century altered these dynamics, facilitating mass production and broadening public access to designed materials. Such technological shifts not only changed the way art was produced but also how it was perceived. By the mid-twentieth century, Swiss design emerged as a response to the chaotic visual landscape, introducing principles of minimalism and functionality. Heavily influenced by the Bauhaus movement, designers like Josef Müller-Brockmann advocated for simplicity and rationality. Their grid-based approach brought order and readability to what often seemed to be overwhelming visuals. Some critics argue that the rigid structure, while effective, limited expressive possibilities and emotional resonance in design. Today, graphic design stands at the intersection of tradition and innovation. While digital tools democratize access and encourage experimentation, designers grapple with preserving artistic integrity. The challenge lies in blending the precision of technology with the warmth of human creativity. Thus, graphic design continues to evolve, reflecting broader cultural debates about art and technology.'),
    ('reading-rap-d5558237a7aeccbb4aea9cca', 'The passage suggests which of the following about the songs of whales? 11. The passage suggests which of the following about the songs of whales? They can best be understood as instinctive behaviors related to aspects of whales'' environments. They evolve over time in ways that are not typical of behaviors generally accepted as cultural. They differ from tool use by chimpanzees, in that they reflect the influence of genetics on behavior. They are considered by some to be cultural phenomena because they are not uniform across whale groups. The author mentions "cumulative innovation" primarily to 12. The author mentions "cumulative innovation" primarily to characterize one view about how culture should be defined explain how some animal behaviors evolve over generations specify which human abilities are shared by some animal species cite one way in which some species adapt to environmental pressures The passage indicates that those holding the "middle position" 13. The passage indicates that those holding the "middle position" have been accused of stretching the meaning of culture too far differ from most scientists in their view of which behaviors are instinctive consider symbolic meaning to be an essential element of cultures recognize some elements of cultural phenomena in animal behavior The word "rudimentary" in the passage is closest in meaning to 14. The word "rudimentary" in the passage is closest in meaning to distinct basic productive interesting The author would most likely agree that the "long-held assumptions" mentioned in the final paragraph 15. The author would most likely agree that the "long-held assumptions" mentioned in the final paragraph have largely been abandoned by scientists today uphold a definition of culture that is too rigid are supported by recent findings about animal behavior reveal something important about socially transmitted behaviors Can animals be said to possess culture? Many scientists argue that certain species do exhibit cultural traits, defining culture broadly as behaviors learned socially and transmitted across generations. Classic examples include chimpanzees using tools in ways distinctive to their local community, or whales developing group-specific songs that evolve over time. These patterns suggest that at least some animals maintain traditions not rooted solely in genetics or instinct. Others argue that applying the term "culture" to animals risks stretching the concept beyond usefulness. Human cultures involve symbolic meaning and cumulative innovation—the ability to build on previous discoveries so that knowledge and practices become more sophisticated over generations. Nonhuman species have not convincingly demonstrated these abilities. Animal behaviors that appear cultural may instead reflect adaptation to environmental pressures or simple imitation, not deeper shared understandings. A middle position acknowledges that while animals may not possess culture in the sense humans do, they can still display rudimentary cultural phenomena. This view emphasizes a continuum rather than a strict divide, suggesting that cultural capacities evolved gradually and appear in varying degrees across species. Ultimately, whether animals have culture depends on how culture is defined, but growing evidence of socially transmitted behaviors challenges long-held assumptions about the uniqueness of human societies.'),
    ('reading-rap-e01f83375de9f2c96a209bc6', 'Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as an aspect of the study of linguistic typology? 11. Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as an aspect of the study of linguistic typology? It includes the study of word order as a means of aiding comprehension. It has revealed both diversity and universality among structural linguistic features. It identifies three basic structural patterns across all languages. It examines differences in morphological complexity among languages. Why does the author note that placing the subject first in a sentence aids comprehension? 12. Why does the author note that placing the subject first in a sentence aids comprehension? To suggest that a larger set of available word orders increases cognitive efficiency To provide an example of a structural feature that follows a universal pattern To help explain why there is only a small set of dominant word orders across languages To suggest that languages that follow a different pattern are harder to learn What is suggested about the morphological complexity of Turkish? 13. What is suggested about the morphological complexity of Turkish? It is greater than the morphological complexity of Mandarin Chinese. It is balanced by a relatively simple inflection system. It developed independently of other elements of Turkish. It requires Turkish to adhere to a relatively rigid word order. The word "pressures" in the passage is closest in meaning to 14. The word "pressures" in the passage is closest in meaning to difficulties conflicts features influences There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Such changes can alter the communicative priorities of a speech community, affecting which features are emphasized or simplified over time. Linguistic typology investigates structural features across languages, revealing both universal patterns and language-specific variation. A central area of study is word order-the arrangement of subjects, verbs, and objects. English follows a subject-verb-object (SVO) pattern, while Japanese uses subject-object-verb (SOV). Most languages conform to a limited set of dominant word orders, which may reflect cognitive efficiency: Placing the subject first helps listeners quickly identify the sentence''s main actor, aiding comprehension. Beyond syntax, languages differ in morphological complexity —the degree to which inflection (such as noun or verb endings) marks grammatical relationships. Turkish uses extensive inflection to express subtle distinctions, whereas Mandarin Chinese relies more on word order and context. These contrasts suggest that languages balance morphology and syntax based on communicative needs. Highly inflected languages can afford flexible word order because grammatical roles are marked morphologically. In contrast, languages with minimal inflection often depend on fixed word order to maintain clarity. This trade-off reflects deeper functional pressures. Languages evolve not randomly but in response to cognitive constraints and social factors, including contact with other languages and cultural shifts. Studying this evolution helps linguists understand not only how languages differ but why certain grammatical strategies emerge and persist across communities.'),
    ('reading-rap-e30249024b7753af6bbf9d8d', 'The passage suggests that the “urban environments” mentioned in paragraph 1 were significant because they 11. The passage suggests that the “urban environments” mentioned in paragraph 1 were significant because they supported theatrical venues used primarily for dance performances fostered social gatherings in which dancing regularly occurred enhanced people’s exposure to a variety of cultural traditions were the first settings in which percussive dances emerged Why does the author mention “early footwork patterns” in paragraph 1? 12. Why does the author mention “early footwork patterns” in paragraph 1? To distinguish certain features of tap dance from similar features of Irish and English dances To cite a scholarly view about the contributions of certain traditions to tap dance To support the idea that West Africans played a central role in shaping tap dance To describe one area where tap dance did not borrow from earlier traditions The phrase “relegated to” in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The phrase “relegated to” in the passage is closest in meaning to popular in limited to introduced at absent from According to paragraph 2, commercial theater was significant in which of the following ways? 14. According to paragraph 2, commercial theater was significant in which of the following ways? According to some scholars, it weakened the improvisational nature of tap dance. It was responsible for bringing tap dancing to the attention of filmmakers. It launched the careers of a new generation of tap dancers who were not African American. According to some scholars, it brought tap dancing to nonurban audiences. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. These debates reflect a continuing inquiry into the ways in which modern urban cultural phenomena arose from older ethnic customs. Tap dance emerged in the United States during the nineteenth century, but its development reflects a long and complex history shaped by African, Irish, and English percussive dance traditions. Scholars generally agree that the form crystallized in urban environments where diverse communities lived in close proximity, but they disagree about the relative influence of these traditions. Some historians emphasize the centrality of West African rhythmic practices and improvisational structures, while others highlight the importance of Irish step dancing and English clogging, particularly in shaping early footwork patterns. By the early twentieth century, tap had become a recognizable theatrical form standardized through vaudeville theater circuits and later through film. African American practitioners such as Bill “Bojangles” Robinson, John Bubbles, and the Nicholas Brothers expanded the form’s technical and expressive possibilities, though their contributions were often relegated to racially segregated performance contexts. Scholars also differ in their interpretations of how commercial entertainment shaped tap’s evolution: some argue that theatrical venues diluted its improvisational roots, while others contend that these spaces enabled innovation by exposing dancers to new audiences and stylistic influences. Despite these disagreements, dance historians view tap as a dynamic, hybrid practice whose history illuminates broader patterns of cultural interaction and artistic creativity.'),
    ('reading-rap-e7ce2874e8c366c253f0bed0', 'What is the purpose of green infrastructure like parks and green roofs? 31. What is the purpose of green infrastructure like parks and green roofs? To increase urban density To prevent flooding To foster community engagement To generate renewable energy The word “fosters” in the passage is closest in meaning to 32. The word “fosters” in the passage is closest in meaning to describes neglects encourages replaces What does the passage suggest is essential for resilience strategies to be fully effective? 33. What does the passage suggest is essential for resilience strategies to be fully effective? Discouraging the uncontrolled growth of urban populations The involvement of local government and citizens Less reliance on smart technology Limiting the number of structures in urban areas Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a challenge for urban resilience? 34. Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a challenge for urban resilience? Financial limitations Political disagreements Integration of new technologies Community participation There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. For example, Singapore’s Intelligent Transport System incorporates artificial intelligence (AI) to predict real-time traffic conditions, effectively manage road congestion, and optimize routes. Urban resilience refers to the ability of cities to absorb, recover from, and prepare for shocks such as natural disasters, economic crises, and social upheavals. This concept is becoming increasingly important as more people move into urban areas and face the challenges that come with densely populated areas. Cities are implementing various strategies to enhance resilience. For instance, green infrastructure, like parks and green roofs, can help manage stormwater and reduce flooding. Renewable energy sources, such as solar and wind, reduce dependence on non-renewable resources and improve energy security. Community engagement is another critical component of urban resilience. Local governments are involving residents in planning and decision-making processes to ensure that the needs of the community are met. This participatory approach fosters a sense of ownership and accountability among citizens, improving the effectiveness of resilience strategies. Technology also plays a role, with smart city initiatives using data and sensors to monitor infrastructure and predict potential problems. Nonetheless, building urban resilience is not without obstacles. Budget constraints and political disagreements can hinder the implementation of necessary measures. Additionally, the integration of new technologies requires substantial investment and training. Despite these obstacles, the pursuit of urban resilience is essential for the sustainable development of cities worldwide.'),
    ('reading-rap-e87bf5f62e80a618270d51db', 'Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that defines an important term. Select the sentence to make your choice. 11. Identify the sentence in paragraph 1 that defines an important term. Select the sentence to make your choice. It can be inferred from paragraph 2 that one intention of making sculptures from plastic bottles is to 12. It can be inferred from paragraph 2 that one intention of making sculptures from plastic bottles is to show that an everyday object has value beyond its immediate usefulness show that drink manufacturers should use more plastic in packaging encourage people to appreciate plastics as a multipurpose material encourage people to value sculpture more highly than other forms of art The word “evocative” in the passage is closest in meaning to 13. The word “evocative” in the passage is closest in meaning to challenging beautiful suggestive informative In paragraph 3, what is indicated about newspapers that are used in collages? 14. In paragraph 3, what is indicated about newspapers that are used in collages? They focus on the daily lives of people rather than on breaking news stories. They become part of a critique of information presented by the news media. They contain more images than text. They are carefully selected to ensure that their content contains no contradictions. According to paragraph 4, the societal role of creative waste transformation is to 15. According to paragraph 4, the societal role of creative waste transformation is to inspire greater experimentation in the fine arts reduce the cost of art production encourage reflection on consumerism and sustainability prioritize artistic values over environmental concerns Creative waste transformation is reshaping the applied arts, those that apply design and decoration to everyday objects. Artists working with waste do more than recycle materials. They redefine what waste can become. And in doing so, they make a powerful statement on sustainability, challenging the norms of resource use. Consider the metamorphosis of plastic bottles. Artists heat and reshape these discarded items and transform them into intricate sculptures, provoking dialogue on consumer habits. Such works suggest themes of rebirth, questioning whether the end of an object’s usefulness is truly its final chapter. Similarly, forgotten newspapers find new life as evocative collages (artworks made by assembling pieces of paper or other materials). These collages weave stories by layering headlines, images, and fragments of text to construct visual narratives that critique media saturation and information excess. Through juxtaposition and repetition, they highlight contradictions, biases, and overlooked truths, inviting viewers to reconsider the narratives they consume daily. This trend sparks broader discourse on art’s societal role. By turning waste into art, creators not only curb landfill growth but also prompt audiences to reflect on consumption practices. Creative waste transformation is a catalyst for change, urging society toward a sustainable future.'),
    ('reading-rap-e8f0396c60263fb82dff4c82', 'What is the main theme of the passage? 11. What is the main theme of the passage? The decline of traditional waste management methods in cities The potential and challenges of transforming urban waste into resources The future of electronic-waste recycling in urban areas worldwide The growth of urban areas as a result of innovative waste-management strategies Why does the author mention Stockholm? 12. Why does the author mention Stockholm? To identify a city struggling with waste management To highlight a successful example of biogas utilization To contrast urban and rural waste management methods To illustrate the process of turning waste into biogas What can be inferred about the role of public participation in waste-to-energy systems? 13. What can be inferred about the role of public participation in waste-to-energy systems? It varies significantly from one urban location to another. It is a minor challenge for these systems compared to economic constraints. It is crucial for the effective operation of these systems. It is undermined by the public''s overreliance on traditional recycling methods. According to the passage, one reason why recovering resources from electronic waste has been difficult is that 14. According to the passage, one reason why recovering resources from electronic waste has been difficult is that regulators have consistently opposed it the right kinds of metals have not been available urban consumers keep old electronic devices for too long extracting metals requires a significant financial investment The phrase "fraught with" in the passage is closest in meaning to 15. The phrase "fraught with" in the passage is closest in meaning to well-known for linked to concerned with full of Urbanization presents a paradox in waste management, both exacerbating and potentially mitigating its challenges. As cities grow, so does their waste; yet this accumulation could be a valuable asset. Rather than merely