    ('reading-rap-e9b7aae1ca5fa5894b573ec8', 'Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage? 31. Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage? Recognizing pervasive consumer biases improves the ability to anticipate market behaviors. Giving consumers more accurate information improves their decision-making abilities. Accepting Kahneman''s and Thaler''s findings has been difficult for modern economists. Anchoring chiefly drives consumer choices, validating established economic strategies. The word "Armed" in the passage is closest in meaning to: 32. The word "Armed" in the passage is closest in meaning to: burdened engaged equipped charged According to the passage, all of the following are true of traditional economic models of consumer choice EXCEPT: 33. According to the passage, all of the following are true of traditional economic models of consumer choice EXCEPT: They assumed that consumers made informed, calculation-based choices. They assumed the use of rational foresight to anticipate market behavior. They significantly influenced policy and corporate strategy for many years. They explicitly incorporated biases like anchoring into their predictions. The passage indicates that the anchoring bias can have which of the following effects on consumers? 34. The passage indicates that the anchoring bias can have which of the following effects on consumers? Make them less capable of performing precise benefit calculations Cause them to cling to initial impressions even when better data emerges Give them too much confidence in the rational nature of markets Induce them to resist policy initiatives aimed at improving economic efficiency The passage presents the two scholars named in the passage as examples of 35. The passage presents the two scholars named in the passage as examples of economists who defended traditional models of consumer behavior researchers who challenged widely held assumptions in economic theory policymakers who worked on maintaining consensus among economists agents who tried to make optimal decisions with incomplete information For decades, the common wisdom in economics held that consumers acted with near-perfect rationality: Armed with access to information and precise calculations, they would make informed decisions that produced maximum individual benefit. The main models of consumer choice assumed this planning ability as a basis for predicting market behavior. However, recent research in behavioral economics has put this idealized vision of the rational consumer into doubt. Studies by scholars such as Daniel Kahneman and Richard Thaler have demonstrated that many psychological biases and mental shortcuts often skew consumer decision-making. These irrational tendencies can lead to choices that deviate from the optimal path suggested by traditional economic theory. For instance, the "anchoring bias" can cause consumers to rely too heavily on the first piece of information they come across when making decisions. The challenge in accepting these findings lies in their disruption of long-held economic models and theories. Such models have influenced policy and business strategies for years, and to reconsider their basic assumptions is no small task. Yet, as the field progresses, the acknowledgment of consumer irrationality paves the way for more careful and effective approaches to understanding and predicting market dynamics.'),
    ('reading-rap-ed56100135e379e25de1a6d4', 'According to the passage, there used to be a mistaken view that plants 31. According to the passage, there used to be a mistaken view that plants often changed their behavior released root exudates only in small amounts were not significantly affected by ecological factors did not actively interact with other plants According to the passage, corn plants may release MBOA into their tissues in response to 32. According to the passage, corn plants may release MBOA into their tissues in response to harmful chemicals released by caterpillars electrical signals in the soil human activities that affect the environment root exudates produced by other corn plants What can be inferred to be the purpose of electrical signals that plants release? 33. What can be inferred to be the purpose of electrical signals that plants release? To attract beneficial animals to the plants To prevent attack by caterpillars on the plants To help the plants make the best use of available resources To communicate with other plants across long distances Knowledge about the presence of other plants can help a plant to 34. Knowledge about the presence of other plants can help a plant to change its growth in a way that reduces competition prevent major damage to its roots find additional resources determine which chemicals to release into the soil The phrase “still in its infancy” in the passage is closest in meaning to 35. The phrase “still in its infancy” in the passage is closest in meaning to highly controversial in a difficult period looking for funding at an early stage The discovery that plants use communication has changed our understanding of plant behavior and ecology. Traditionally, plants were considered passive organisms, reacting to environmental changes rather than actively engaging with their surroundings. However, studies have shown that plants can send chemical signals through the soil. These signals, often carried by root exudates (substances released by the plants’ roots into the soil), can inform neighboring plants about threats like plant-eating animals or disease-causing agents. For instance, when a corn plant is attacked by caterpillars, it can release specific chemicals that warn nearby corn plants. In response, the neighboring corn plants may release a chemical known as MBOA into their tissues, which makes them less palatable to the caterpillars. Plants also use electrical signals to respond rapidly to environmental changes. These signals travel through the plant, allowing it to adjust resource allocation. Some plants can even detect the presence of other plants through their roots. This allows them to alter their growth patterns to avoid direct competition. Despite these discoveries, the study of plant communication is still in its infancy. Researchers are continuously uncovering new ways that plants interact with each other and their environment.'),
    ('reading-rap-f04bee90f6b464220dc4d249', 'The word "fostered" in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word "fostered" in the passage is closest in meaning to accepted revealed preceded promoted Which of the following best describes the main purpose of paragraph 1? 12. Which of the following best describes the main purpose of paragraph 1? To argue that the fall of Rome had minimal impact on European governance To highlight the cultural achievements that followed the Roman Empire''s collapse To present a common explanation for the political fragmentation after Rome''s fall To contrast the Roman Empire''s decline with the rise of local kingdoms Why does the author mention the Carolingian Renaissance? 13. Why does the author mention the Carolingian Renaissance? To illustrate that cultural growth was still possible despite political fragmentation To argue that feudalism was a direct result of the Roman Empire''s policies To show how Charlemagne attempted to restore the Roman Empire''s boundaries To emphasize the role of military conflicts in shaping post- Roman Europe The passage mentions each of the following as a consequence of the Roman Empire''s collapse EXCEPT 14. The passage mentions each of the following as a consequence of the Roman Empire''s collapse EXCEPT the rise of feudalism and competing regional kingdoms the expansion of the Byzantine Empire into Western Europe the preservation of classical knowledge in monastic communities the transfer of political authority to local leaders There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Such developments suggest that decentralization may have inadvertently laid the groundwork for a more pluralistic and resilient intellectual tradition. The fall of the Roman Empire reshaped the geopolitical landscape of Europe and the Mediterranean in profound ways. One widely accepted hypothesis is that the collapse of Roman administrative and military structures led to the fragmentation of centralized authority. Without Rome''s unifying legal framework, power devolved to local warlords and tribal leaders, paving the way for feudalism and a mosaic of competing kingdoms. This decentralization fostered chronic instability, as seen in the frequent conflicts among the Franks, Visigoths, and Lombards. However, some scholars argue that this fragmentation was not purely detrimental. The absence of a dominant empire may have encouraged regional innovation and adaptability. For instance, the Carolingian Renaissance under Charlemagne suggests that localized rule could still produce cultural and intellectual flourishing. The emergence of monastic communities during this period also played a crucial role in preserving classical knowledge and fostering educational continuity across fragmented territories. Another compelling example is the Byzantine Empire, which retained Roman governance in the East and acted as a stabilizing force for centuries. Its survival challenges the notion that imperial collapse inevitably leads to disorder. While the fall of Rome dismantled a centralized system, it also opened space for diverse political experiments—some chaotic, and others surprisingly resilient.'),
    ('reading-rap-f148a2c69bd701834b8d6f3e', 'Why does the author note that “meteorite impacts leave craters”? 26. Why does the author note that “meteorite impacts leave craters”? To illustrate how over 2,000 square kilometers of forest were flattened To compare the energy released by meteorite impacts and that released by TNT To point out why people initially believed that a meteorite struck Tunguska To explain why a meteorite was unlikely the cause of the Tunguska explosion The word “prevailing” in the passage is closest in meaning to 27. The word “prevailing” in the passage is closest in meaning to complete dominant traditional confusing The phrase “this theory” in the passage refers to the theory that 28. The phrase “this theory” in the passage refers to the theory that a comet or asteroid may create a crater an airburst led to the Tunguska explosion explosions several kilometers above the ground can release large amounts of heat a comet and an asteroid collided several kilometers above the ground According to the passage, why have researchers “analyzed soil, vegetation, and tiny particles” in Tunguska? 29. According to the passage, why have researchers “analyzed soil, vegetation, and tiny particles” in Tunguska? To unveil chemical differences between comets and asteroids To determine whether an explosion truly took place in Tunguska To find out which type of celestial object was likely the cause of the Tunguska explosion To collect chemical clues that show how the explosion changed Tunguska There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 30. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Also absent were fragments of meteorites. On June 30, 1908, a massive explosion lit up the sky above the remote forests of Russia''s Tunguska region. The blast, believed to be caused by an extraterrestrial object, flattened over 2,000 square kilometers of forest, unleashing energy equivalent to 15 megatons of TNT (a widely used explosive). Initially, this was believed to be caused by a meteorite that struck Earth. However, meteorite impacts leave craters—large bowl-shaped holes. In Tunguska, no crater was found. The prevailing explanation is an airburst. As a comet or asteroid enters Earth''s atmosphere at tremendous speed, friction between the celestial object and the atmosphere creates large amounts of heat. This heat can cause the comet or asteroid to explode several kilometers above the ground. While this theory explains the lack of a crater, it raises questions regarding the identity of the celestial object itself: Was it a comet or an asteroid? Comets are icy, while asteroids are rocky or metallic, and each leaves different chemical clues. Researchers have analyzed soil, vegetation, and tiny particles from the area, but so far the evidence is inconclusive. Continued geochemical studies may one day unveil the identity of the Tunguska explosion''s cosmic perpetrator.'),
    ('reading-rap-f3d29f9504924cfb48ba1434', 'The main purpose of the passage is to 31. The main purpose of the passage is to share research findings related to spice use promote the widespread use of specific spices describe an outdated theory of spice consumption contrast traditional and contemporary spice consumption What data did the scientists most likely collect for the study mentioned in the first paragraph? 32. What data did the scientists most likely collect for the study mentioned in the first paragraph? The number of spices used in a variety of countries The types of microorganisms found on different spices The food preferences of customers at traditional restaurants The number of traditional Southeast Asian recipes used in Europe What is suggested about spice usage? 33. What is suggested about spice usage? Spices were used to conceal the odor of spoiled food. Spice consumption caused a rise in demand for native plants. Spices may prevent bacteria on food from developing. Spice use has decreased substantially in Southeast Asia. All of the following are mentioned about spices EXCEPT: 34. All of the following are mentioned about spices EXCEPT: They are derived from plant products. They require refrigeration to maintain their function. They are associated with cultural practices. Their use varies considerably by cuisine. The word “precipitated” in the passage is closest in meaning to 35. The word “precipitated” in the passage is closest in meaning to threw separated caused rained Are you fond of spicy food? Spices have been used for thousands of years to enhance the flavor of food, originally in cultures where spice plants were native to the area. Today, spice use is widespread across cultures, though certain spices are more prevalent in some cuisines. Indeed, scientists have suggested that hot-climate cuisines feature a greater amount and variety of spices. For example, a recent study found that the number of different spices was much greater in traditional recipes from Southeast Asian countries than in those from northern Europe. Why do hot-climate cuisines feature more spices? Some believe that spice consumption is related to cultural preferences. While culture certainly plays a part, scientists have recently suggested that the antimicrobial properties of spices, which prevent the growth of dangerous foodborne microorganisms, ensured their widespread use in hot climates. In such climates, food spoilage was a concern before the availability of refrigeration. Spices with greater antimicrobial or antibacterial properties, such as allspice, cinnamon, cumin, garlic, and onions, were frequently featured in the traditional recipes of hot-climate cuisines and in different combinations. Scientists concluded that food safety precipitated the use of many and multiple spices in hot regions.'),
    ('reading-rap-f542967f7fbb155c9f91e282', 'According to the passage, dynamic stretching may result in all of the following EXCEPT 11. According to the passage, dynamic stretching may result in all of the following EXCEPT decreased range of motion better performance in sports improved coordination more efficient communication between nerves and muscles The word “impair” in the passage is closest in meaning to 12. The word “impair” in the passage is closest in meaning to reinforce aid end weaken The passage implies that competitive athletes may most benefit from static stretching when they 13. The passage implies that competitive athletes may most benefit from static stretching when they need to increase muscle strength have issues with muscle activation have completed a training session are preparing for a sprint Why does the author refer to “timing, context, and type” in the passage? 14. Why does the author refer to “timing, context, and type” in the passage? To specify factors that must be considered when stretching To list the variables that affect an athlete''s performance To refer to additional kinds of stretching developed by researchers To underscore the complexity of factors influencing coordination There are four locations in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Elite swimmers often perform static stretches after intense training sessions to relieve muscle tightness in the shoulders and hips and prevent overuse injuries. Stretching has long been championed as integral to physical fitness, widely believed to enhance flexibility and reduce injury risk. However, research supports a more nuanced picture, with the distinction between dynamic and static stretching being particularly critical. Dynamic stretching, involving controlled, fluid movements through a range of motion, apparently improves athletic performance by priming muscles for activity. In contrast, static stretching, the holding of a position for an extended period, seems to hinder performance of subsequent exercise. Related studies show that static stretching can temporarily reduce muscle strength, likely due to reduced muscle activation through the nervous system and a decrease in muscle stiffness, both of which can impair the ability to generate force. For example, athletes engaging in static pre-competition stretches often exhibit diminished sprint speed and vertical jump height. There is, however, a key qualification to this contrast: While static stretching may be detrimental before exertion, it remains valuable post-exercise, aiding in recovery and flexibility maintenance. Meanwhile, dynamic stretching not only prepares the body physically but may also enhance coordination and neuromuscular efficiency. The evolving understanding of these techniques challenges the one-size-fits-all approach to stretching, suggesting that timing, context, and type are essential variables in optimizing physical readiness and long-term fitness outcomes.'),
    ('reading-rap-f7171b0afa43909a837e996d', 'Why does the author contrast Japanese rock gardens with gardens that recreate natural abundance? 31. Why does the author contrast Japanese rock gardens with gardens that recreate natural abundance? To show that rock gardens are more visually complex than other types of gardens To explain that rock gardens achieve their effects in a way that is special to them To argue that traditional gardens are less influenced by religious ideas than rock gardens are To explain why Japanese rock gardens became closely associated with Zen Buddhist temples The word “elemental” in the passage is closest in meaning to 32. The word “elemental” in the passage is closest in meaning to universal geometrical familiar basic What does the passage suggest about elements in Japanese rock gardens such as gravel and white stones? 33. What does the passage suggest about elements in Japanese rock gardens such as gravel and white stones? They function mainly as decorative features without symbolic meaning. They communicate an instantly understood system of meaning. Their meaning can vary, given factors such as object placement. Their meaning is determined by the place they originated from in the natural landscape. The passage includes information about all of the following aspects of the Ryōan-ji rock garden EXCEPT 34. The passage includes information about all of the following aspects of the Ryōan-ji rock garden EXCEPT who designed it what some of its natural components are how some of its elements are arranged how visitors are expected to view it There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 35. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Such perceptions can be shaped by the conditions of viewing. Japanese rock gardens, known as karesansui or “dry landscape gardens,” constitute a highly formalized mode of artistic representation closely associated with Zen Buddhist temples, especially during the Muromachi period (circa fourteenth–sixteenth centuries). Unlike gardens that recreate natural abundance, they deliberately exclude water and dense vegetation. Instead, they rely on carefully positioned rocks, raked sand, gravel, and occasional moss to suggest natural forms. Japanese rock gardens thus evoke landscapes in a distilled and contemplative manner. This apparent simplicity is not a reduction in expressive capacity but a form of abstraction. Rather than imitating nature’s surface, these gardens reduce it to elemental forms that evoke spatial and relational patterns. Expanses of white gravel may suggest flowing water, while clusters of stones imply mountains or islands. Meanings, however, are not fixed, depending on the arrangement and the viewer’s perception. Many rock gardens are designed to be viewed from a specific vantage point, directing attention in a controlled sequence. Ryōan-ji in Kyoto may be one of these. It has a rectangular field of raked white gravel that contains fifteen dark stones arranged in small clusters, each set within patches of moss. Yet from any particular viewing position, not all fifteen stones are visible at once. This arrangement encourages viewers to mentally complete the scene, turning perception into interpretation.'),
    ('reading-rap-f8c90326304a6b742a1f2b1e', 'The word “mimic” in the passage is closest in meaning to 11. The word “mimic” in the passage is closest in meaning to replace combine imitate challenge How did MYCIN diagnose bacterial infections? 12. How did MYCIN diagnose bacterial infections? It used a human specialist to evaluate patient data. It applied set rules to analyze patient data. It relied on information provided directly by the patients. It used data on infection rates to infer the probability of a particular type of bacteria. What is a major limitation of current expert systems mentioned in the passage? 13. What is a major limitation of current expert systems mentioned in the passage? They are unable to process large datasets. They often provide incorrect results. They need frequent updating. They use databases that are always changing. What can be inferred about the future of expert systems? 14. What can be inferred about the future of expert systems? They will probably be limited to use in medical applications. They will one day be able to update themselves without human input. They will become less accurate as the amount of new data increases. They will soon be replaced by more relevant technologies. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 15. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. Additionally, their performance may decline in unfamiliar or rapidly changing environments where new information is constantly emerging. Expert systems are a branch of artificial intelligence designed to mimic the decision-making abilities of human experts. These systems use a knowledge base of specialized information and rules to solve specific problems. Their goal is to replicate the expertise and reasoning of professionals in fields like medicine and engineering. An early expert system is MYCIN, developed in the 1970s to diagnose bacterial infections and recommend antibiotics. MYCIN used rules provided by medical experts to analyze patient data, infer diagnoses, and suggest treatments. MYCIN often performed as well as human specialists; however, MYCIN''s system needed constant updates, requiring extensive input from medical professionals. Despite their potential, expert systems have limitations; since they rely on static knowledge databases, they can become outdated without ongoing input from human experts. The process of updating the system can be labor-intensive. Advancements in machine learning and natural language processing could address these issues. By integrating these technologies, expert systems could learn from new data and adapt, improving accuracy and relevance. Researchers are optimistic that these advancements will transform expert systems, enabling them to tackle a wider range of challenges and operate more independently.'),
    ('reading-rap-fb5c5afce6b0ed89ed15fdd4', 'The passage indicates each of the following about circulating libraries EXCEPT: 11. The passage indicates each of the following about circulating libraries EXCEPT: They offered a type of literature that some people considered frivolous. They were primarily visited by female professionals. They charged subscription fees to members who were then allowed to borrow books. They emerged in England at a time when leisure reading was becoming increasingly popular. The word "stoked" in the passage is closest in meaning to 12. The word "stoked" in the passage is closest in meaning to overcame reflected expressed increased Why does the author provide the information that "books were expensive to buy" in eighteenth-century England? 13. Why does the author provide the information that "books were expensive to buy" in eighteenth-century England? To present one view about how circulating libraries affected access to books To acknowledge that a relatively small part of the population could afford subscription fees To explain why booksellers operated circulating libraries as well as bookstores To distinguish circulating libraries from nonprofit public libraries According to the passage, Fergus disagrees with Allan about whether 14. According to the passage, Fergus disagrees with Allan about whether leisure reading became more prevalent during the eighteenth century the emergence of circulating libraries had an effect on social hierarchies circulating libraries made a more diverse range of books available to the public circulating libraries facilitated the introduction of nonprofit libraries The author would most likely agree with which of the following claims about circulating libraries? 15. The author would most likely agree with which of the following claims about circulating libraries? They were responsible for an increase in literacy among the lower classes. They changed the way most people thought about novels and travel narratives. They were too few in number to have had a significant effect on the economics of bookselling. They affected how and what people read in England in a meaningful way. Circulating libraries emerged in eighteenth-century England as commercial institutions that transformed patterns of reading long before the rise of publicly funded libraries. Typically operated by booksellers or entrepreneurs, they relied on subscription fees that allowed members—predominantly middle-class women, but also clerks, tradespeople, and provincial professionals—to borrow popular fiction, travel narratives, and conduct literature. Their success reflected both the expansion of print culture and the growing popularity of leisure reading. As distributors of novels that were often viewed as frivolous or sensational, circulating libraries stoked anxieties about the effects of reading, especially on young women. Some historians, such as David Allan, argue that circulating libraries played a democratizing role by widening access to books beyond elite circles at a time when books were expensive to buy. Others, including Jan Fergus, emphasize their commercial logic, contending that they reinforced existing social hierarchies by catering primarily to those who could afford subscriptions. A further line of scholarship questions whether these libraries should be viewed as precursors to the nineteenth-century nonprofit public library movement or as fundamentally distinct institutions shaped by consumer capitalism. Despite these disagreements, circulating libraries undeniably reshaped England''s literary marketplace, mediating between print production and everyday reading practices in ways that profoundly influenced the nation''s cultural life.'),
    ('reading-rap-fcb877e86ce9cff28b0635f8', 'All of the following contributed to the status of Carthage as a wealthy and powerful city-state EXCEPT 31. All of the following contributed to the status of Carthage as a wealthy and powerful city-state EXCEPT its geographic position its trade network across three continents its large fishing industry its ship-building technology The passage indicates which of the following about Carthage''s maritime technology? 32. The passage indicates which of the following about Carthage''s maritime technology? Carthage''s ships were designed primarily for the transport of small items of high value, such as silver and gold. Carthage''s ships were not as advanced as Roman ships. Carthaginians developed a system of switching ships at colonial ports to go longer distances. Carthaginians were skilled at navigating over long distances. What can be concluded from the passage about Carthaginian agriculture? 33. What can be concluded from the passage about Carthaginian agriculture? It was far inferior to Carthaginian maritime technology. It produced more than enough food to feed the Carthaginians. Its innovations borrowed much from Roman technical knowledge. It was transformed by farmers from Africa, Europe, and Asia. What can be inferred about the consequences of Carthage''s success? 34. What can be inferred about the consequences of Carthage''s success? It led to mutually beneficial relations with Rome. It caused internal strife among Carthaginian traders. It led to a neglect of the military. It contributed to external conflicts. The phrase "culminating in" in the final sentence is closest in meaning to 35. The phrase "culminating in" in the final sentence is closest in meaning to revealing ending with coinciding with following Carthage, founded by Phoenician merchants around 814 B.C.E., became a powerful city-state due to its strategic location on the Mediterranean. This position allowed Carthage to establish a vast trade network that extended across the Mediterranean and into Africa, Europe, and Asia. Carthaginian traders dealt in goods such as silver, gold, tin, and textiles. The Carthaginians excelled in maritime technology, constructing advanced ships for long-distance journeys and bulky cargo transport. They developed efficient trade routes and set up colonies, expanding their economic reach. Their prowess in navigation and trade transformed Carthage into one of the wealthiest cities in the ancient world. Carthage''s wealth was also built on innovations in agriculture. Carthaginian farmers employed advanced irrigation techniques and crop rotation, leading to abundant harvests that supported both local consumption and export. However, Carthage''s rise did not come without challenges. Its growing wealth and dominance in trade led to rivalry with Rome. The Punic Wars, a series of three wars fought against Rome from 264 to 146 B.C.E., were rooted in competition for control of Mediterranean trade routes. Despite initial successes, Carthage was ultimately defeated. The final blow came when the Romans launched a brutal siege on Carthage, culminating in the city''s destruction.'),
    ('reading-rap-fdeb6000ce9ff3558cc559b5', 'Why does the author mention genetics and upbringing in paragraph 1? 26. Why does the author mention genetics and upbringing in paragraph 1? To name the factors that contribute to the unconscious habits people form To list factors we may be unaware of that shape our decision-making To argue that some people have more freedom to act than others do To note two factors that contribute to the formation of people’s personalities What does the author suggest may be true of “our sense of control” over our choices? 27. What does the author suggest may be true of “our sense of control” over our choices? It can drive our actions if we train ourselves to think independently. It helps us develop narratives that shape our choices. It is the product of unconscious habits. It is nothing more than an illusion. In the second paragraph, what is the relationship between sentence 1 and sentence 2? 28. In the second paragraph, what is the relationship between sentence 1 and sentence 2? Sentence 1 presents an argument, and sentence 2 restates it in simpler terms. Sentence 1 makes a claim, and sentence 2 supports it with evidence. Sentence 1 presents an idea, and sentence 2 challenges an assumption behind the idea. Sentence 1 introduces a viewpoint, and sentence 2 considers its implications. The word “unravel” in the passage is closest in meaning to 29. The word “unravel” in the passage is closest in meaning to fall apart gather strength form evolve According to the author, why is it important to consider questions about the meaning of free will? 30. According to the author, why is it important to consider questions about the meaning of free will? Ideas about free will affect how we hold each other responsible for our actions. Free will is an important part of what it means to be a modern human being. The debate over free will and determinism is one of the fundamental questions of our time. Without genuine autonomy, there is little value in institutions such as law and education. The debate over free will asks whether our decisions come from independent thought or are shaped by factors beyond our control. Although we may feel like we choose freely, influences such as genetics, upbringing, and unconscious habits suggest our freedom may be limited. The key question is whether these influences simply guide our choices or actually determine them. If they determine them, then our sense of control may be more of a story we tell ourselves after the fact than something that drives our actions. Some philosophers maintain that even within a causally determined world, we can still act freely—so long as our choices reflect our own reasoning and desires. Yet this view raises questions about whether those desires are truly ours or merely the result of prior conditions. Others push further, suggesting that even our capacity to reflect on our actions is shaped by forces beyond our control, leaving little room for genuine autonomy. These tensions are not just theoretical; they shape how we think about responsibility in law, education, and relationships. If the assumption that someone might have acted differently begins to unravel, then our notions of accountability may need to be reimagined.'),
    ('reading-rap-fe8411639546b0b11ec79d0b', 'The word "persevere" in the passage is closest in meaning to 26. The word "persevere" in the passage is closest in meaning to hurry change hesitate persist According to the passage, which of the following is true about students with a growth mindset? 27. According to the passage, which of the following is true about students with a growth mindset? They believe their intelligence is unchangeable. They avoid challenges. They view effort as a means to improve. They are naturally talented. All of the following are mentioned in the passage as ways educators can foster a growth mindset EXCEPT by 28. All of the following are mentioned in the passage as ways educators can foster a growth mindset EXCEPT by commending students'' efforts praising students'' innate abilities giving constructive feedback using mistakes as learning opportunities According to the passage, the implementation of growth mindset strategies may be challenging because of 29. According to the passage, the implementation of growth mindset strategies may be challenging because of difficulty in changing students'' attitudes lack of support from students'' parents insufficient research on growth mindset overemphasis on academic performance There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. 30. There are four locations ■ in the passage that indicate where the following sentence could be added. This reluctance can be further compounded by a lack of immediate, visible progress, which may discourage continued effort. The concept of a growth mindset has gained attention in educational psychology. Coined by Carol Dweck, the phrase "growth mindset" refers to the belief that abilities and intelligence can be developed through dedication and hard work. This contrasts with a fixed mindset, where individuals believe their abilities are static. Research suggests that students with a growth mindset are more likely to embrace challenges, persevere through difficulties, and see effort as a path to mastery. For example, a student who struggles with math but believes they can improve with practice is displaying a growth mindset. This belief encourages resilience and a positive attitude towards learning. Educators play a crucial role in fostering a growth mindset in their students. Techniques such as praising effort rather than innate ability and providing constructive feedback can help students develop this mindset. Creating a classroom environment that celebrates mistakes as learning opportunities can reinforce the growth mindset philosophy. However, implementing growth mindset strategies is not without challenges. Some students may find it difficult to change how they view their abilities, especially if they have been accustomed to a fixed mindset for a long time. Additionally, educators themselves must genuinely believe in the growth mindset principles to effectively convey them to their students.'),
    ('reading-rap-fe9caa2af2e2e6e581d4400b', 'The primary purpose of the passage is to 31. The primary purpose of the passage is to outline some benefits of GM technology and advocate for its adoption argue that GM technology is too dangerous and should be discontinued summarize some potential benefits and drawbacks related to the use of GM technology propose a set of steps toward resolving the debate over the use of GM technology The word “persistent” in the passage is closest in meaning to 32. The word “persistent” in the passage is closest in meaning to disastrous long-lasting inconvenient technical According to the author, GM technology can do all of the following EXCEPT 33. According to the author, GM technology can do all of the following EXCEPT improve the fertility of soil boost agricultural productivity improve human nutrition protect the health of agricultural workers The passage suggests which of the following about “Patented GM seeds”? 34. The passage suggests which of the following about “Patented GM seeds”? They can sometimes alter the nutrients in crops in ways that harm human health. Their use can benefit farmers when profits are shared by biotech companies. Their adoption could potentially subject farmers to unfair control by businesses. Their widespread use in agriculture could help to expand and protect biodiversity. The author mentions “the rapid pace of technological change” in order to 35. The author mentions “the rapid pace of technological change” in order to acknowledge the promise that GM technology holds for farmers suggest why research on the health effects of GM technology has lagged question one argument used to advocate for expanded use of GM technology explain some of the resistance to implementation of GM technology The use of genetically modified organisms (GMOs) in foods has raised a number of concerns, yet genetic modification (GM) technology can provide solutions to many persistent problems facing humanity. For instance, by engineering crops to resist pests, tolerate drought, or grow in nutrient-poor soil, farmers can produce more food with fewer resources. This can be especially valuable in regions facing food insecurity or harsh environmental conditions. GM technology can also enhance nutritional content. Rice, for example, can be enriched with vitamin A to combat malnutrition. Furthermore, reduced reliance on chemical pesticides benefits both the environment and farm workers. Yet some critics anticipate harm from GMOs. One major issue is ecological impact. Genetically modified plants may crossbreed with wild species, potentially disrupting ecosystems or reducing biodiversity. GM critics also worry about long-term health effects, even though current scientific consensus finds GM foods safe to eat. Additionally, GM technology has economic effects: Patented GM seeds can increase farmers’ dependence on large biotech companies, raising questions about fairness and control within the food system. Public skepticism remains strong, fueled by limited transparency and the rapid pace of technological change. If GM foods are to serve the public good, thoughtful regulation, candid communication, and ongoing research will be essential.'),
