    ('reading-rdl-25140f617f981938f89b04ef', 'The course description mentions the sonnet’s “strict formal rules” primarily to 28. The course description mentions the sonnet’s “strict formal rules” primarily to suggest that sonnets are more difficult to write than other kinds of poems identify a way in which form itself can convey meaning explain how the sonnet has evolved since the early sixteenth century challenge a myth about the kinds of subjects that poets have addressed in sonnets The course description suggests that students will 29. The course description suggests that students will read poems other than sonnets submit multiple papers during the course take quizzes on each week’s assigned reading do both creative and academic writing The course description mentions each of the following EXCEPT 30. The course description mentions each of the following EXCEPT the origins of the English sonnet structural features of the sonnet aspects of the sonnet to be discussed in the term paper subject matter that poets have explored in sonnets English 202: The Sonnet
The sonnet entered English literature in the early sixteenth
century, adapted from the Italian form pioneered by Petrarch,
and has inspired experimentation ever since. Poets writing in
English have created endless variations on the basic formula of
fourteen lines of rhymed verse in iambic pentameter.
From the Renaissance to the modern era, poets have used the
sonnet form to explore romantic, existential, and political themes
in ways both serious and satiric. Because the sonnet is governed
by strict formal rules, it is sometimes used to playfully illuminate
the tension between constraint and creative freedom.
Readings will include sonnets by Sir Philip Sidney,
William Shakespeare, John Donne, William Wordsworth,
Claude McKay, Gwendolyn Brooks, Edna St. Vincent Millay,
Cathy Park Hong, and Jericho Brown. Students will experiment
with writing their own sonnets and will read them aloud in class.
A final ten-page term paper will be due on May 15.'),
    ('reading-rdl-2e985fa62a755425c2c4af46', 'What is one activity you can expect to do at this event? 28. What is one activity you can expect to do at this event? Join a cooking class Discuss dietary choices Attend a psychology lecture Watch a wellness documentary Which of the following is indicated about the event''s target audience? 29. Which of the following is indicated about the event''s target audience? The event is intended for both teaching staff and students at the university. The event is geared toward students in the psychology department. The event is primarily designed for those pursuing certification as holistic wellness trainers. The event is offered for students experiencing stress during the exam period. What is suggested about the In Tune With Well-Being event? 30. What is suggested about the In Tune With Well-Being event? There will be career counseling for students seeking wellness careers. It is a recurring monthly event at Rowancourt University. It is an opportunity for like-minded university community members to connect. Only members of the Wellness Council can preregister. IN TUNE WITH WELL-BEING:
