    ('reading-rdl-5477133100b2982be0a02471', 'According to the article, what philosophical perspective does kintsugi promote? 28. According to the article, what philosophical perspective does kintsugi promote? Minimalism, emphasizing simplicity and the elimination of excess Ethics, focusing on honor and discipline in craftsmanship Humility, valuing imperfection and the transient nature of life Purity, highlighting spiritual cleanliness and ritual renewal What does the article suggest about the symbolic meaning of repairing pottery with precious metals? 29. What does the article suggest about the symbolic meaning of repairing pottery with precious metals? Wealth and luxury are essential for authentic art. Flaws can be turned into sources of value and beauty. Artisans prioritize aesthetics over philosophical significance. Objects lose their cultural value when restored with cheap materials. Why does the author of the article mention "a perfection-driven world"? 30. Why does the author of the article mention "a perfection-driven world"? To argue that modern society has completely abandoned traditional art forms To suggest that flawless craftsmanship is the goal of contemporary design To contrast kintsugi''s philosophical message with a common present-day attitude To explain why kintsugi techniques are rarely used in professional pottery today Kintsugi, an ancient Japanese art, transforms broken pottery into
objects of beauty by highlighting, not hiding, their cracks. Artisans
use lacquer mixed with powdered gold, silver, or platinum to repair
fractures, creating pieces that often become more
valued than their original form.
Originating in the late fifteenth century,
kintsugi reflects the philosophy of wabi-sabi,
which honors imperfection and transience.
Its message extends beyond ceramics,
inspiring personal growth and resilience by
advising people to embrace flaws as part of
life’s narrative. Rather than viewing damage
as failure, kintsugi reframes it as transformation,
symbolizing strength, authenticity, and renewal in
a perfection-driven world.
Today, workshops and studios worldwide teach both its
technical craft and its profound philosophy, offering learners not
only artistic skills but insights into acceptance, individuality, and
creative expression. Kintsugi stands as a timeless reminder that
beauty lies in uniqueness and in the stories our “cracks” reveal, an
idea that applies to academic life and beyond.'),
    ('reading-rdl-567fec0bc129b8daa6939239', 'Alex''s question about oral history interviews suggests that he 28. Alex''s question about oral history interviews suggests that he believes the source requirements are too demanding for the assignment prefers using interviews over written documents as sources has not yet begun gathering sources for the paper has identified research materials he is unsure how to categorize What does Alex''s email suggest about the History 302 research assignment? 29. What does Alex''s email suggest about the History 302 research assignment? Students are expected to find sources without guidance from their professor. Students may request deadline extensions for individual components of the paper. Students are expected to submit their work in stages. Students must complete all source research before beginning to write. Which of the following best describes Alex''s approach to preparing for the research paper? 30. Which of the following best describes Alex''s approach to preparing for the research paper? He has not carefully read the assignment instructions. He wants to ensure he fully understands the expectations before proceeding. He is requesting additional time because he has fallen behind schedule. He has already completed his research and is seeking final instructions. To: j.hajduk@stateuniv.edu
From: alex.tan@stateuniv.edu
Sent: October 5, 02:15 P.M.
Subject: Clarification on History 302 Research Assignment
Dear Professor Hajduk,
I’m writing to request clarification on the guidelines for our History 302
research paper, due November 15. Specifically, I would like to confirm
whether the 12- to 15-page length requirement includes endnotes and
bibliography, or if those should be additional.
The assignment instructions mention a minimum of five secondary
sources, with at least two primary sources. However, I’m unsure
whether oral history interviews qualify as primary sources under these
guidelines. Could you clarify, please?
Your email dated September 24 states that citations should follow the
Chicago Manual of Style. Are full bibliographic details required in
endnotes or only in the bibliography?
Finally, is an annotated bibliography due with the initial draft on
October 20? Or can that wait until the final deadline?
Thanks in advance for your guidance.
Best,
Alex Tan'),
    ('reading-rdl-56e4e55a13571dc16fe04e95', 'What is the main topic of the article? 25. What is the main topic of the article? The results of a recent student survey A new transportation option at a university The history of sustainability at Harrison University A review of local bike shops near a college campus What can be concluded about students at Harrison University? 26. What can be concluded about students at Harrison University? They mostly live off campus. They are not in favor of electric bikes. They prefer riding bikes to class over walking. They are concerned with protecting the environment. What is suggested about Benny’s Cyclery? 27. What is suggested about Benny’s Cyclery? It specializes in electric bikes. It is located on Harrison University’s campus. It will ensure that the shared bikes are in good condition. It has a long-standing relationship with Harrison University. Harrison University recently launched a bike-sharing program to
promote sustainable transportation and reduce parking congestion.
Students can now rent bikes from racks located near residence halls,
academic buildings, and the student center. The program offers free
rentals for an hour and charges a small fee afterward, making it
affordable for short trips across campus.
Each bike is equipped with a GPS tracker to ensure security and ease
of locating. “This is about giving students greener options,” said
transportation coordinator Alex Nguyen. “We sent out a survey last
semester, and students were overwhelmingly in favor of the program.
They see it as making a difference environmentally.”
The initiative was funded through a partnership with Benny’s Cyclery,
which sells the bikes at a discount and provides maintenance. Early
feedback has been positive, with students praising the convenience
and environmental benefits. The university plans to expand the
program next semester by adding electric bikes.'),
