    ('reading-rdl-63a2d6224475f45dc33f70b4', 'What is the discussion mainly about? 23. What is the discussion mainly about? Understanding a difficult concept in literary analysis Deciding which ancient Greek plays are the most powerful ones Figuring out how best to respond to Professor Lane’s questions during class Strategizing how to answer an anticipated essay question on an exam At 4:51 p.m., why does Dong-woo mention Oedipus? 24. At 4:51 p.m., why does Dong-woo mention Oedipus? To introduce a counterexample to a point made by Anita To further explore an example introduced by Anita To point out an exception to Aristotle’s idea of the tragic hero To suggest that Professor Lane is mistaken about Aristotle At 4:52 p.m., what does Wally imply when he writes, “Wow, this is so different from what I learned about the tragic flaw when I studied it in high school”? 25. At 4:52 p.m., what does Wally imply when he writes, “Wow, this is so different from what I learned about the tragic flaw when I studied it in high school”? He did not study ancient Greek drama in high school. He was taught to interpret the tragic flaw as a moral failing. He did not enjoy his literature classes in high school. He forgot how his high school teachers interpreted Aristotle. Wally Banes (4:45 P.M.)
Did you guys catch what Professor Lane said about the idea of the tragic
flaw in Aristotle’s Poetics? I’m still a bit confused.
Anita Verenne (4:47 P.M.)
Yeah, the key point was that it’s not a moral weakness—it’s a mistake or
limitation tied to the hero’s character.
Wally Banes (4:48 P.M.)
I was puzzled... the flaw can be admirable yet lead to a downfall?
Anita Verenne (4:50 P.M.)
Aristotle calls it hamartia—a misjudgment, not evil intent. Oedipus, for
example, is tragic because his determination to uncover the truth shows
courage, but it blinds him to warnings and drives him toward disaster.
Dong-woo Namgung (4:51 P.M.)
Professor Lane also noted that Oedipus in Sophocles’ play is arrogant
and quick to anger, but these traits weren’t “evil” for the ancient Greeks.
Wally Banes (4:52 P.M.)
Wow, this is so different from what I learned about the tragic flaw when I
studied it in high school!
Dong-woo Namgung (4:53 P.M.)
Yeah, Professor Lane said it’s hard for us to let go of moralizing when
we read literature.'),
