    ('reading-rdl-f59b3f7f86b00c9a7afe4483', 'What is the main purpose of the article? 25. What is the main purpose of the article? To examine how alternating between classroom and community enriches students'' understanding of course material To explain how community perspectives have led to curriculum changes To highlight the challenges students face when conducting fieldwork in public places such as a café or a grocery store To compare this course with other public policy classes offered at the university The course includes all of the following EXCEPT 26. The course includes all of the following EXCEPT classroom discussions about zoning policy informal meetings with town residents examination of town records interviews with municipal administrators What is most likely true about students who succeed in this course? 27. What is most likely true about students who succeed in this course? They tend to enjoy community-based projects far more than campus discussions. They prefer a structured and predictable syllabus above all else. They are comfortable adapting to last-minute changes in class times and settings. They focus primarily on community perspectives and less on research design. Zoning Policy Beyond the Classroom
Dr. Paredes'' hybrid Zoning Policy Field Methods course bridges the gap
between academic theory and the real world. The seminar alternates
between campus and town locations: On-campus discussions focus on
theory and research, while in town, students interact with community
members in the café of a neighborhood grocery store and also browse
the municipal archives. Students'' campus-based assumptions are often
challenged when residents describe the effects of zoning changes.
This approach connects theoretical perspectives to lived experiences,
but creates logistical uncertainty. Because residents'' schedules can
shift, planned group meetings are sometimes adjusted, and revised
details may arrive late, frustrating students who prefer predictable
routines.
One course highlight makes this unpredictability worthwhile: Group
interviews with residents vividly reveal how zoning policies affect
everyday people, as when removing commercial use permission leads
to store closures. Students willing to stay flexible will gain valuable
insight into how theory plays out in real life.'),
    ('reading-rdl-f6c89ab8e6ebff0dd8faac68', 'Ethan is writing the email because he wants 28. Ethan is writing the email because he wants information about Professor Rappaport''s project on coastal resilience feedback on a paper he presented at a research symposium to inquire about enrollment in courses in Ecology and Data Analysis to work in Professor Rappaport''s research laboratory Why does Ethan tell the professor about a paper he recently presented? 29. Why does Ethan tell the professor about a paper he recently presented? To persuade her that he is qualified to be her assistant To learn her opinion about his academic work To request her advice about publishing in an ecology journal To demonstrate his interest in public speaking What is stated about Ethan''s weekly schedule? 30. What is stated about Ethan''s weekly schedule? He can work up to twelve hours. He is available full-time. He can commit one full day each week. He prefers weekend shifts only. To: Leila Rappaport
From: Ethan Li
